Republican strategy to counter Obama’s re-election
Republican leaders justified their policies by the need to combat electoral fraud.
However, the Brennan Center considers the latter to be a myth, claiming that electoral fraud is rarer in the United States than the number of people killed by lightning.
In fact, Republican lawyers have documented only 300 cases of electoral fraud in the United States in ten years.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will negatively influence the turnout.
In this sense, these measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
Unlike Canada, U.S. states are responsible for organizing federal elections in the United States.
It is in this spirit that a majority of U.S. governments enactured new laws from 2009 making the registration or voting process more difficult.
This phenomenon has gained momentum after the November 2010 elections, which included 675 new Republican representatives in 26 states.
As a result, 180 bills restricting the exercise of the right to vote in 41 states were introduced in 2011 alone.
The new electoral laws require voters to present a photo ID card and proof of U.S. citizenship.
Moreover, these laws also reduce advance voting periods, invalidate the right to register as an elector on polling day and deprive citizens with judicial records of their right to vote.
Prior to the 2006 elections, no U.S. state required voters to present a photo ID card.
Indiana was the first state to make such a requirement.
The U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of Indiana law in 2008.
The republican authorities hastened to extend this practice to other states.
For the past two years, they sponsored bills in 34 states to force voters to present a photo ID card.
It is important to note that, unlike in Quebec, U.S. citizens do not have a universal identity card such as the health insurance card.
In fact, 11% of U.S. citizens, or 21 million people of voting age, do not have photo identification cards issued by a government agency in their state.
Moreover, five million new voters in 2012 do not have such identification.
However, it often costs more than one hundred dollars to obtain the required identity card.
The new restrictions disproportionately affect young people, minorities, and low-income people.
Indeed, 25% of African-Americans, 15% of people earning less than US$35,000; 18% of citizens over the age of 65 and 20% of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 do not have the required identity card with photo.
So much more.
Students, voters who are seen as voting more for Democratic candidates, are not allowed in several states to use their photo ID cards issued by their institution.
On the other hand, these same states allow members of fishing or hunting clubs who vote more on the republican side to use to vote the cards issued by these clubs.
Prior to 2004, there was no state requiring proof of citizenship to vote.
Arizona was the first to present such a requirement.
Since 2011, a dozen states have passed laws requiring voters to prove that they are U.S. citizens.
These measures are clearly aimed at limiting Hispanic voting.
However, it appears that two out of three Hispanic voters favor the Democratic Party.
In addition, Republican lawmakers in 2011 sponsored laws abolishing voter registration on polling day in eight states.
In addition, they limited the right of individuals and groups to provide assistance to voters wishing to register.
These restrictions are not without consequences.
For example, during the 2004 general election, voter registration campaigns helped to register about 10 million citizens.
However, the measures adopted since 2009 have reduced by 17% the registration rate of new voters in 2010 compared to 2006.
In addition, Republican lawmakers have passed laws in five other states aimed at reducing the advance voting period.
For example, in the 2008 Florida general election, 33% of voters who voted in advance were African-Americans, while the latter accounted for only 13% of voters in the state.
The same happened with the Hispanics.
These accounted for only 11% of voters, but 24% of citizens who voted in anticipation.
In return, the blanks that made up 76% of voters accounted for only 46% of voters voting in anticipation.
Of course, Democratic legislators and their supporters have vigorously objected to the enactment of laws restricting voter registration.
Several bills have been blocked by the vetoes of Democratic governors.
The U.S. Advocate-General intervened to suspend the most controversial laws.
They were able to partially limit the damage.
For example, only 16 out of 34 states have enacted laws requiring the presentation of a photo ID card.
Nevertheless, the new rules put in place will undeniably make it more difficult to exercise the right to vote in 2012.
Democratic critics denounce the partisan nature of the laws that have been passed and they see it as an obvious goal of influencing the results in 2012 in key states.
A 2011 report from the Brennan Centre shows that the states that passed these laws represent 171 of the 270 votes required by the Electoral College to win the presidency.
It is too early to say with certainty that these legislative changes at the level of the electoral system will have significant impacts on the outcome of the 2012 presidential elections.
But one thing is certain: these new provisions will negatively influence the turnout.
In this sense, these measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
Prostate cancer screening: taking the test or not?
Indeed, the APS test would sometimes present erroneous results, with false negative results or false positives, which lead to unnecessary medical interventions.
Enough to make even more hesitant men already reluctant to pass screening tests.
Pass the test or not?
We have sought the advice of two experts.
In studies in the United States, there was a lot of contamination between control groups, so it is difficult to interpret these data and have firm recommendations.
Another study, that European one, found a difference in mortality between patients who were screened and those who did not.
This study also demonstrated, with follow-up after 12 years, that we are between 30 and 40% more likely to have metastases if we are not detected.
So I recommend the test from the age of 50, or from the age of 40 if you have a direct parent who has ever had prostate cancer.
Men of African-American descent are also at higher risk.
The key is to make the right decision once a cancer is detected.
There are aggressive cancers and others that are indolent.
It is really necessary to make the patient understand the degree of risk of his cancer, by offering him the possible options, by not necessarily treating prostate cancers that do not affect long-term life, and rather by opting, in these cases, for active surveillance of the disease.
Today, many men who are diagnosed with cancer will not be treated, because their cancer is not aggressive and does not threaten their lives.
We're going to suggest active surveillance and if the disease progresses, we're going to offer treatment.
Increasingly, there are precise criteria for deciding who should or should not be treated.
I would still recommend taking the test.
But the important thing is to have a discussion with your doctor to determine whether to pass it or not.
In collaboration with the International Society of Urology, Movember has created a tool to evaluate the pros and cons of the APS test.
We can download this document (in English for the moment, a translation will be offered shortly) at: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer-screening
Preventing disease
Unfortunately, there is no miracle recipe to prevent cancer.
Despite advances in research, adopting good lifestyle habits remains the best way to reduce the risk of suffering.
It is estimated that if everyone ate well and moved enough, one could prevent 30% of cancers.
"If no one smoked anymore, this rate would rise to at least 50%," said André Beaulieu, spokesman for the Canadian Cancer Society.
On the other hand, it is estimated that approximately 10% of cancers are caused by heredity.
Many also remain completely unexplained.
For the Canadian Cancer Society, tobacco control remains a priority, despite the decrease in the number of smokers.
Cigaretes are related to 85% of lung cancer cases.
It is also a risk factor for many others.
It harms people’s health a lot.
"Again today, there are 1.5 million smokers in Quebec," said spokesman André Beaulieu.
Encouragating data: 10 years after quit smoking, the risk of dying from cancer drops by half.
Weight
Overweight and obesity also promote the onset of the disease, according to CSC.
They would increase the risk of breast, colon and rectum cancer, esophagus, pancreas and uterus.
"Research shows that the regular practice of physical activity throughout a lifetime protects against colon cancer," it added.
Food
The organization also recommends limiting its consumption of red meat.
In too large quantities, it raises the risk of colorectal cancer.
Charcuteries too, and they should be avoided.
Preserving meat by smoking, drying or salation can result in the formation of carcinogenic substances.
"The latter can damage the body's cells and lead to the development of cancer," it said.
The vitamins
In recent years, several scientists have studied the links between vitamin supplements and cancer.
However, their research is inconclusive at the moment.
Vitamin E studies are contradictory, according to CSC.
While one of them saw a decrease in the risk of prostate cancer, another noted an increase instead.
The effect of vitamin D on cancer is not clearly established either.
Moreover, Mr. Beaulieu emphasizes the importance of talking about his concerns and his family history with his doctor.
"Passing a screening test doesn't give cancer."
Higgs boson shelled
The announcement of the likely discovery of the Higgs boson created quite a stir, last summer, and for good reason.
It is indeed believed that this boson participates in the mechanism responsible for the mass of everything in the Universe, just that.
And then, it is the last particle whose existence is predicted by the Standard Model - our best, or "less worse" explanation of the nature and behavior of matter - but which has not yet been empirically observed.
But for physicists, it's not yet completely certain that it's the Higgs.
We know without the shadow of a doubt that we hold an authentic new particle, and that it is very similar to the Higgs boson predicted by the Standard Model.
In addition, new data released this week at a major physics congress in Kyoto seems to confirm this, but it still lacks data to be perfectly certain.
Suppose, however, that this is the Higgs, since the chances of being wrong seem slim, and let's see what it's about.
There is, in this lower world, a fatally inescapable law that two things cannot end up in the same place at the same time.
No way to break this rule - and don't try too hard, you're going to hurt yourself.
However, even if particle physics is a very strange world, it turns out that it also has a law of this kind: the principle of exclusion of Pauli, which stipulates that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state" - this "state" consisting roughly in some of their characteristics.
From there, physicists classify the particles into two categories.
In one corner there are good citizens named fermions, who wisely obey Pauli’s principle.
And in the other agitate the bosons, a dirty bunch of anarchists who have no respect for anything - in any case, not for this principle, which means that they can indeed find themselves in the same place, at the same time.
These bosons are then divided into two groups, according to the (absolutely Extraordinary site, for that matter) The Particle Adventure, of Berkeley Laboratories: the Mesons, which we will not address here, and the "forcible particles", by which the great forces of nature spread and to which the Higgs boson would be somehow related.
These bosons, we must underline it here, are not all creatures as exotic as one might think.
In fact, if you manage to read this chronicle, it is thanks to a boson of an extraordinary banality: the photon, or the "particle of light" which is the "messenger" of the electromagnetic force.
When, indeed, a particle having an electrical charge accelerates or changes direction, it "disturbs" the electromagnetic field in this precise place, a bit like a pebble launched into a pond.
From this "disturbance" arises an electromagnetic wave (light, or infrared, or ultraviolet, etc.), and this wave is nothing more than a photon - and therefore, one of the "force-carrying" bosons.
More Stable Field
The same goes for the Higgs boson, except that it's another field, the Higgs field, which has to be "disturbed" for this boson to appear.
However, this Higg field is much, much more stable than the electromagnetic field; to excite it, it is necessary to reach very, very high energies, as if it were a frozen pond of which only a very large rock could ride the surface.
This is why it takes a huge particle accelerator like that of CERN - the Large Hadron Collider is a 27 km ring in circumference! - to reach such energies.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field is again useful to explain the relationship between Higgs and mass.
In fact, not all the particles or all the materials interact with the electromagnetic field.
Some, such as magnets, do, but others don’t – a piece of paper, for example, will never hold by itself on a fridge.
And in the same way, it's not all the particles that interact with the Higgs field: those that do have a mass, while the others (like the photon, here) don't.
Now, what "can bring" all this research, asks Ms. Plamondon?
For science, this is used to verify the validity of the Standard Model (MS), and it also allows physicists to scrutinize any discrepancy between the observations and predictions of the MS.
There are also many to wish ardently that we can find them, because the slightest difference could open a door on a "new physique" and plug some holes of the Model.
This one, it must be said, still has enormous deficiencies, proposing no explanation for gravity (oops!) or dark matter, which forms about 80% of the matter of the Universe (re-oups!).
But we haven't found such deviations at CERN so far.
Repercussions
The repercussions of this research in the daily lives of Monsieur and Madame Tout-le-Monde are more difficult to predict, but it would be wrong to presume that there will be none.
Here: In the very beginning of the 60’s, the pioneers of the laser, in the Bell Laboratories, did not suspect the revolution that their work was going to trigger.
They saw scientific applications, but nothing else.
In fact, we have already told us fire the physicist Willard Boyle - who worked in the Bell Labs, where the laser was invented in 1960, and who himself developed the first continuous laser (the first were pulsed), in 1962 -, initially the laser was rather seen as a "lab gadget".
Imagine...
And then, the applications can also come from all the instrumentation that surrounds the research.
For example, this same Willard Boyle developed a small light sensor in 1969, during his optics work.
This sensor, although it wasn't its original intention at all, now serves as an "eye" for all the digital cameras in the world, and earned it the 2009 Physics Nobel.
This of course doesn’t mean that LHC’s activities will necessarily transform our lives, but it means that, really, you never know...
Palliative care - The best way to die... | Le Devoir
With its Commission to die with dignity, Quebec recently debated the delicate question of the end of life.
Debate must resume shortly as a bill prepares.
However, in this essential area, much remains to be done.
Le Devoir tried to take a closer look.
Just a few weeks ago, Mr. L. still lived alone in his apartment in Montérégie.
The prostate cancer gnawing at him had given him a two-year respite.
"They had given me five years to live, I was seven years old," he said, half-figue half-grape, lying in his bed at Beloeil's Victor-Gadbois palliative care house, where he arrived the day before.
"But it's always a shock, you can't be prepared for that," he said.
Illness does its work: an enormous weakness that prevents it from going alone to the toilets, and even eating alone.
Sitting in front of an appetizing lunch, he agrees to let himself be helped to eat, resigned.
Brave, he even manages to smile, speaks to strangers who are busy around him, bring him his medicine, offer him a bath.
Courage of Ordinary Death.
"My biggest wish is to be cured of diarrhea, it's humiliating," he said.
A few hours later, the team found a cure for this evil.
"During our lives, we learn that a man pisses standing," says Pierre Brodeur, psychologist at Maison Victor-Gadbois.
Regressing at the child's stage, for some people, is an unacceptable humiliation.
"It depends on the person's ability" to accept regression, he notes.
Because, in the opinion of several people working in palliative care, great moments are taking place at the very heart of this regression.
Patients entering the Victor-Gadbois palliative care house all suffer from cancer.
They have a three-month maximum life prognosis.
At this stage, the team of doctors and nurses around them no longer provides so-called "curative" care.
For Ms. A., 89, the worst fear is to die "conscious and suffocated".
But the disease made me discover my children.
“I have good children,” she added.
"I don't have any more wishes in life," she said, before agreeing to be given a mask to help her breathe.
In the next few days, she is still waiting for a last visit from her son from Italy.
In Victor-Gadbois, a group of volunteers are provided with body care and food aid.
It's palliative care, which you give when there's nothing else to do.
To make death more comfortable.
In Quebec, there is a palliative care bed for 11 700 inhabitants.
It's very little when you know that you all end up dying someday.
Here, life continues in the best possible conditions, explains Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors of the house.
Whether in terms of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
At the dyer, we will agree to provide mouth care to the brandy or Pepsi, depending on the request.
Diabetics will no longer be required to control their sugar levels.
Death is part of everyday life.
Last night, Mr. X was served a beer, who died during the night.
This morning, it's his son who'll finish the beer at the feet of the deceased.
"We help loved ones as much as patients," says Nathalie Savard, director of care.
At Maison Victor-Gadbois, the days follow each other but do not resemble each other.
Alongside a 93-year-old man who enjoys his last family assembly in the courtyard, well wedged in his pillows as a 36-year-old man tragically dies, surrounded by his parents, his wife and his two young children, having tried everything to survive.
“For six months, there have always been three to five beds that are occupied by cancer patients under the age of 45,” worries Dr. Christiane Martel.
53% of patients who are admitted to home Victor-Gadbois come from their homes, 47% come from the hospital.
Palliative Care Access Problem
It is said that 77% of Canadians simply don't have access to palliative care, i.e. care designed to ease suffering when a patient has reached the terminal phase of life, whether at home, in the hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as Victor-Gadbois and Greater Montreal’s Home Palliative Care Society, more or less specialize exclusively in cancer care.
It is precisely this great deficiency in Quebec health care that raises fears among several physicians specializing in palliative care the adoption of legislation regulating euthanasia and assisted suicide.
Since October, a manifesto signed with palliative care luminaries including Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr. Bernard Lapointe is circulating to testify to their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr. Christiane Martel, Quebec's health care system is non-performing enough to ensure that everyone will be entitled to quality palliative care before one agrees to proceed with euthanasia.
Recently, she says, I have seen a patient spend 14 days in the emergency room, in great pain, without us doing what is necessary to relieve her.
I'm afraid that patients will only ask to die because they don't receive adequate care.
And at the same time, several oncologists are relentless on their patients until the last day, despite the worst predictions.
Hélène Richard’s survival expectations were already minimal when she gave up challenging chemotherapy.
When I told my oncologist that I was quitting treatment, she told me that she regretted that I stopped fighting, she said.
Still, she told me that I was finished!
Not all-powest care
Dr. Martel believes that 90% of patients applicing to die thank caregivers for failing to comply with their request after they have been relieved of their pain by a palliative care team.
But it must be said that palliative care is not absolutely all-powerful in the treatment of pain.
According to Elsie Monereau, director of palliative care at Greater Montreal’s Home Palliative Care Society, patients are refractory to pain treatments in 8% of cases.
At the very end of life, physicians often resort to palliative sedation, which amounts to putting the patient to sleep until the time of death, either sporadically or permanently.
You can't pretend to not hear that part of suffering anymore.
More and more, the unrelieved patient will have the possibility to have this palliative sedation.
Patients who aren’t relieved always say the same phrase, “I want to die.”
But that doesn’t necessarily mean “I want you to euthanize me,” it means “I want to be relieved.”
This report was made possible through a journalism award from the Canadian Institutes of Health Research.
Large-scale real estate scandals in Quebec
Road officials, construction contractors, political party fundraisers and specialists of the Italian mafia tell day after day what they know about a formidable "system", combining building industry, officials, politicians, trade unionists and mafioso.
An "industry" that cost Quebec taxpayers dearly, especially in the 1990s and 2000.
"It's curious as the system crumbles since we've taken on the great means," says Jacques Duchesneau, deputy in Quebec City and former chief of police of Montreal.
It is through him that the scandal erupted in 2011, through a thorough investigation into the embezzlements related to road construction contracts in Quebec, to which the Liberal Prime Minister at the time, Jean Charest, had consented only backwards.
The "Duchesneau report" established a direct link between industry, covert financing of parties and corruption of civil servants.
"Since the beginning of the investigation in 2010, he points out, the only Ministry of Transportation would have spared a billion dollars on contracts", some curbing their instincts to receive a quota!
The Charbonneau Commission "has already dropped two mayors", he added, hoping that it could "demonstrate the schemes behind the individuals".
Permanent anti-corruption unit established in 2011
The Permanent Anti-Corruption Unit, created in 2011, is also working with its army of government auditors, investigators and analysts.
The more the police officers of the "Hammer Squad" who, since 2009, would have led Montreal's "sewer cartel" to put the soft pedal on the inflations of contracts...
In recent weeks, she has carried out serial searches and laid charges for fraud and corruption against municipal officials, such as Frank Zampino and Richard Marcotte, mayor of a suburban city.
The next on the list is Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned from his position as mayor of Laval, Quebec’s third-largest city.
He is suspected of repeatedly pocketing bribes in exchange for public contracts.
Montreal’s road engineers and entrepreneurs of Italian descent, including Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito.
The latter caused a sensation by explaining to the commission the mechanics of the "system" of obtaining public contracts.
For years, he himself paid 3% of the value of the contracts obtained in Montreal to an intermediary linked to the mafia who spent the money in Union Montréal, the party of Mayor Gérald Tremblay.
Mr. Zambito sowed to all winds in the 2000s, donating more than C$88 000 (approximately 68 000 euros) to provincial parties, especially the Liberals then in power.
He also admitted to having organized an illegal fundraiser for the ex-vice-prime Liberal minister, Nathalie Normandeau.
Sewage contracts whose costs it inflated
In Montreal, the "system" of corruption worked smoothly.
Gilles Surprising, former public works engineer, detailed it well in front of the commission: in ten years, he received gift construction companies, invitations to trips, golf tournaments, restaurants, hockey games and bribes totalling 736 000 dollars, in exchange for sewage contracts whose costs he inflated.
Other road officials have confessed that they have greased their paw by inflanting the bills from 30% to 40%, by false extras.
Then an organizer of the mayor's party, Martin Dumont, accused Mr. Tremblay of deliberately turning a blind eye to a parallel budget supplying his crates with dirty money.
Following these revelations, Mr. Tremblay resigned in early November, plunging Montreal into a major crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was one of Montreal's first elected officials to sound the alarm.
Mayor of the borough of Rivière-des-Prairies, in the east of the island, she revolts since 2010 against the sale of a municipal land bought 5 million dollars and sold... 1.6 million to developers, in the midst of real estate boom.
70% dirty money in election campaigns
From the investigation that will finally be put in place, she says that she "pulls on a thread to understand the functioning of the system, infiltrated by ants, to stop the gangrene and pin the offenders".
The process, she says, is "painful but positive."
We're in the process of clearing the wound, but we'd need an investigative unit in Montreal and a watch, so we wouldn't see questionable practices back.
Just since we're cleaning.
Regularly.
Jacques Duchesneau notes that "officials stole hundreds of millions of dollars", but is mainly concerned about the role of "elected officials aware of the scheme", when they did not dip in the jaggling!
Estimating at 70% the share of dirty money in the financing of election campaigns in Quebec, he ironic: "I was told that it was only a pale reflection of reality."
The Quebec government proposes to limit donations to the parties to 100 dollars but it will not change the situation, according to him: "As long as we do not strictly limit election expenses, there will be dirty money in politics."
He advocates a complete revision of the system of granting public contracts and party financing: "We can not go further; get to the bottom of things, with courage, will rebuild the house on a more solid basis, with more controls and laws."
If this story tarnishes the international image of Quebec and Montreal, Mr. Duchesneau invites those who laugh to look into their own courtyard...
“PSG isn’t FC Barcelona!”
This season, you have taken on a new scope with PSG.
How do you explain this progress?
We can explain it by an individual awareness but also by the new dimension of PSG.
Great players have arrived.
Every day, I progress by their side.
The technical staff brought me a lot too.
On a daily basis, these elements push me to raise my level of play.
In the game, it's easier.
Everything goes very fast in football.
But I don't ignite.
From my beginnings to the pre-training center of INF Clairefontaine to my visit to Saint-Etienne, I have always advanced in stages.
So you take advantage of the competition installed by Carlo Ancelotti...
This summer’s rookies are accustomed to playing very high-level games.
They also know that every workout is crucial.
Which makes a player like me want to cope and provide the maximum.
In addition, Carlo Ancelotti brings me a lot about my positioning.
He's supported by deputies like Claude Makelele, who played in the same position as me.
Is Ancelotti the man of the situation?
Of course. Of course.
Ancelotti inspires respect among all technicians.
Today, he has no equal in Ligue 1 and is one of the best European coaches.
He has a lot of experience and won a lot of titles with fancy clubs.
He's worked with great players.
I think he'll win more titles in Paris.
In January, I had a comforting discussion with him.
Then I came out of a series of wounds.
The trust he gives me also explains my performances.
What do you think of PSG’s first part of the season?
In Ligue 1, Lyon took the place of leader.
But we're ambushed.
One of our major goals is the Champions League: we qualified for the 8th finals with the way.
What is the objective of the club in this competition?
We're gonna try to go as far as we can.
From now on, anything can happen.
But we'll have a say in the face of very good European teams.
We first want to finish our hen, in front of Porto, to be able to receive during the 8th final return.
Can PSG become a big European club in the short term?
He already has the budget...
To become a great European club, Paris needs to win titles and register over time.
This is not the case today.
Financially, PSG gives itself the means for this project to materialize.
In Ligue 1, not to win the title, like last season, would be a big failure?
Of course, that would be a big disappointment.
This year, we really want to pocket this title of champion.
Last season we didn't go far.
In May there was disappointment because we had the qualities to finish first.
We've had a really big season.
We're finished with 79 points.
Normally, with 79 points, we're supposed to be champion...
But another team, Montpellier, had an even more fantastic season.
This year, I think it's the right one.
Even if the big teams like Marseille, Lyon or Bordeaux are fighting for the title, I think we have the weapons to win.
Do you think the media expect too much from PSG?
It's normal that we're expected a lot from in view of what has been invested and the players we have.
We totally accept it.
Afterwards, when we win 4-0 at home against Troyes and we still find things to reproach us, it's certainly a little frustrating.
I wonder what more people expect.
We'll never win 4-0 every weekend.
We'ren't FC Barcelona.
We're trying to set up a game project.
A team is built over time.
We've proven in the Champions League that we can respond.
Look at Manchester City who has not managed, for two seasons, to qualify for the 8th finals, they have also made huge expenses!
In view of the sums invested, we imagined you with 15 points of advance to the truce!
It was neglecting our opponents and the French championship.
Lyon and Marseille, who were not good last season, were "boosted" by this new PSG.
This shows that Ligue 1 is exciting.
I hope that in May we can have a smile by telling ourselves that, in spite of all the difficulties, we finally won.
PSG seems totally dependent on Zlatan Ibrahimovic’s exploits.
So much the better we say there's a "Zlatan dependency."
This means that Ibrahimovic is very successful and puts a lot of goals.
He came for it and he proves he's the star of Ligue 1.
He demonstrated wherever he went that he was a great player, a world star.
Within the group, we respect the man and the player.
He also respects the men he has around him.
What he's done is truly outstanding.
It drives others to raise their level of play.
Thiago Silva, who is one of the best defenders in the world, also allows everyone to progress.
How did you live Euro 2012 with the French team?
Like a disappointment.
I wanted to participate in this Euro.
Sadly, my injury prevented me from grappling playing time.
I've seen things over there and I've come out strengthened.
Today, I manage to achieve good matches in selection.
That's what I've been hoping since my baptism with Les Bleus.
Lessons have been learned from what has happened in Ukraine, and we now have to have exemplary behaviour.
What do you look at the first months of Didier Deschamps at the head of the Blues?
He's got the results for him.
We are well placed in the qualifying group for the World Cup.
The coach is rigorous, close to the players, and inspires the winning.
Just like Laurent Blanc.
But I don’t want to make a comparison.
Blanc had achieved his goal by qualifying us for the Euro.
I hope that Didier Deschamps will lead the Blues to Brazil.
Is the good draw (1-1) snatched, on October 16, in Spain a founding match?
This match gave us confidence.
Everyone fought for everyone.
Before this clash in Spain, I had never experienced such a match in my career.
With Bitcoin, pay and sell without banks
Quite the opposite of current monetary exchanges, based on central banks, identified transactions and processing fees between stakeholders.
Moreover, as often in these technologies, a political vision is palpable: the conviction that the current monetary system, made of banking monopolies, leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin, invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (a pseudonym), is both a virtual currency (but convertible into dollars, euros...) and a secure exchange protocol like BitTorrent, which allows the exchange of peer-to-peer files.
Approximately 200 000 transactions have already been recorded thanks to 15 000 computers on the network.
A small thousand websites accept bitcoins as donations or as a means of payment.
The price of bitcoin, after reaching a peak of 30 dollars (23 euros) in June 2011, fell to 2 dollars, five months later, before returning today around ten dollars (prices are listed on bitcoincharts.com).
There is nothing very impressive, compared to world exchanges in real currency or financial products.
However, the European Central Bank (ECB) was interested in a report on virtual currencies published in October.
It describes bitcoin as "the most successful virtual currency", "in competition with the dollar or euro" and "similar to conventional currencies".
Bitcoin differs from other types of virtual currency such as "credits", used to progress in a video game that you win by playing or that you can buy (and sometimes exchange in return).
The social network Facebook has also developed this kind of system.
But, each time, a central authority monitors and processes exchanges.
With Bitcoin, all nodes in the network are custodians of the book of accounts, auditors, issuers of currency, and buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
Each transaction between two users is actually between two e-mail addresses like an e-mail.
Except that a user can choose a different address for each payment, thus ensuring anonymity.
A set of information related to this transaction is signed electronically by a dual-key encryption system.
Thus, the network can verify the authenticity of the transaction.
Thanks to the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the bitcoins exchanged exist indeed in the public account book, disseminated throughout the network.
The key step is to write this new transaction in this book.
It goes through the resolution of a mathematical challenge to computers, and whose winner, a kind of temporary central banker, will have the privilege of adding this additional line.
It is a file hash phase, i.e. the transformation of a large file into a shorter and more unique digital footprint.
The computers "take up" the new transaction and add a number to it before "chopping" the set.
The goal is to find the number that gives a particular imprint (many zeros at the beginning).
Once this number is found, the other nodes easily verify that it is the right one.
The transaction is then indestructibly linked to the chain of all the other transactions; any modification would change the footprint.
If, to defraud, a user wanted to pay twice with the same money very quickly (less than ten minutes), only one of the two transactions would be validated by the network - the other remaining orphan because both have different fingerprints.
The computer having solved the challenge wins 50 bitcoins.
To avoid inflation, this reward is halved regularly, probably before the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is therefore limited to 21 million, but they are divisible up to the one hundred millionth, which leaves margin...
The difficulty of the challenge is also met with each increase in computing power.
The life of the network has already had ups and downs.
Websites providing services for Bitcoin have been attacked and bitcoins in stolen deposits.
“The flaw used does not apply to the protocol itself,” says Pierre Noizat, who has just launched Paymium, a real-currency payment company using the Bitcoin network.
The ECB also mentions the possibilities of money laundering through this anonymous service.
However, cash also has this defect.
Heavy-duty actors such as Wikipedia deny donations of this nature.
Others, such as the WordPress blogging platform, accept them.
Recently, Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron, of the Weizmann Institute in Israel, analyzed the book of accounts and showed that nearly 80% of bitcoins do not circulate.
In November, "giant balances" were launched.
“Thirty thousand dollars have been redeemed,” said Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul, which converts bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, also the author of an educational book on this currency, strongly believes in the potential of this technology as a network of transactions.
Its system, Paytunia, is equivalent to a credit card (in real currency) or contactless mobile payment, but it uses Bitcoin to validate transactions, which are thus cheaper.
The wearer also manages his identity and can therefore be anonymous.
The system is easy to implement for merchants who do not need to install new terminals or software.
It is enough for them to communicate an address that a phone can "photograph and recognize", says Pierre Noizat, who insures having thousands of users.
There is a general movement to question hierarchical systems for more horizontal systems.
“It will take time for Bitcoin to win, but 2013 could be a turning point,” he predicts.
The ECB, in its report, also plans to reassess the various risks, now considered high, in the event of the success of this currency.
We've left Afghanistan.
And then?
French troops have left their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and the Americans are expected to follow by the end of 2014.
It is time for the Afghan army to regain possession of its territory and for the Afghans to choose their future, without expecting anything from us.
It is above all the Afghan peasants that we have punished as terrorists.
And ourselves, with our 88 soldiers killed, and the wounded, the mutilated.
The Taliban consist of foreign extremists, former refugee chiefs in Pakistan, but often peasants who refuse the foreign armed presence, such as in the time of the Soviets.
They want to defend their traditions, ancient and archaic, even if they have been joined by jihadists, Pakistanis, Arabs, Uzbeks, Tajiks.
Tolerated, sometimes aided by local insurgents, they will no longer do so when Westerners become more discreet.
The departure of the French troops from the Nijrab base, which I observed from the top of the hills planted with almond trees thanks to French credits, was made in order.
Convoys of trucks and armored vehicles joined Kabul without being attacked, flown over by helicopters.
There will be no surge of the Taliban on Kabul at the end of 2014.
Circumstances have changed since their irresistible advance from 1994 to 1996.
Kabul was then empty, the country with fire and blood because of the struggles between different factions.
Their takeover of the country had then been perceived as a kind of liberation, a return to security.
The Afghan women paid the price of obscurantism of these peasants overwhelmed by the Al-Qaeda organization, but their situation has not improved today.
Former mujahedeen, Afghan and present-day Taliban government join in the desire to keep women in an inferior position.
The main notables of the anti-Soviet war returned to power in 2001.
They turned into businesses, seizuring government land to resell them in building land to refugees who returned from Iran and Pakistan, benefiting from huge U.S. subcontracting contracts.
They disconsiderated themselves; most of them did not fight for themselves.
People, as I've heard in the countryside, aspire to a government that doesn't make up of thieves.
Many young people want to leave, as will those who knew how to take advantage of the American largesses: the capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and its ideologies.
They rubbed shoulders with the modern world during their exiles in Iran or Pakistan, they appreciated the benefits.
Approximately 65% of the population is under the age of 25; Kabul now has 5 million inhabitants, one-fifth of the total population.
In cities, government schools are full of girls and boys alike.
We will have to provide work for these young people who will no longer want to return to the obscurantism of the former parties or the corruption of certain leaders.
All of them, including armed opponents, are fond of mobile phones; television, and its Turkish soap operas that present a modern world, is followed everywhere.
The army is now present.
Will these authorities who command it be considered legitimate?
Former commanders of the anti-Soviet struggle are already concerned with reconstituting provincial militias, which will evade central power.
Afghanistan, a country of mountains, with strong local identities, should be able to benefit from some decentralization, in the image of Western nations, but the United States wanted to transform it into a centralized state, with strong presidential power, abolishing the post of Prime Minister that existed since the 1964 Constitution.
President Karzai does not want foreign controls, in particular during the elections scheduled for April 2014.
But his country has, since the 50's and already long before, dependent on foreign aid.
No industry has been restarted, no dams are in condition, no significant irrigation system is repaired.
Everything is imported; nothing is produced except fruits and vegetables.
Prioritization is left to private initiative.
In a country ruined by thirty years of war, government control over infrastructure would have been necessary.
There has been widespread that Afghanistan possesses immense mineral wealth.
This only added to the feeling that Westerners were only there to seize it.
Without energy to process iron or copper ore on site, or means of transportation to export it through the mountains, there is no mining.
The Chinese have already almost leaved Mes Aynak's copper mine, letting international archaeologists (funded by the World Bank) excavate the huge Buddhist site and remain the province's largest employers.
It will also take a day for Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which imports and exports are largely dependent, to restore normal ratios.
The departure of the French fighting troops ended on 20 November.
The new cooperation treaty provides for the continuation of traditional aids: high school of girls, boys, department of French at the university, French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, medical and agricultural fields, support to the Archaeological Delegation.
Since 2009, in an attempt to "gain hearts and minds" and to carry out the impossible task of coinciding aid and offensive actions, a service of "civilo-military actions" of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), abolished in 2012, carried out, and carried out by a small French NGO, many works of collective interest and actions of agricultural rehabilitation in dozens of mountain villages.
These works, greedy in local manpower, were able to help contain the insurrection: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit trees, soil protection and increased cultivable areas.
What will we leave as a souvenir, after two billion euros of military spending?
A much smaller budget would contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, which are very harsh in these valleys, often located more than 2 000 meters above sea level.
The embassy has received dozens of written requests for small agricultural achievements from local communities in Kapisa province.
In order to free themselves from the insurgency carried out by foreign groups, what the farmers have told me they want, it would be necessary to maintain in their favour a small, well-controlled civil aid that directly affects them.
A Constitution at the Forceps in Egypt
It's a new poker shot from President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt remains more divided than ever around the constitutional declaration, which provisionally grants him full powers, he has decided to play his back-ahead.
Taking everyone by surprise, he announced on Wednesday that the Constituent Assembly would vote its final text the following day.
Just a week ago, the head of state had given this House two more months to finish its work.
For almost two years, Egypt has been relying on a provisional text, several times amended which weakens institutional stability and leads to legal imbroglios.
This new initiative has only widened the divide in the country.
For his opponents, the President perseveres in his "autocratic delusion", continuing to "betray his word" and "trample on law".
On the side of its supporters, it is assured that it is a way to put an end as soon as possible with the institutional and political crisis, by accelerating the transition process.
A referendum should be held within fifteen days.
A very short period of time that obliges the Brothers to renounce their plan to explain the text, article by article, to the Egyptians.
For the President, it is also a way of regaining popular and democratic legitimacy while protest is raging throughout the country.
Mohammed Morsi seems convinced that the Egyptians will vote favourably, as he claimed in an interview with the U.S. weekly Time.
Especially since this recourse to a hasty vote takes the ultimatum to the Egyptian people: "Either you vote for my text, or I keep the full powers", these supposedly to end after the adoption of the Constitution.
It is in a strange atmosphere that 85 members of this Constituent Assembly, with a large Islamist majority, voted the text yesterday.
Most Liberals were missing.
Mid-November, shortly before the constitutional declaration, they slammed the door, believing they had not been able to assert their views.
Representatives of human rights, religious minorities and civil society had done the same.
In order for the necessary quorum to be reached, 11 people, replacement members, were hastily added yesterday morning.
Some are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
Unsurprisingly, it is most often unanimously that the articles were passed.
Commentators had fun because one of the only differences of the day was expressed around... the time of prayer, some members of the committee believing that the constituent's pendulum was poorly settled.
The text, which was still being passed last night, contains 234 articles.
Objects of all attention, Article 2 finally remains identical to that of the 1971 Constitution, stating that "the principles of Sharia law are the main source of law."
Salafist parties, for which the establishment of Islamic law is a major demand, hoped to be able to replace "principles" with "the rules", which would have allowed stricter application.
For the Islamists, the fact that this article has not been modified is a guarantee of their goodwill and respect for the other components of Egyptian society.
"Hypocrisy", answer the liberals, who only see it as a coup of communication.
According to them, the Islamization of the Constitution is done through other articles.
In particular, they refer to Article 220, which gives Al-Azhar University an advisory role, in particular with regard to verifying the conformity of laws with Sharia law.
According to Egyptian specialist Sophie Pommier, this is worrying because "the people called to pronounce themselves are not elected and have no democratic legitimacy.
You can see the beginnings of a theocracy.”
In addition, the fears of the Liberals are fuelled by the fact that the next rector of this university should be much less moderate than the current one.
“Right now, there’s no concrete religious implication.
With this Constitution, we remain within the framework of the Civil Registry.
Most of the lawyers who have worked on this text are not ulemas but academics, for some trained in the French system" tempers Alexis Blouet, who prepares a thesis on the Egyptian constitutional transition.
But he acknowledges "that there may be an ambiguity around Article 220, because the terms used borrow from religious vocabulary.
It refers to "fiqh" in particular [Islamic case law].
And the question in the future might arise as to the extent to which civilian judges are competent to rule on it.”
Beyond its religious aspect, the text voted yesterday is highly criticized because of the extensive powers it grants to the President of the Republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood argues that these are markedly reduced in comparison to what they were under the old regime.
Another contentious point: the powers conferred on the army.
In accordance with the wishes of the military, the review of the defence budget will not be submitted to Parliament but to a National Defence Council.
Nor will the trials of civilians be prohibited in military tribunals, as requested by human rights associations.
The latter also express their concerns about the text, which they consider liberticide.
Blasphemy crime is maintained and the insult is now prohibited, which could have serious consequences for freedom of expression, particularly in the press.
In addition, there is no article that ignores women’s protection, Human Rights Watch’s Heba Morayef points out.
According to she, the only positive is the prohibition of torture by Article 36.
The word did not appear in the previous Constitution.
While the Egyptian president was scheduled to speak last night on television, demonstrations are planned this afternoon.
The supporters of the head of state will march on Saturday.
In Israel, holy sites, the Centre of the World and a sea of brine await Ukrainian tourists
The Promised Land brings together the greatness of biblical truths, modern comfort and a wilderness.
Argoumenty i Fakty has chosen five great reasons to travel to Israel.
Get adoring the holy places
Unavoidable rendezvous: the banks of the Jordan River, where Jesus was baptized.
It is said that whoever penetrates these "baptismal backgrounds" will be touched by Divine Grace.
As for Galilee, it is the land of the miracles of Jesus: where the water was turned into wine, where Jesus walked on the water, where he soothed the storm or where the fishing was miraculous.
It is at this very place that Jesus appeared before his disciples after the Resurrection.
But where you will find the most holy places, it's in Jerusalem.
Believers roam the Rue de la Suffrance or the Chemin de Croix (in Latin: the Via Dolorosa).
This path begins near the fortress Antonia (the praetorium), where the trial took place, then led by the streets of the old city to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre on the Golgotha, to the place of the Crucifixion, then to that of the Anointing Stone and the burial.
This is where the Christian center of the world stands, a symbol of the salvation of mankind.
According to Christian legend, the Monastery of the Cross is erected in the place where the tree used to make the cross on which Jesus was crucified grew.
Jerusalem is also home to the most sacred place of the Jews: the Wailing Wall which constitutes the rest of a temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 BC.
According to tradition, people of different confessions leave small words accompanied by prayers that are then answered.
Travel at height
Visit the ruins of Massada Fortress, an ancient refuge against enemies built by Herod the Great in 25 B.C. for his family.
These ruins are located on the steep rocks of a mountain 450 m above sea level.
Only mountaineering enthusiasts can go there on their own.
For the others, a cable car takes them to this summit full of history.
In the north of the country, between 1 600 and 2 040 m of altitude, is the ski resort of Mont Hermont which welcomes many tourists in winter.
A shuttle can take you there from the foot of the mountain.
The ski slopes are a total of 45 km long.
Old faiths want pagan gods to have once inhabited the top of the mountain.
Visit unique museums
It is home to nearly 300 museums.
It is impossible to visit them all during a single stay.
Here are the five most interesting that are really worth a visit.
First of all: the Museum of Israel located near the Knesset (Parliament).
It is home to the very ancient manuscripts of Qumrân, or Dead Sea Sculpins, discovered in the caves of the Desert of Judea, as well as nearly 500 miles of archaeological and anthropological objects.
See also: The Tel Aviv Museum of Art.
We mainly exhibit the paintings of the Impressionists and Expressionists Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall and Picasso.
In Acre, Hammam Al-Basha awaits you with its Turkish baths composed of castings of visitors and supervisors of the time.
Once in Caesarea, enter the unique Private Ralli Museum where you can admire Rodin’s sculptures.
There are no guides or souvenir shops.
Entrance is free and donations are forbidden.
The fifth museum is Yad Vashem's Holocaust or Memorial Museum, located in Tel Aviv, which returns to the most dramatic historical pages in history.
The most tragic part is the children's memorial, built in memory of the 1.5 million exterminated children in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You enter and enter into total darkness.
Stars are sparkling.
Then you hear the names of the Jewish children and the country names in which they died.
Ukraine is also mentioned there.
Health and well-being
Israel's three major seaside resorts are located on the shores of the Mediterranean Sea, the Red Sea and the Dead Sea.
Each offers swimming pools, water parks, dolphinariums and giant aquariums.
Note: you can swim in the Red Sea even in the winter, because the temperature of the water does not fall below 21 ° while that of the air can reach 23 °.
The Dead Sea is even warmer and you can swim there all year round.
By the way, it is the most unique sea in the world: it occupies the lowest point on Earth, 417 m below sea level.
Azure-colored water contains brine and retains swimmers very easily, even those who do not know how to swim.
The landscapes that surround it are surrealistic.
We come here to carry out a treatment based on salt water, silt, curative sludge and to treat various diseases such as dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes but also to simply relax.
Discover the secrets of antiquity
They are guarded by Tel Aviv’s Old Town, Jaffa, on the shores of the Mediterranean Sea.
It is crossed by the famous Via Maris which connects Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in Greek and Egyptian legends of antiquity.
According to legend, this is where Noah built his ark and Perseus saved the beautiful Andromeda, with whom he lived here a long and happy life.
Tourists especially like to walk through the narrow alleys that bear the name of the zodiac signs.
It is said that if you touch the walls of the street corresponding to "your" sign, fate will smile at you.
In Jaffa you will be able to meet newlyweds who have come to do photo shoots from all over Israel and even other countries.
In Caesarea, city of King Herod, you can walk in the Roman theater, "conquer" the fortress of the Crusaders.
Under the Roman Empire, Caesarea was the main city of Judea and the residence of the Roman prosecutors, including Ponce Pilate.
The carefully restored theatre is now used for evening concerts and opera performances.
Note to Tourists
If you are leaving for Israel, do not worry about your English: about 30% of the population speaks Russian.
For your trip, it is better to prefer dollars to the euro, because they are easily redeemable in shekels (now 1 dollar is equivalent to 3.8 shekels).
When it comes to urban transportation, it’s mostly buses, but Jerusalem is equipped with a fast tram and Haifa owns the country’s only subway line, consisting of six stations and able to connect the lower and upper parts of the city.
It's almost an underground funicular.
The journey through all types of public transport costs 6 shekels and allows you to make changes for 1h30.
In Israel, the Jewish tradition wants to celebrate the Sabbath on Saturdays.
From Friday evening to sunset on Saturdays, markets, shops and public transport cease their activity.
The working week begins on Sunday morning.
Many cafes, restaurants and hotels only serve kosher food, which excludes pork, seafood, scale-free fish, and dishes consisting of both milk and meat.
We offer a multitude of dishes based on lamb and beef, soups and desserts based on coconut milk but also hummus, traditional Jewish dough, different sauces, balls of ground chickpeas, falafels, fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
On the other hand, there are many well-fed cats that roam the haughty air.
You can see them in the evening sleeping on the roofs of parked cars.
These felines love passing places and do not refuse to be delighted.
The rental of a car varies according to the brand from $37 for 24h (Hyundai Getz) to $188 (Audi A6, Volvo S80).
Insurance costs $15 a day.
Renting a bike will cost you 15 shekels during the day.
Entrance to the museum costs an average of 30 shekels.
The Language of Numbers
Three million tourists from around the world visited Israel in 2012.
They were mainly people who came to travel and rest from the United States, Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England and Ukraine.
118.8 miles of Ukrainian tourists went to Promised Land from January to October 2012, 51% more than in 2010, before the cancellation of visas on February 9, 2011.
"Which language has, in Moscow goes": migrants save money to learn the language
While MPs and human rights activists are polemicating about what the compulsory language testing law will give, fraudsters selling “false” certificates have already appeared in the country
Every year, 13 million migrant workers come to Moscow, St. Petersburg and other Russian cities.
Most of the time they are Central Asian nationals: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their only goal: to earn money to help their families staying at home.
Since December 1st, a law has come into force and stipulates that every migrant worker will have to pass a Russian examination.
For the time being, this rule applies only to those who intend to work in the services, housing, personal services and retail sectors.
However, over time, promises the Federal Migration Service, the tests will become mandatory for all immigrants.
Moreover, it will be necessary to validate the knowledge of the language, but also that of the history of Russia and the foundations of law.
You will have to validate your language skills in Russian to obtain and renew your work permit.
Only nationals of countries where the Russian has official language status are exempt.
Persons who have obtained teaching certificates or diplomas prior to the fall of the USSR in 1991 are also not affected by this law.
Beneficience, negative attitude and protection of rights
The network of examination centers will be under the aegis of the Pushkin Russian Language Institute, the People's Friendship University, Moscow State University, St. Petersburg State University and other Russian higher education institutions.
Immigrants will be able to pass the examinations in all cities of the country: more than 160 centres have already been opened throughout the country.
This initiative to introduce examinations was supported by the deputies of the State Duma and the Federal Migration Service.
But, before the law comes into force, human rights defenders have already repeatedly asked the question in the press: what will it do?
What is the obligation to know Russian will change for Russians and immigrants?
First of all, the representatives of the migration service respond, this will reduce the number of people suffering "from slavery at work".
Many talk about defending the rights of migrant workers, says Victor Sebelev, director of the office of the Russian Federal Migration Service in Tajikistan.
The protection of rights must begin prior to their departure.
Only an organized selection system will make it possible to solve the problems of foreign workers at 90%.
The problems arise for migrants without professions, without training, those who do not know the language and who do not have medical certificates.
Without the understanding of the language, Sebelev certifies, the future migrant is destined to meet dishonest people who, under the pretext of helping him, will provide him with a "travel" in an overcrowded cottage where he and dozens of his fellows will torment for months without food and without paper and will have to work between 12 and 14 hours a day.
We receive a lot of complaints from our migrants.
"At home, we promise them one thing, and when they happen, we cheat on them, we take their passports, and they don't receive the planned salary," confirms the Head of the Directorate of Migration Services for the work of the Tajikistan Migration Service, Tolib Charipov.
Don't argue with me, policeman!
However, many nationals of the Central Asian republics who wish to come to earn money in Russia confide that they not only know the language of the country they wish to visit, but also that they write with difficulty in their own language.
It is of course not their fault, but their misfortune: few Turkmens, Uzbeks, Tajiks can afford to follow even a passable primary education.
Indeed, their families don't even have enough to feed their children without talking about dressing them properly and equiping them for school.
As soon as they get into adolescence, these youngers leave to make money as soon as the opportunity arises.
“It’s hard when you don’t know the language,” they say.
"You feel diminished and diminished."
However, human rights defenders note an important aspect of the language law.
Examinations will only be for migrants with legal status.
Without status, there will be no examination or official work thereafter.
However, most migrant workers continue to live illegally in Russia.
"Welcome or defense to enter"
Many immigrants say it’s not so easy to obtain official status in Russia.
The reason? The administrative barriers and always these linguistic difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs money: between 12 and 16 ruble miles.
And then you can get a false recording quickly for only 1 500 rubles.
The Russian police know that we have false documents, that we do not have a recording, hence cases of extortion.
“They ask us for 100, 200 rubles, something to buy cigarettes, tea,” Oumed Khushkadamov, a Tajikistan national, told reporters.
"Get it fast, don't skimp and buy yourself a beautiful piece of art."
From the first day of the entry into force of the Language Act, it was discovered that the registration documents of migrants were not the only ones to be false.
Several false language examination certificates have already been confiscated by employees of the Federal Migration Service.
Falsified papers come out of an ordinary color printer.
Of course, their new owners did not have them for nothing: every migrant who thus hoped to facilitate the task of the examinations had to disburse seven thousand rubles for this document.
It is two and a half times more expensive than the official procedure: it will cost the migrant worker three thousand rubles.
Representatives of power and human rights defenders agree that the short-term goal is to protect the system against corruption so that it is impossible to simply buy these "pieces of paper".
The authorities can give migrant workers who have not been able to validate the first-hunt exam in time to take language courses.
Moreover, immigrants who do not know Russian will be offered work in areas that do not involve actively communicating with people.
The Ministry of the Interior does not deal with the arms market
The share of crimes involving legal weapons is very low
The Russian Ministry of the Interior proposes to tighten the law against civilian weapons bearers.
This is the reaction of the authorities to recent incidents: whether it is the shootings made during the weddings or the killing organized by the Moscow lawyer Dmitry Vinogradov, who left seven dead.
The police want to ban the wearing of rubber guns in public places and raise the legal age to obtain the permit to carry weapons by increasing it from 18 to 21.
The idea was supported by the head of the Duma Committee for Security and Anti-Corruption, Irina Yarovaya who promised that the amendments to the Arms Law would be introduced to the State Douma soon.
Not everyone is pleased that the Russian authorities are trying to solve the problem by “squeezing the screw.”
An open letter has appeared on the Net in which its authors, representatives of various shooting centers, demand to abandon "this senseless hardening".
The percentage of crimes committed with a registered weapon is minimal, Vasiliy Lesnikov, a lawyer and crime specialist for the BBC, told the Russian service.
According to the statistics of the Ministry of the Interior, over a six-month period in 2012, 142 crimes were committed with a firearm registered in the police services while 1,168 thousand crimes were recorded over the same period.
The authors of the open letter are certain that the strengthening of laws concerning civilian weapons will not prevent criminals from sourcing from the black market.
According to them, we can find today in Moscow any weapon for a reasonable price.
However, the Ministry of the Interior claims that it controls the dissemination of illegal weapons.
The suppliers: from the factory to the officer
The weapons black market is supplied through several channels.
There are five main channels, says Victor Baranets, a retired colonel who served ten years in the Ministry of Education and Headquarters.
Screenshot drawn from a site taking commands of weapons
The first is "the military trace", i.e. weapons that were stolen during military operations in the Caucasus.
“These weapons were stolen by the Russian officers, but also by the Caucasians,” Baranets said.
The second type of weapons from the black market are those stolen by criminals from law enforcement.
Baranets explains that these are bothers stolen from police stockpiles as well as those stolen directly from law enforcement officials.
Illegal pistols go on sale from military warehouses.
Many of them have often suffered explosions.
"Attested versions indicate that some fires have been intentionally started to conceal the deficit," the former soldier said.
For Baranets, weapons manufacturers are for something.
"We see private weapons factories that do not compete with the competition in the international market and sell the weapons on the black market, especially in Moscow," said an expert.
One of the other sources on the black market is smuggling.
A significant proportion of pistols and machine guns come from poor countries, such as Kyrgyz.
"There is a local production, sometimes artisanal, a mafia has formed and organizes the supply," adds the former soldier.
Where did the guns come from?
The experts assessed the approximate share of each source of illegal weapons on the black market.
A report on the subject was compiled in 2011 by the Centre for Problem Analysis and Study for Public Administration.
Specialists analysed the reports of the Ministry of the Interior and Rosstat, the criminal records and the public data of weapons portals.
The vast majority of illegal weapons, the researchers explain, comes from the military and law enforcement.
Half of the weapons on the black market found themselves there "because of the fault of employees whose work is related to weapons," the report said.
According to the researchers, 17% of the weapons come from the scenes of military conflicts, 14% are stolen at the time of manufacture and 5% are from "smuggled excavations".
A seller and consultant of an armory who asked to remain anonymous assures that for a long time no one has bought the weapons found by these specialists of the clandestine search; they are too old.
Most often, according to him, sellers will look for new goods in military warehouses.
The person buys through a sign a unit, e.g. a TT pistol.
It is delivered to him by passing the goods through a fence.
"He transports it to the city and resells it for 900 € per unit with two chargers," he says.
“It’s true that the police are aware of all cases, so, when crimes are about to be elucidated, they make a purchase of control from illegal arms dealers.”
“Like in a luxury shop”
Both the seller and the buyer are usually found through knowledge.
I visited sites, blogs, until a guy showed up and offered to come to Begovaya station where someone would become taking me to a "corner" to haggle.
The price of the pistol, they gave it to me on the spot
Victor Baranets as Military Observer
To find a gun, I have to know someone who has a contact, explains the salesman. I know someone, but I don't trust him.
There are sellers in the markets, but there you also have to come saying for example "On the part of such a person, who asked me to pass on that his daughter has lost a tooth".
Now I even buy a pair of American fists with the help of someone I trust.
He only gives them to me because he knows I won't give it away.
Newcomers are looking for weapons in different ways.
Former soldier Victor Baranets tried himself as a buyer of illegal weapons in the mid-1990’s when he was preparing to publish an article on the subject.
The schemes, according to him, have not changed.
We gave him a photo album in which there was "everything we want".
"I thought I was in a luxury boutique," the soldier recalls.
