Nearly a quarter of U.S. teens go through cashier work at McDonald's
In recent days, a wave of protests has risen against the low-wages practiced by Walmart supermarkets and famous fast-food chains such as McDonald's, Burger King, Taco Bell, Wendy's and Kentucky Fried Chicken.
At the moment, no one can predict whether this wave is going to grow or run out of steam quickly.
These actions are supported by trade unions and a number of left-wing associations.
In parallel with the increase in modest wages received by grassroots employees at Walmart and in fast-paced food chains, the organizers of the demonstrations wish to obtain the creation of trade unions.
For the moment, this sector is not covered by the trade union movement.
46 cents a year?
Demonstrations began last week, after Thanksgiving, the "Black Friday", at a time when a surging rain of sales seizing millions of people fell on America and sometimes triggering esclanders.
That day, some employees of the company Walmart, which employs 2.2 million people around the world, left their jobs to organize pickets with unions and left-wing activists in the company’s stores whose products are aimed at consumers of the modest and middle classes.
Walmart markets everything from diapers to shotguns to car batteries, vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
Products are on average 8% to 27% cheaper than in large supermarkets.
That’s why a lot of low-paying employees at Walmart go shopping only in the workplace.
The price and choice of products have made Walmart one of the most powerful groups in the United States.
According to critics, Walmart can afford to sell its lobsquittely because of the low wages of its employees.
They also complain about difficult working conditions, such as the lack of forklifts or portable scanners.
Participants of the "Black Friday" demonstrations demanded a salary increase, they complained that the price of medical insurance provided by the company had increased from 30 to 100 dollars per month.
For the basic employee at Walmart who receives $9.5 per hour, this is an amount he cannot afford.
Scientists at Berkley’s California University show that if Walmart raised the average salary to $12 an hour, it would cost the US$3.2 billion group.
That’s 1.1% more of what it currently spends.
If Walmart transferred the entire wage increase to the shoulders of consumers, every race in the store would cost them 46 cents more.
They'll spend in just one year only US$12.39 dollars more than now.
Walmart supporters note with satisfaction that the protests took place in just nine states and did no harm to the group.
The "Black Friday" continued in stores from 8 a.m in the evening to midnight the next day and Walmart sold over this period 5000 items per second.
Its coffers carried out a total of 100 million transactions for the "Black Friday".
Company spokesman Dan Fogleman said in an interview with the left-wing Huffington Post that "less than five" Walmart employees had left their posts and that the protest was only a "com' operation among so many others" by the organizing union.
"Next customer!"
The protests continued this week in New York and also concerned Walmart stores (in this progressive city, they are not much appreciated, which is why they are not yet established there) but McDonald's and other cheap restaurants.
McDonald’s announces selling billions of portions and then they don’t even give you sick leave and don’t pay you the job you did honestly!
Jumaane Williams, New York City Council Member
Currently, the minimum wage provided for by federal and New York legislation is $7.25 per hour.
Fast-foods increase them over time, but shortly, on average its basic employees receive $8.90 an hour in New York.
No one touches less than them in this town.
For me it's inconceivable to live with this money in New York.
There was a time when almost a quarter of American teens worked at McDonald’s after high school while living with their parents.
Few people saw it as their main source of income or wanted to stay there for a long time.
Today, I find myself interviewing McDonald's employees who are complaining about having to live with this salary and sometimes having to feed their children.
On the other hand, on the Wall Street Journal forum we find comments saying that it is irresponsible to make children if you do not know what you are going to feed them with.
Demonstrators who launched the movement Thursday at 6:30 a.m. near McDonald's on 40th Street and on Madison Avenue demanded that cashiers and fast-food cooks be paid at least $15 per hour, a multiplication of more than twice than the current minimum wage.
They also called for the creation of trade unions in the fast food sector.
U.S. law prohibits the administration from obstructing it and punishing protesters in a union through bullying or lay-off.
Administration rarely simplifies life for them.
For a trade union, seizing fast food remains a difficult task, for concrete reasons.
One of the main ones is the extraordinary rotation of staff.
They don't agree
Other tumultuous demonstrations were held close to several other fast food chains.
The highlight of the protest was the mid-day rally near Times Square’s McDonald’s with the intervention of several local Democratic policies, including that of Jumaane Williams, a member of the City Council, who said: “McDonald’s announces selling billions of portions and then they don’t even give you sick leave and don’t pay you the work you’ve done honestly!”.
The protesters were backed by other New York Democrats in sight, in particular by mayor candidate Bill De Blasio said that "we all need to support fast-food workers so that they get a fair salary and the economic well-being that every New Yorker deserves.
The New York Times has characterized this event as the most important that the fast-food industry has experienced in its history.
But only a few hundred people participated, not all of them were fast-food workers, who employ tens of thousands of people in New York.
It's currently difficult to say whether this spark of mass movement will make little ones.
"At the moment, we can only moderately deceive the brain"
Lately, fans of new technologies have become accustomed to perceiving augmented reality through special glasses.
Initially, it is Google that unveiled this summer its current model at its annual conference and in November it was learned that Microsoft had applied for the patent.
However, it emerges from the interview with the director of the interactive 3D technology group of the Microsoft laboratory in Cambridge, Shahram Izadi, that for the scientists of this company, the glasses are already ancient history.
They are tempted by the prospect of manipulating virtual objects in bare-handed airs, by the creation of open virtual spaces.
- Could you tell us in a few words what your research group is doing?
- We are working on the interaction between man and the machine and at the same time trying to broaden the boundaries of this interaction.
Humanity as a whole has remained for the moment on pixels on a flat screen and sometimes with touch.
We want to look 5 to 10 years ahead of us and guess the radical changes that will occur with this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors are a step forward, and almost no Xbox sells today without Kinect because everyone is interested in gesture control.
- What does the future hold for us too?
- Although Kinect has rendered the interaction physical, a lot of things take place on a flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
Information entry has been improved (the system receives more data), but performance, for now, not really.
We’re trying to change that and we’re working on truly 3D imaging systems running on different technologies, including projection technologies.
We have to get the computer world out in ours, in the physical world, make it more tactile.
To do this, however, it is necessary to identify not only the user, but also the space that surrounds him.
Thus, we will be able to complete the real world with virtual objects in a more practical form.
And to begin with, let’s get rid of these stupid virtual reality headsets!
- What do you think about voice control?
She's appreciated, but isn't she overestimated?
We cannot say that this is the panacea: the question of confidentiality arises because we do not always want to inform others of his actions or intentions.
In fact, each type of interaction with the computer is good, but everyone in their niche.
For example, to control devices in public places, we had a project for which we thought of gestures, not broad gestures, but rather discreet, restricted gestures.
The gestures were not captured by the camera of the device, but by a wristband worn by hand that recorded the movements of the bones and muscles.
It's currently large, but it's theoretically possible to reduce it to the size of a watch.
In fact, the future will be marked by the mixed command, for example: gesture + voice.
- That's to say? - No.
For example, how would you ask me to pass this can?
You'll do it with the word and the gestures.
- I usually only talk.
- Oh, that's gonna be hard to recognize.
- So you want to force users to adapt according to what the machine may or may not do at any given time?
- Not necessarily, but it's a mutual rapprochement.
I think that very soon we will have to work on the development of new types of sensors that will allow us to better identify the reactions of humans.
They can, for example, be laser sensors, they produce a fairly good resolution in depth and this is very important.
- If we're interested in your work on Xbox Kinect sensors, what don't you like in modern cameras?
They lack resolution, depth or anything else?
- In principle, it is about the current generation that we rely in our work on 3D recognition.
It would of course be good to get about 8 megapixels with a speed of 1000 c/s.
But the problem is not the megapixels themselves, but the quality of the matrix and the depth.
Compared to this last point, all current technologies are quite good, it adds work to algorithm designers.
It is necessary to keep in mind the resolution according to the axes X and Y, but also Z.
Speed also matters a lot, the number of frames per second.
Human movements are quite dynamic, and 30 c/s are really not enough, especially for gestures.
Steven Bathiche, of our Redmond laboratory, gave the touchdown of the sensors a regular processing delay of between 1 and 100 μs, the current sensors being closer to the second value (60-100).
Not everyone understands how this influences the interaction of man and machine.
I would need a device of this type in my work, but do not require touch, to have more frames per second.
Don't we have to increase the number of cameras?
- The Kinect now has three "cameras", one of which is in fact an infrared emitter and the second, a receiver of the reflected signal.
And the third camera is actually a usual sensor of the visible field.
It does not define the depth of the object.
A lot of cameras could solve this problem...
Or make it worse by increasing the required volume of calculations.
It would be interesting to create a flexible product similar to Kinect, play with the camera curve and see how it could help define the locations.
As far as I can remember, Microsoft, unlike Google, didn’t present its glasses to the public.
Don't you think that from the point of view of the daily use of augmented reality technologies, glasses are one of the platforms with the most perspectives?
- Of course, walking around all the time with a smartphone hands raised is not practical but here is what I think: the best option would be an "transitory" augmented reality that would allow you from a cloud platform to switch from glasses to the smartphone.
The glasses are a very personal device and that's what's their strength (the personal elements are the only ones you're the only one to see) and at the same time their weakness: Augmented reality from glasses won't allow you to work on virtual objects in collaboration with other people.
- Let's imagine for a moment that the manipulation of virtual holographic objects in the air is only accessible to Tony Stark in Iron Man but also to mere mortality.
There is a problem on which opponents of this idea point the finger: there is no tactile sensation!
Hands feel nothing!
What answers does your group prepare for this challenge?
- In my classes, I often say that augmented reality is the seventh breakthrough in the interaction between man and machine.
I think that the eighth could very well be the addition of tactile sensations.
At the moment, one of the interesting techniques is the use of the second hand to serve as an image support.
It perfectly records the pressures!
But there are also technologies that are actually intended to attribute palpability to these "images in the air": for example the interference of several ultrasonic rays directed towards a precise point where the finger is located and which gives you the sensation, for the moment still weak, of a breath of air on the tips of the fingers.
There are also bracelets worn on the wrists that influence the nerve endings of the fingers, they are also an axis of work with perspectives.
- Have you been trying to fool the brain?
Make him believe that he feels what he should normally feel by the moment he sees something?
That's a good idea, we haven't tried yet.
There’s still a task down that’s going to struggle for a long time: how to make someone believe physically in a very small space that they’re walking in an almost infinite open space — we’re working on the concepts of treadmills (nothing to do with those found in fitness clubs), mobile platforms and also giant balls.
For the moment, we can only moderately deceive the brain, there are still many years of work.
That’s what makes work on virtual reality so interesting for the researcher: many things are still in the absolute state of fertilization.
The reign of taste and not culture - Rosbalt.ru
"Rosbalt" continues the project "L'avant-garde petersbourgeois" dedicated to urban dwellers who find themselves in advance, at the forefront of culture and art.
This ultra-selective list has already hosted eminent actors of the art scene of St. Petersburg whose achievements go beyond the border of the city and who have found recognition in Europe, having been known throughout Russia.
"Rosbalt's new hero" is extroverted painter Kirill Miller.
The whole city knows Kirill Miller, this rather bearded man, all dressed in red that can be met near the Russian Museum, the Summer Garden or even in trendy celebrations and vernissages.
Kirill Miller's paintings always bring together the crowds, regardless of their exhibition space.
He is one of the fundamentally social and philosophical petersbourgeois storytellers, one of the founders of the new myths.
Kirill Miller is a distinguished representative of the Petersbourg avant-garde of the late 1980's and the early 1990's.
It is also the symbol of the city, its appearance makes the inhabitants smile and puts them in a good mood.
He recently took in his hands an organ of Barbary and he became the symbol of this instrument in St. Petersburg since he had matured enough for this difficult role because of his existence, his philosophy and his image as a bohemian.
- Kirill, why do you travel the city all in red and not in yellow or turquoise blue for example?
- I chose red as a designer specialized in appearance and image.
In this world, red is a compromise between the painter, the image creator and society.
Even if in society, everything that is not grey arouses aggressiveness and negative agitation.
However, my provocations are intended to begin the discussion.
The whole history of my provocative actions is an invitation to discussion.
- And when did you understand that you had to be a painter?
- At the exhibition of the Nevskiy Palace of Cultivation where my works were located.
I understood that this was my way.
Then the wave of unofficial old painters had passed and the new, free, in my kind, were misunderstood.
I began to make friends with the painters of the new wave, with those of the Postgazonevchchina who gave birth to "Pouchinskaya-10" and then there was no more wave.
I am interested in theatre, fashion, music, all genres except words.
- And all of this ended up in your artistic clinic... it was important for me to find myself at the center of culture in St. Petersburg where the best creative forces must come together.
In 1995, I took a place in Pouchkinskaya-10 which housed before the works a musical and artistic club, a bohemian club, the house of the bohemian Petersburg.
Many were born: The NAMES, Tequila-jazz, I still see Chnour coming with the project "Van Gogh's Ear".
Chnour and his friends were singing easy songs in play-backs in tight-fitting jerseys, they were among them Igor Vdovin, now fashionable composer.
When the band started performing live, it became "Leningrad".
Trakhtenberg hosted quite a lot of broadcasts before Hali Gali.
We gave them Trakhtenberg and it was then that his great career began, but his training and basic education, it was at home that he received them.
Galerie "D137", Club "Griboïedov": these are repercussions of the artistic clinic.
Our employees went there as accustomed.
I'm a hero of the last century, of when culture meant something.
In 2000, a poll was organized in the press: this was the prize "The personalities of our city".
I've been named "Painter of the Year," and that's when my reign ended.
It’s not convenient to work in a new era with old rules, I’m a representative of the truth, honesty and culture of the past century.
Today, it's easy to get popular, but culture and popularity are different things, one can be popular, but not really a person of culture.
- Your works are characterized by a recognizable style.
- Many of my paintings are hits, they have a very strong news and acuity.
I'm going to have an exhibition scheduled "The Russian Museum Through the Clowns".
Clowns belong to a timeless category.
Before I was someone social, today it hurts and it's scary to be like that.
But together with the clowns, everything disappears, the tragic falls.
I love the grotesque. I have grotesque ideas.
For example, the idea of saving the world through total and mandatory disguise.
Today, it is the habit that matters, we are interested in the external mechanism, but not inside the person.
Maybe we can't give him a hand, but you have to spit in his face.
And the lie will be erased by disguise.
- Recently we've seen you as Barbarie's organ player. - A cultural city must have this type of hero.
Which but me can play it.
- But maybe commercial art can also be beautiful?
- Today commercial art must be cared for, delicate, sweet.
A disintegration of culture is happening.
Before, people were divided into herds, bohemians loved one thing, denied another.
Today, everything is divided into micro-communities, it's difficult to please everyone.
I'm not a hundred-dollar bill to please everyone.
Now you have to think about who you're going to like.
Each star has 100 fans.
- But several thousand people come to see Stas Mikhailov!
- They're the people we've discarded who come to see him, it's the sexual and social side walking.
But for the cultural side, 300 people will come, 10 miles will not come and in the end it's less management, less money, everything withers.
I've got my fans, the main thing is not to cheat on them, not to ruin the experience.
When I was young, I painted paintings that a collector hung next to Falk or Larionov.
I started with the paintings that are usually the ones we paint at the end of life.
Ideas are often confused today.
They say: there is the spiritual culture, the culture of consumption.
Consumption doesn't have culture, it has nothing to do.
I am a representative of the culture of yesterday, I grew up with the example of painters who lived in poverty and died in poverty and who refused money precisely for painting.
I'm for that culture.
- Kirill, what's missing in St. Petersburg?
- Good cultural experts.
There is this concept: the civil servant of culture.
Not everyone can work in culture.
Under the reign of the good tsars, it wasn't like that, the tsars didn't fully understand the culture, but they understood that we had to keep good experts.
There are good consultants in Moscow today.
We have people here in St. Petersburg who might be experts, but they are discarded, as we need higher experts who will properly evaluate these experts and make their way.
Currently, it is the "reign of taste" that blooms.
The same Erarta, but they differ in the sense that they honestly say that they do not take all contemporary art, that there are painters who have to find other museums.
- For you, what's St. Petersburg?
- St. Petersburg is not the cultural capital, there is in Moscow much more culture, there is soil for this.
And on our stones, art struggles to grow.
We need a cultural soil and we have more writers than readers, it's bad.
In Europe, there are a lot of curious people who will see exhibitions, concerts.
With us, this layer is very thin.
We have to make art fashionable as was the case at the beginning of the last century.
Project carried out with the support of the St. Petersburg Scholarship
Giving birth in space
Earth is in danger.
Global warming or collision with a deadly asteroid.
Caravans of space shuttles with earthlings on board go in search of a rescue planet.
To save humanity, a big question arises: how to continue to make our species live in weightlessness or even on a relief planet?
I don't think there's much choice.
There are in fact two real planets that can be conquered, if only by hypothesis.
“It’s about Venus and Mars,” says Vladimir Sourdine, a researcher at the State Astronomical Institute P.K. Chternberg (GAICh).
If on Mars the conditions are more suitable for life, the temperature at the surface of Venus is 500.
You can only live there at a very high altitude or on the orbit of Venus... in space.
We began to look at the issues of reproduction in space by focusing on flora.
Another half-century ago, experiments on plants were carried out.
Four generations of peas grown in orbiting did not differ in any way from earthly varieties.
Then, insects were put into orbit, small drosophilian flies breeding.
In 1979, quail eggs were sent into space to see how a weightless embryo developed.
A completely normal chick is obtained at the outlet.
It is then that the problems begin.
"The problem is related to the fact that the chick has to find support, he has to put himself on his feet and begin to move," explains the director of the laboratory of the Institute of Medico-Biological Problems of the Russian Academy of Sciences (IMBP) Vladimir Sychev.
By finding no support, the chicks were agitated disorderly.
And after 10 hours, the newborns saw their instincts completely atrophy.
Chicks didn’t react to light or sound.
Unfortunately, they would simply die after four days.
"We've spanked their eyes twice, then we've given up, because it's simply impossible to work with them there," Sychev said.
The last biological "miniature arch" with animals was sent into orbit 16 years ago.
The experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, biosatellite "Bion" will only embed same-sex species.
There was an experiment with rats sent with their fetus into space.
In principle, nothing supernatural has been discovered.
"It was still biosatellites and then it's the only experiment. We have to do this kind of research," says Vladimir Sychev.
After landing, space rats gave birth to their offspring.
However, it is not yet possible to perform reproductions directly in space.
That's no small feat.
By losing their usual environment, animals are no longer able to realize their sexual instinct.
Contrary to the beasts, man is capable, in principle.
Homo sapiens possesses abstract thought, it is able to create a suitable emotional background.
This type of experience is not carried out for ethical reasons.
But women have been going into space for 50 years.
The biggest risk was taken by Tereshkova.
And the female organism is the most precious that humanity has.
Our seagull flew away and no one on Earth could tell if it would last after a space flight.
If she could have given birth to a child after the theft.
“No one was answering this question,” says Vakhtang Vatchnadzé, a retired rocket and space sector.
In June 1964, just one year after her theft in space, the first cosmonaut woman Valentina Terechkova gave birth to a little girl.
The child's father, Andrian Nikolayev was also a cosmonaut.
In 1988, the second female cosmonaut Svetlana Savitskaya, after having been twice in orbit and having even worked in an extra-vehicular environment, gave birth to a son.
However the risk persists.
We have very few cosmonauts who have had nothing and who give birth to a healthy offspring after long thefts.
"And if they are longer, it poses a danger even during flights in orbit," concludes the pilot and cosmonaut, hero of the Soviet Union and heroes of Russia, Valeriy Poliakov.
However, humanity must seek new solutions in biotechnology, protection against radiation and the creation of artificial gravity.
Hydrology laboratory of the Cosmonaut Preparation Center: mandatory stage of flight preparation.
Here, in zero buoyancy, cosmonauts train extra-vehicular outputs.
The water mimics the weightlessness.
If for adults, the water is comfortable but nevertheless a foreign environment, for infants, it is their real element.
Small amphibious men seem to confirm this: life on Earth comes from the ocean.
Since the baby evolves about 9 months in his mother’s belly lymphatic fluid, it is easier for him to get used to the water.
It is in principle logical, because from his birth until his first bath, only 2 weeks goes by.
"It's very little to forget," explains infant swimming instructor Marina Aksenova.
In a word, if for the child who has just born the weightlessness is a more natural state, the woman needs gravitation, a force of weight.
In weightlessness, the muscles of the belly, pelvis atrophy very quickly, the baby's ability to push is lost.
All right, let's don't know that by relying on the delivery simulators.
We can, of course, prepare a childbirth in a special room, the woman will manage to get him out.
"And then?" asks Valeriy Poliakov to invite you to reflect on this complex issue.
On the other hand, the child also needs artificial gravity.
If it does not feel the earth's attraction, the body does not form the skeleton or the muscular system.
You can’t dress an infant like an adult with a special combination for workouts.
He will simply be deprived of his vital needs.
"And this experience that we would consider in this case by imagining the birth of a child in a foreign environment will lead to the return to Earth of a handicapped child, fully disadapted," predicts the Director of the Bioethics Committee of the Institute of Medico-Biological Problems of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Igor Pestov.
The birth of children in space is still theoretical.
However, over time this will become a reality, when the Earthlings go into their shuttles to a distant planet that will become the habitat of their offspring born in space.
The head of the ISC (Higher Institution for the Control of Public Finances) said: "the control of the pseudo-self-employment (in the Czech "Švarc system") failed because of the politicians.
In the field of public procurement, the Czech Republic has qualified inspectors and high-quality legislation, but in terms of its implementation.
Miloslav Kala, the vice-president of the Higher Ministry of Public Finance Supervisory in an interview for Aktuálně.cz, said.
“The law will never be perfect, but its application must be fair and, in my opinion, that’s what’s missing with us,” Kala said in her commentation on the current situation.
A joint review by Czech and German inspectors also suggests similar conclusions.
As an example of an abusive practice, Petr Nečas's approach to the "Švarc system".
Mr. Prime Minister recently stated that the SDO is not going to bother contractors with controls, so is it permissible or prohibited?
“The law must be enforced in one way or another, so if it forbids something, even the head of government can’t help act an authority tasked with going to inspect to demand compliance with the law,” Kala said.
At the meeting of the Monitoring Committee in the Chamber of Deputies, you referred to a Czecho-German project in which the public procurement legislation of these two countries was compared.
What was there a concrete question of?
This is a parallel control that we launched about two years ago.
In simple terms, European legislation provides for ways in which to manage public procurements and specific national legislation, as well as a practice itself, follow.
We have gathered all this and although this control procedure has not yet been completed, very interesting differences have appeared to us: generally speaking, our legislation is perhaps "morely strict and of better quality", nevertheless, according to some parameters, the practice as such is better in Germany.
This therefore confirms that it is not sufficient to continuously develop increasingly detailed rules, but that it is necessary to devote oneself to the very implementation of the law.
How can this project help you concretely and what happens to you?
A common control of this kind should precisely contribute to what we stop trying to clarify the laws, to lower and improve the thresholds, when it does not have a really positive effect.
The principle of good management means obtaining the thing in question at a reasonable price (which does not always mean the lowest), in order to exclude any enrichment and possible criminal activity.
But by lowering the thresholds of the markets, we will not necessarily reach this goal.
It could be that this system is so overwhelmed by paperwork, that those who are looking for the flaws will find much more opportunities than if the threshold had remained higher.
You are talking about internal problems relating to the implementation of public procurement legislation.
How does the control system in Germany work?
Is there an institution analogous to our ISC or a different mode of organisation?
With regard to institutions, similarly to the CSI, the Bundesrechnungshof, which is similarly organised, it also has a council constituted a little differently, but these two institutions proceed fundamentally in a similar way .
Competencies are also, to a certain extent, similar, with a nuance however in relation to our system: Germany being a federal structure, it therefore also has courses of the accounts of this type at the level of the different Länder.
The BRH can only control federal money, which corresponds here to state resources.
The public funds managed here by the regions and the municipalities, are checked there at the level of the Länder accounts.
When it comes to national legislation, is it simpler than ours?
I do not want to make a general comparison without concrete data, however, in some areas Germany is often referred to as a model, but it cannot surely be said that it is doing better in all areas.
Maybe it's due to the fact that they have better detectives?
No, surely not, but I repeat, I do not have comparison data.
However, it must also be said that this country, which we consider as a model, is also facing a whole range of problems.
Otherwise they wouldn't have any advantage in cooperating with our institution, right?
With regard to national legislation, what did the amendment of the Public Procurement Law have signified for you as an institution, does its effects are already felt?
Since this amendment came into force relatively recently, it does not yet have an impact on our control operations.
As we do ex post checks, we have to count with some delay.
Our observations concern only, for the moment, the process of preparing future controls - we have launched a new instrument of "detection of the risks of mismanagement" with which we have screened nearly 14 000 public contracts - these changes will of course be felt, because the thresholds have been modified as well as the conditions of certain types of tendering procedures and so on.
Do you therefore regard the adoption of this law rather as an advantage or as an additional burden for the administration?
I believe that this law is a step in the right direction and I hope that the future will confirm it.
The problem that may arise is that these rules become stricter and it is not possible to respect them.
Already under the previous regulations, the claimant (e.g
Is it really helpful to make the law tougher in this way?
No, not this way, I think.
The system must exclude those who want to attack or abuse the system but not punish those who make formal errors without consequence on the decision-making process.
Such a system will put more pressure on the administration.
So what's the solution to get away with it?
Let's look where we're not going.
Recently, the Prime Minister said that the ODS was not going to bother contractors with controls from the so-called Švarc system - what does that mean?
Is the Švarc system prohibited or allowed?
The law must well be applied in one way or another, so if it prohibits something, even the head of government cannot prevent an authority responsible from going to inspect and require compliance with the law.
He can say, “We’re going to change the law and allow it,” but he can’t say we’re going to act like nothing happened.
The Law on Contracts has relatively strict rules on the formalities to be completed precisely in order to protect the tender.
However, it is also tragic that a bidder should be eliminated solely because of formal flaws.
The law will never be perfect, but its application must be fair and that is, in my opinion, what is lacking in us.
The roads are in frozen places, but most remain passable.
In some parts of the Czech Republic, the roads are frozen and also snowy.
However, the network remains largely passable, in some places with increased vigilance.
Karlovy Vary
In the Karlovy Vary area, the roads were passable this morning, but in frozen and also snowy places.
Temperatures have dropped and oscillate between -5 ° and -10 ° C but they should rise slightly in the day.
The snowfall has stopped in the region and only a thin layer of snow remains in the plains.
However, the ridges of the Metalliferous Mountains (Krušné hory) are covered with about 30 centimeters of snow.
Data from the Regional Road Maintenance Service indicated that due to fog, visibility was reduced in places.
The R6 expressway and the region’s national roads are now without difficulties.
However, however, vigilance remains, for example, on certain decks whose coating can be frozen and slipping.
All departmental roads and third category lanes, including mountain roads, are also passable.
On some of their stretches of frozen snow remains and in traffic-packed places remain on some of their stretches.
Particularly in high-lying areas, drivers need to increase vigilance.
Pardubice and Hradec Králové regions
The Directorate of Roads and Highways reports on its website that some East Bohemian roads are threatened by the formation of ice and that layers of traffic-packed snow can be found on roads in mountainous areas and located at altitude.
Road maintenance services warn drivers against the risks of ice formation in areas of the Pardubice region located at altitude.
There may be ice in the vicinity of Lanškroun, Ústí nad Orlicí, Polička, Svitavy or Vysoké Mýto, and this, mainly on departmental and third category roads.
Roads I/43 and I/34 around Svitavy were chemically treated.
Snow is mainly found in the Giant Mountains (Krkonoše) and the Aigle Mountains (Orlické hory).
In Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov districts, a traffic-packed layer of snow is present on roads in high-lying areas.
In Eastern Bohemia, the weather today will be dry, most often clear, with possible cloudy passages.
It will blow a weak wind and temperatures will usually oscillate between -3 ° and + 1 ° C.
Pilsen Region
Roads in the Pilsen region are now passable, although, in places, it is necessary to show increased vigilance and to adapt its driving to weather conditions.
It freezes this morning, temperatures oscillate between -3 ° and -9 ° C.
After the latest snowfall and cooling that followed, some communication routes can be in frozen places.
Drivers can expect to pass through foggy areas, but visibility will gradually improve.
This is reflected in the information provided by the Regional Road Maintenance Service.
The D5 motorway can be practiced almost without difficulties, but road communications maintenance services recommend increased vigilance between kilometers 80 and 131.
The siding of most national roads is dry and frozen.
Some sections can be frozen in South Pilsen district and Tashov district.
The departmental and third category tracks are wet and in this case applies the warning against frozen sections.
Drivers must be vigilant, especially on the less crowded roads in the Šumava region.
Olomouc region
Drivers travelling to areas of the Olomouc region located at altitude must today count with a layer of thick and soft snow on the roads.
This layer remained on the so-called "Červenohorské sedlo" pass and on the road towards Videlský kříž, after the chemical processing operations.
Snow fell last night forced road maintenance personnel to go to the field; according to the services in charge of the road, more than three centimetres fell in the Šumperk district.
In other parts of the area, most pavements are passable without difficulties.
On the heights of Šumperk district are leftovers of snow.
Drivers travelling towards Jeseník should expect to meet at the so-called "Červenohorské sedlo" pass a layer of thick and soft snow," the head of the Šumperk district road maintenance services told ČTK today.
Their colleagues in Jeseník district have also visited during the night; according to them, after the chemical treatment carried out, the roads, are now cleared and wet, even in the highest areas.
In the district of Olomouc the roads are passable without difficulty, in the district of Šternberk drivers should, however, pay attention in the sections in forested areas with roads that have remained humid.
Ústí nad Labem and Liberec regions
As early as this morning, North Bohemian road maintenance services reported several difficult sections.
According to police data, in addition to some places covered with snow or ice, the mountain road from Telnice to Knínice na Ústecku is closed.
Temperatures will remain below zero at lower altitudes, and therefore the snow and ice should be maintained on the roads. In the plains, especially in the south-east of the Bohème Central Plateau, there is, on the contrary, no problem and the roads are generally dry.
No traffic difficulties have been reported at this time.
Road maintenance services have reported the presence of ice, particularly around Štětí.
According to the meteorologists, the nocturnal conditions were ideal for its formation: rain and melting snow during the day, unobstructed skies and frost during the night.
From the main routes of traffic, difficult conditions on the national road 13 at the border between the regions of Ústí nad Labem and Liberec are also reported.
The closure of traffic between Telnice and Knínice is due to branches of trees that have curved under the weight of the snow until it reaches the pavement.
Šimon Ornest said: “In concert, we want to collect positive energy.”
What do you think about the fact that in less than a month it should be the end of the world?
It's just one of those alarming news, the kind of traps we like to fall into.
In our group The Tap Tap, we laugh about it and we think that we are the only band able to collect a remnant of positive energy in order to repel or prevent the end of the world.
In addition, in December, you set up a quite unique project: three concerts against the end of the world.
Can you present this project to our readers in more detail?
It's a charitable action that we've been planning for two years already.
We have decided to use the marketing potential of the end of the Mayan calendar announced for December 21 at 11 a.m.
The day before, on December 20, three concerts will take place simultaneously, in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
They will end practically at the time when the Mayan calendar will end on Kiribati Island which is in the Pacific Ocean, on a time zone in advance of 12 hours compared to us.
Who got that idea?
Initially I think it was my idea, then we designed everything with our creator, Honza Augusta.
Apart from the fact that we want to collect enough positive energy to save the world, we also want together, with the public, to reflect on the state of the world that we leave to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar, we have created a collection of unique items, including shoes, T-shirts, bags, and original keys against the end of the world, buying them online at www.e-tap.cz people can also support us.
The Tap Tap with other performers has also recorded an anthem against the end of the world entitled "End of the World cancelled" (in Czech "Konec světa zrušeno").
It is already very popular on YouTube, will we also hear it during these charity concerts?
Of course, all at the end, finally, if we succeed and the end of the world doesn’t happen...
It will be sung simultaneously by all performers in all three concerts.
Hymns will also meet at some point as part of a live show, quite unique, of Czech television.
The lyrics of this song were written by Tomáš Hanák who also plays the main role of Jesus in the clip, Xindl X also sings...
How did you come to cooperate with them?
We collaborate with other personalities of the Czech show-biz, due to the fact that we organize many concerts and charity events...
We strive to involve them intensively in these projects.
It turned out that most of them are interested in such cooperation and it amuses them.
What will the benefits of these concerts against the end of the world be used for?
Equipping the Studeo training centre, which is accessible to people with reduced mobility and which we are working to achieve within the framework of the Jedlička Institute, with the Tap association, and this for six years.
Teachers go regularly to the students of the Jedličkův Institute and offer them activities that interest and amuse them.
The students themselves do not have the means to go to classes, we try to help them in this way.
As part of the completion of the construction of the Jedlička Institute, we will transfer this project to a new independent building.
Several music bands and performers will go on stage during each of these concerts?
By what criteria, do you choose them?
We have tried to develop a program for all generations, including children.
For example, in Prague, Chinaski, Support Lesbians, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý will take the stage.
Full details can be found at www.kpks.cz
Are you also preparing for other "mega events" of this kind in the future?
In May, we will play for the first time as part of the Prague Spring; here too, we will surely prepare a quality program with interesting guests.
We’d also like to play next year at the House of the Czech Republic in New York and personally, once there in the United States, I’d like to combine concerts in Washington and Chicago.
Your international projects are minimalist, so far you have played for example in Madrid, Brussels, London and Moscow.
The Tap Tap is, however, a group of people with disabilities.
How do you manage the organization and logistics of your tours?
It's not as bad as it sounds at first.
We have five members in an electric chair whose machines have to travel in the hold, of course we also carry a lot of luggage and boxes of instruments....
So far everything has gone more or less always well, ČSA and British Airways were always perfectly prepared for us, they surprised me more than once.
Even in Moscow, from where we came back recently, everything went smoothly.
Thanks to your trips abroad, you can compare how these countries differ in terms of access facilities for people with reduced mobility, the attitude of public opinion towards people with disabilities and so on.
What have been your experiences so far?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other places where everything works better than at home, we have met precisely in Moscow that in the east all is still only in an initial phase.
Compared to Prague, Moscow is still very little accessible to people with disabilities; there it is not yet common for a person to travel alone in an electric chair in the center of the city.
They do not yet consider it obvious to give priority to a person in an elevator.
Luckily, there, too, an association trying to draw attention to the problems of people with disabilities is being created.
How do we still have to catch up with the more developed countries?
There's a lot that we're late for.
It is important to note that improvements and refinements still depend on the efforts of those involved.
In London and Madrid, it is quite common that people with a heavy disability move alone in public spaces; they can go to the bathroom, the museum, anywhere...
It is still not very common there that a large number of people with disabilities actively participate in social life, on this point, you, The Tap Tap, are rather at the forefront of progress!
Respect for the public and access facilities are one thing, but the situation will really begin to change when we have famous athletes, artists, actors, politicians or jurists among us.
At the moment, these are only isolated cases of people who have a strong will.
The band The Tap Tap is currently very popular, let's go back a few years, what prompted you in 1998 to create this band?
I started working as an educator at Jedlička Institute, where I found myself surrounded by a lot of young people ready to invest in something.
And since I am myself a musician (I play, among others, saxophone), with a colleague, we created a music activity.
And then, over time, as our moderator Láďa Angelovič says, we've lost a little control of the situation (laughs).
Success has only happeneded in recent years if I'm not mistaken?
It's true, creating connections with well-known singers and actively working to promote the band has helped us.
We understood that, if a job is not visible, it is as if it does not exist in substance.
Thanks to subsidies from the European Union, we can, in addition, allow ourselves to have quality teachers, equipment, and so on.
Were yoe aiming to achieve this level with The Tap Tap?
From the very beginning, I felt that there was potential to change things.
Show business is full of things that copy each other.
In a way, it makes sense: all that is new is accepted cautiously and it lasts for a long time.
Rare are the original projects, and I dare to say that Tap Tap is one of them.
The first impression of a person who sees us for the first time is naturally pity, it’s natural...
But pity is totally useless, because people with disabilities are not suffering beings, abandoned by all, that we have to take pity.
They are people who can live a life in their own right and flourish, provided they have the necessary means of course.
I often say that when people with a disability manage to achieve something, it is not only an advance for them but for the whole of society.
Isn’t your success also due to the fact that you’re a demanding leader, as you’re qualified by many people?
If we want to do quality work, we have to be uncompromising in many areas and demand some discipline.
But I think it's perfectly normal.
Some people come to us with a romantic vision and head in the clouds and, when they realize that they have to come to rehearsals twice a week, follow the preparatory classes and spend a lot of time on the roads for concerts, they quickly lose their enthusiasm.
But it works in the same way in every group that wants to work and achieve goals.
The Tap Tap Group currently has twenty members.
How many of them were there in 1998?
Only one, Láďa Angelovič.
We're an open group, people come and go, we can't do anything.
Our doors are always open to those who want or would want to join us.
This will be the case the day before the planned end of the world, Thursday, December 20, 2012 from 9 p.m.
This action will take place in Prague in the Incheba arena, in Brno in Fléda and Ostrava in Plynojem with the participation of twelve bands and other musicians from the Czech Republic.
At the end of the evening, these three cities will be connected by audiovisual relay when the song of The Tap Tap "Fin of the world cancelled" will be sung together.
The aim of the concert is to gather financial resources (up to 25 million CZK) to equip the Studeo Multifunctional Training Center in Prague in the Jedlička Institute.
The price of a ticket to this concert is 400 CZK, admission is free for children up to 12 years old, pre-sale tickets are open in Bohemiaticket.
Poland and the cosmos.
Last week, at the meeting of ministers of the countries of the European Space Agency, Poland was admitted as a twentieth member of this agency, it is the second Member State of the former Eastern Bloc (the Czech Republic became a full member on 12 November 2008).
Poland had started close cooperation with ESA in 1994 and in the following years participated in a number of the Agency's projects.
However, Poland's road to space began much earlier.
Already before the Second World War, passionate Poles were working on spaceflight; they did not always meet understanding.
For example, I would like to mention A. Šternfeld's lecture at the Warsaw Astronomical Observatory on 6 December 1933, during which he presented ideas drawn from his avant-garde work "Introduction to Astronautics".
The developments of this young engineer (born in 1905) left the listeners cold and years later Šternfeld recalled that only Dr. Jan Gadomski was interested in his work.
In 1934, Šternfeld won the Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch Prize in France for his book "Introduction to Astronautics".
Doctor Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966) later became a great propagator of astronomy and astronautics.
He published hundreds of articles in Polish newspapers and wrote numerous books devoted to this scientific discipline.
Gadomski has become a propagator of the world-renowned astronautics, his contribution was rewarded in particular by the fact that his name was given to one of the craters on the far side of the Moon.
As early as 1925 a draisine was built in Poland to be equipped with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, we do not know the author or the details of this project.
It is also not clear whether the rocket should be used to propel or brake the draisine.
The only well-known information on this draisine is that of the press of the time.
From 19303, the Polish artillery began to devote itself to rockets.
The research was conducted by the Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia Institute in collaboration with Professors Mieczyslaw Wolfke and Gustaw Mokrzyckiego.
It appears from the documents retained that this research had reached the testing stage.
Of course, with the arrival of the German armies, the search stopped.
In 1937, the plans of a photoelectric fighter rocket, designed by engineer Rohoziński, appeared in the specialized press and the following year was published the book "Rakieta - torpeda powietrzna i rakietobomba lotnicza" by an authort named Leliwy-Krzywoblocki.
These two projects were intended for military use of rocket engines.
Just before the war, all plans to use the rocket technique were carried out by the Provisional Scientific Advisory Committee ("Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy"), which coordinated all work.
This committee had been established in 1937, but after two years of operation, the beginning of the war ended its activities.
After the war, other works devoted to aeronautics were published in the Polish press, thanks to the Polish astronautic society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
For the first time, there is talk of a company in the November 1954 issue of the newspaper Problem, in which there are four more detailed articles devoted to aeronautics.
In one of these articles, written by Professor Mr. Subotowicze, it is proposed to create a society dedicated to astronautics
At that time, artificial satellite projects had already been launched and it was clear that the field of space research had a future.
At the beginning of 1956, the Polish Astronautical Society (PTA) made efforts to enter the International Astronautical Federation (created in 1951) and in the autumn the PTA became a regular member.
In the following year, the first president of the PTA, Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902 - 1959), became Vice-President of the International Astronautical Federation.
He retained his office until his death in 1959.
Beginning in 1956, the PTA made a significant contribution to the successful development of the RM (Rakieta Meteorologiczna) meteorological rocket, which became the first Polish rocket to conduct scientific research.
The first model, RM-1, was completed in 1957 and the first launch took place on October 10, 1958.
This rocket with a range of 1800 meters had a length of 80 cm and weighed a little less than 5 kg.
Subsequently an upgraded version, RM-1A and, in the summer of 1959, flight tests of the two-storey RM-2 rocket were initiated in the Bledov Desert.
This rocket had a length of 1.4 meters and probably weighed 11.5 kg.
The next prototype should have been able to carry out concrete scientific work - the RM-34 rocket should have had a range of up to 14.5 km and been used to observe the wind at altitude.
However, in 1963, all development work was halted.
The Meteor-1 rocket developed during the period 1962-1965, succeeded to the first RM-type rockets.
This rocket, designed with two stages, had a total length of 510 cm and an initial weight of 32.5 kg.
Three models (called Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C) were developed; they were distinguished by the dimensions of the space reserved for scientific tools.
The Meteor-1A rocket had a space with a volume of 0.4 liters, while in Meteor-1B this volume was 0.34 liters and in Meteor-1C of 0.62 liters.
The maximum range of all types of machinery was 37 km.
In the period 1965 - 1968, Meteor-2 rockets were developed in the aeronautical institute, the first flight tests began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had an initial weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a payload of 10 kg at an altitude of about 60 km.
Subsequently the Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 versions were constructed.
It is still worth mentioning Poland's entry into the COSPAR (Committee for Space Research (COSPAR) in 1960 and the creation of a COSPAR national committee two years later.
As part of the Interkosmos programme, Poland also participated in space exploration on artificial Soviet satellites and, in 1978, pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became, after Vladimír Remek, the second non-Soviet cosmonaut of this program.
Repealing the law on works of public utility is not a solution.
Last week, the Constitutional Court repealed Public Works Act.
This decision raised a strong discussion in public opinion.
Undoubtedly, it is interesting to examine this issue from a broader perspective.
Liberalism-oriented economic systems, both in the EU and in the context of globalisation, are based on the principle of more unregulated economic competition.
This concretely means that the different economic entities as well as the national economic systems are perpetually in conflict with one another.
This stems from the principle of freedom of trade and free (limitless) movement of private capital combined with unbridled financial speculation.
Considerable differences in the price of labour (salaries) give rise to pressure on prices.
The following meaning should be ended: a producer strives by importing low-lying goods sold to withstand economic competition by "making the competitor's price more expensive", with the aim of removing a greater share of the market and thus increasing its own profits.
On a large scale, this means for most entrepreneurs: either transferring production abroad, buying at low prices, or disappearing. The result is significant unemployment in countries where labour prices are high compared to other economies.
Given that private capital has no responsibility from a social point of view and thus of responsibility for the unemployment it generates, the state's social charges necessarily increase.
All this situation intensifies the perfect bad will of gentlemen entrepreneurs to pay taxes and thus to compensate for the economic and social damage caused to the whole of society by this race for profit.
This situation is at such a point of public awareness that it is not necessary to provide concrete statistical data.
Unscrupulous practices of private capital generate in the various economies a situation in which the governments of the different countries are forced to engage in mutual competition in order to artificially lower the social level of their own population in order to attract foreign capital.
In other words, governments put their own people at the mercy of private capital without taking into account social collapse.
This is mainly reflected in the amendments to the laws in force.
The aim is to constrain economically its own population to accept the timing of prices of private capital, particularly in the field of wages.
On the one hand, this occurs through a system of economic constraints in the event of long-term unemployment and, on the other hand, by restricting the rights of employees in the field of labour legislation.
The result is increasing poverty and deepening the differences between the rich and the poor.
In the RFA, there have been public food distribution points for years for the poor who cannot provide for themselves with their own work.
There are already the millions of these people.
As part of the increase in the competitiveness of the German economy, it is becoming quite widespread for employees to receive such wages that the state is forced to pay an additional fee so that they reach the subsistence minimum.
For example, such a scandal has been uncovered in the case of employees working as auxiliaries in the federal parliament.
The economic measures of virtually all the Member States in the south of the EU undoubtedly generate a similar situation in which, under the pressure of a catastrophic collapse of society, people are forced either to emigrate (as was the case in the 19th century), or to live with wages of misery, on the margins of society, in the hope that one day private capital will invest in their country.
You have to ask the following question: where will this capital come from?
If it comes from other EU countries, then poverty will pass from one country to another; this capital may not come either, or a Chinese, an Indian, a Brazilian, a Turk, a Moroccan, an Egyptian or African will continue to work for a fraction of a European salary.
This also applies to all of Latin America.
The liberal theory and the media claim, by getting tired, that the state cannot make capital investments in its own economy, that the directed economy leads to an economic catastrophe.
Private capital strongly defends the idea that the state cannot intervene in the economy through regulatory interventions.
We must then ask ourselves whether private capital does not influence and, even, does not rule today, according to its own selfish interests, politics and at the same time, the whole state.
The answer to this question is without a doubt yes.
Proof of this is that there is an almost all-powerful lobby present in every state.
As a result, there is a hopeless situation in the field of corruption, through the granting of mutual benefits in legislation, where everything is condemnable but not punishable.
In the FRF, the situation is such that, due to the lack of financial resources, national ministries delegate the development of bills to law firms closely related to the industry.
Those bills were then passed in the Bundestag.
The totality of power does not come from the people, as declared by Western-style constitutions, but from powerful financial groups serving their own interests.
It is evident that democracies oriented towards liberalism are now rapidly confronted with the situation described by Appianos in his book entitled "The Crisis of the Roman Empire in the Time of Caesar and Pompey": "The State had long been in full decay and the functions were attributed by violence.
Bribe blows, favors obtained illegally, and stones and swords were also used to obtain these posts.
Below-and-the-table and corruption proliferated without limits and the people went to the elections with votes already sold"... ... "Honest people did not seek to obtain functions, the result once the state remained 8 months without consuls"... "We really began to say that the remedy to this difficult situation was an autocracy and that it was necessary to choose an energetic man".
In conclusion, as in antiquity, today's society is based on the preference of personal profit, without regard to the interest of society as a whole.
In its essence, private capital is no longer capable of understanding and asserting the interests of society as a whole.
The result is, now and in the past, decadence without precedenting elites, without any effort to carry out far-reaching reforms.
We must therefore seek the causes of the emergence of fascist and communist regimes in the unscrupulous liberalization of the economic systems of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.
As things stand, the disappearance of these systems for the benefit of democracies of the liberal type can be seen as a kind of pause before a new stage.
The fact that the current elites completely ignore the possibility of hundreds of thousands of future loss of life, the humanitarian and social crises of which we are witnesses today as well as the crimes against humanity as we know them from ancient and recent history.
Repealing the law on works of public utility is not a solution, at least in the long term.
Under the pressure of economic competition, both internationally and at European internal level, the Government of the Czech Republic will be forced to continue to look for ways to reduce the social level of the population.
Indeed, this trend is systemic.
The solution consists of socio-political reforms, which will strengthen state capital investments in the economy, increase citizens' influence on the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the state.
Israel: Laboratory of Chaos.
"Nothing will ever come out of the violence and never can" says Sting in the song entitled Fragile whose main verse of the chorus is "let's not forget how fragile we are".
“If my sons didn’t want wars, there wouldn’t be,” said Gutele Schnaper, Mayer Amschel Rothschild’s wife on her deathbed in 1849.
Following the latest wave of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, as usual, many reactions arose.
Some take the side of Israel, arguing for its right to self-defense and putting the Palestinians in the role of terrorists, others support the Palestinians, invoking the racism of the State of Israel, the genocide committed against the Arabs of Palestine and considering Israel as a terrorist state.
I don't want to reflect on who's guilty and who's the victim, in his waves of recurring killings; after all, the current inhabitants of Israel, including those of the Autonomous Territories, were born in this political situation and have not experienced the beginning of the violence.
I would like to offer readers a point of view located "behind the curtain", on the one who benefits mainly from the tensions that have already persisted for more than 95 years (from the Balfour declaration in 1917) in a small piece of countries in the Middle East.
Some of these reflections are based on the historical facts available under consideration, others emerge from my own understanding of that, or more precisely of the group of individuals, which is the main instigator of the events of modern history.
The history of humanity is above all the history of a struggle for power.
In each age, we can find an Alexander the Great or a Napoleon.
What is not so obvious is whether these individualities acted independently or whether they owed their actions to the throne to someone who directed their actions according to previously defined goals.
We have to admit that we live in a time when global wealth is concentrated in the hands of a few individuals and that this financial concentration generating certain powers could not happen in a single generation.
Among families possessing an astronomical wealth is particularly distinguished the Rothschild family, which could be considered as the head of the octopus (I do not know if these families still have a leader but I do not exclude it).
Little is written about them.
Of course.
As early as the 19th century, they acquired the world's first news agency (Reuters) in order to put an end to the establishment of links between their names and serious criminal acts in which they were embossed and which had always meant for them a strengthening of their power, an increase in their fortune or both.
They have major holdings in almost most of the world's central banks and prepare or wage wars against countries in which they do not yet have such holdings (before the invasion of Afghanistan the number of these countries were 7, after the invasion of Iraq of 5, after the overthrow of Gaddafi only 4, meanwhile Russia placed the Russian central bank under the control of the Russian government).
Anyone who tried to stand against this family is dead.
Abraham Lincoln refused to renew to the Bank of America of the Rothschilds the status of a central bank and began to issue during the civil war his own money (government, I hear well) and was assassinated in a theater in 1865.
JFK began making clean money and wanted to dissolve the FED, he was assassinated in 1963, Congressman Louis McFadden was poisoned in 1936 when he wanted to file a lawsuit against the FED for triggering the great economic crisis of 1929.
Their thirst for power at the global level led in the years 1859 to 1871 the formulation of a plan of three world wars by Albert Pike, a Freemason initiated in the 33rd degree, being the highest level.
The objective of the first war was to eliminate Europe's great state monarchies, the second had to suppress colonialism, especially British, and the third should reduce the world's population between 1 and 0.5 billion individuals (such a quantity of slaves is sufficient for their luxury and comfort), creating a single universal religion (ecumenism being nothing but the beginning of such a solution) and, in the end
The method used by the clan of the wealthiest families headed by the Rothschilds is to create a crisis and then to propose a solution (order ab chaos - the order following the chaos).
These solutions are false and always lead to a worsening of the situation (see the creation of the FED intended to avoid the repetition of crises such as those they provoked in 1907).
