It must be understood: that one who has common sense counts the numbers of this ferocious beast.
This number denotes man and it is six hundred and sixty-six."
The movie Argo: When everything goes to the worst, call Hollywood.
In November 1979, a crowd of demonstrators made up of Islamist students stormed the U.S. embassy in Tehran and held 52 diplomats hostage.
They were to be released in exchange for the Shah, Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, who, after the revolution, had fled to the United States, who had supported his regime for several decades.
The situation did not offer the U.S. administration a positive outcome, it could not, indeed, abandon the Shah, because it would have seriously shaken the confidence of its allies in other countries.
However, in Iran, where the revolution led to the establishment of a theocratic regime, it was unable to secure the release of the hostages.
It was a blow for the prestige of the United States, a blow further aggravated later by the fiasco of the attempt to release hostages by force.
Sequestered diplomats were finally released 444 days later, following negotiations organized by the Algerian government.
Their fate sparked in the United States a wave of solidarity and anti-Iranian sentiment.
Iran’s debaccy contributed heavily to Jimmy Carter’s defeat against Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential elections.
Argo’s actor and director Ben Affleck describes an episode of this story that offered America a small victory.
Just before she was busy, six employees escaped from the embassy.
After complicated adventures, they found themselves in the residence of the Canadian ambassador.
In collaboration with the Canadian authorities, the CIA managed to get them out of Iran through an incredible coverage: they left the country with Canadian passports as members of a film crew who came to make scoutings for the shooting of a film with a major sci-fi show.
Combination of genres
Exfiltration expert Tony Mendez's plan requires collaboration from Hollywood.
In order to make the legend more believable, specialized magazines mentioned this film project, press conferences were organized and a fictitious production company had real offices.
The details of the operation remained secret for a long time, the film being based on the memories of Tony Mendez.
Affleck's film is an unusual combination of several genres.
It is on the one hand a political thriller designed in a realistic way but also a "caper movie", the story of a successful blow, of a treachery - as, for example, in the trilogy Ocean's eleven, twelve and thirteen.
The atmospheres follow one another: sequences of Tehran of documentary style (in the subtitled sequences are presented iconic photographs of the time of events and how, thanks to these photos, these same situations have been transcribed in the film, there are no great differences),
lighter passages of Hollywood, treated with irony and some exaggeration,
and then scenes from CIA headquarters and other institutions with costumed men discussing the situation around conference tables, in the corridors and on the phone...
Ben Affleck managed to restart his career remarkably.
Of ridiculed actor, he has become a respected director and his acting has stopped arousing ironic remarks.
Argo is his third feature film after the dark policeman "Gone Baby Gone" (2007) and the thriller "The town" (2010).
It’s also Affleck’s fish that doesn’t take place in his hometown, Boston.
The sense of atmosphere of a place is one of the characteristics that placed its first films above the Hollywood standards.
Affleck is also demonstrating this sense of atmosphere in Argo where Tehran's role "is held" by Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place in the streets, during the reconstruction of real facts; the initial sequence of the fall of the embassy is impressive by its clarity which also suggests confusion and unpredictability occurring when suddenly, in a given place, is written a page of history.
Affleck and his collaborators achieve a similar effect in fictional scenes (the fake filming crew in the Tehran bazaar).
Too many actions in too many places
The director had to be satisfied with a subject offering in fact few thrilling scenes, in the cinematic sense of the word.
It does very well, it sometimes adds certain elements to reality and there, the result is not always as elegant (the scene in which the threat of crisis at Tehran airport is dismissed by a phone call in America and the pursuit on the airstrip are quite far-fetched).
Argo's weakness lies in its dispelled character, due to the need to show too many events in too many different places.
If it is true that Alan Arkin and John Goodman are quite charming in their role as Hollywood co-operators, their characters would have deserved a bigger place and they are not, far from there, the only ones in this case.
Affleck's film lacks a bit of dramatic tension, it lets itself be seen with moderate interest, appreciating the realization and retro style of the 70's.
It trains the spectator, but with difficulty.
As a reminder of a story still, in a way, unfinished to date, and as an illustration of the exaggerated character of the representation of the secret services as omnipotent and omniscient, he does the trick.
Rules for inflating balloons, bananas and the circus
The www.bankovnipoplatky.com server, which conducts an annual public inquiry into the most absurd bank fees, has now decided to launch the competition of the regulations or ideas from the most absurd "EU workshop".
"The fact that the EU is planning, in recent times, to adopt a quota of 40 per cent of women for the management of major European companies has been at the origin of our decision," said Patrik Nacher.
Among the nonsense mentioned is already, for example, the recent decision of the European Court to unify insurance premiums for men and women.
Until now, however, women were advantaged in terms of life insurance tariffs because they represent, objectively, less risk for insurance companies.
People can report until the end of the year any other such incredible idea of the "EU workshop".
"The vote will take place before the end of February 2013," Nacher informed.
Controversial EU regulations include, for example, the obligation to add organic components to fuels, which has harmful consequences for the environment, or the prohibition on the use of reliable mercury thermometers, only because they contain a relatively small amount of a dangerous substance or the directive on the size of chicken cages, which has led to an increase in the price of eggs.
Critics have also been voiced in the past against the de facto ban of using the phrase "butter spread" or withdrawing the sale of conventional light bulbs.
Quality bananas must be 14 centimeters in size.
The European administration often reacts under pressure from a commercial or industrial lobby whose demands are usually defended in Brussels by a state or a group of states (for example the Czech Republic currently imposes the demands of its banks by threatening to use its right of veto).
The interests of the pressure groups are, for example, at the origin of the fact that in the Union, bananas must not be less than 14 cm in size or have "abnormal curvature".
The European Commission defended itself by arguing that it was merely harmonising non-homogeneous national standards that complicated trade.
The standards for fruits and vegetables have been relaxed despite opposition from some states, claiming that the existing directives lead to food waste.
The first prize can be awarded in this survey to last year's EU regulation, according to which a warning must appear on inflatable balloons indicating that children under the age of 8 should not inflate without adult supervision.
The EU has referred to a U.S. investigation that balloons are, among toys, the leading cause of child death by suffocation.
A similar restriction applies to children under the age of 14 when it comes to blowing in fair trumpets.
With us too, we've got weird ideas.
The following rule concerning European civil servants themselves is quite absurd: because of the sensitivity of this theme for Greece, anyone having an official function within the EU cannot use the name "Macedonia" and should, instead, speak of FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia).
In collaboration with the association "Laissez Faire" bringing together liberal economists, server Bankovnípoplatky.com has appointed in this survey, in addition to some nonsense mentioned above, the Union regulation concerning the volume of food reserves in the territory of the Czech Republic.
The EU had set maximum volumes for food reserves that could be found in the territory of the Czech Republic on the day we joined the EU.
The Czech Republic then exceeded, for example, the volumes fixed for canned mushrooms and therefore risked a hefty fine.
The organizers of this survey considered interesting the idea of paying money to certain countries because they do not have access to the sea and the idea of granting a grant to apply for subsidies.
These ideas, however, are not from Brussels but from Prague.
"The fact of not having a sea handicaps us.
We will ask the European Union for a repayment". Thus, in the autumn of 2004, the Minister of Agriculture at the time Jaroslav Palas (ČSSD) said.
He argued that, because of our wheat production, and as a result of the so-called "interventionist" purchases, the state had found itself with full stocks that it had then had to export.
According to Palas, the EU should have given us millions of euros, because the Czech Republic is far from the ports.
In the end, the European Commission responded positively to Czech demand and organised a call for tenders for the purchase of wheat from countries without maritime access.
Grants for obtaining grants have been proposed to municipalities by the Ministry of Local Development headed by Minister Pavel Němec (US-DEU), which should, in practice, be used to draft applications for grants granted by Brussels.
The EU states: "weird regulations are rare."
While regulations often give rise to criticism in the Member States, according to many experts, the EU's efforts to regulate and improve the effectiveness of the functioning of the Union and in the field of its general development are rather commendable.
For example, according to experts, is the problem of the use of EU subsidies for projects that have absolutely nothing to do with the strengthening of European integration, but which have been imposed by the Member States during budgetary negotiations.
The fact that the Czech Republic, like other countries of the Union, must fight in Brussels for the right to use specific denominations for these traditional products (fight from which it does not always come out victorious) arouses strong emotions among the Czechs.
If at the end of six years of battle against the Germans and Austrians, the Czechs successfully defended the name "Olomoucké tvarůžky", for the name "Tuzemský rum" which dates back to us in the 19th century, our producers had to replace it with the name "Tuzemák".
Only products based on cane sugar alcohol, and not based on beet sugar, may bear the name of rum.
In the official list of registered products of the EU, we find today in the company of world-renowned cheeses such as Feta or Gorgonzola, the massepain of Lübeck or Parma ham, the biscuits of Karlovy Vary ("Karlovarské suthary"), the carps of Pohořelice and Třeboň ("Pohořelický
Pardubice's gingerbread ("Pardubický perník") and cakes called "Hořice's Cornets" ("hořické trubičky") can also be proud of their community brand.
“People want me to save the Republic, but I’m just an amateur,” said Senator Okamura
Mr. Senator, how do we have the idea of running in the presidential elections?
It's not to be a senator or president that matters to me.
If everything worked smoothly in our country, I wouldn't run anywhere.
But I can’t stand to see how the country has been looted for the last twenty years, that thieves aren’t locked up and taxes and the age of retirement are rising.
I didn’t have an ambition to become a politician.
But when I see something I don't like, then I try to provide a solution for it to change.
And since I am more than forty years old and I am an independent member of any party, I have no other solutions to influence the situation than to stand in the presidential or senatorial elections.
You entered the Senate but quickly set out for the Castle (N.d.T.: seat of the presidency).
Don't you turn your back on those who elected you?
I've always said I'll get into the battle for the Castle according to the results of the senatorial elections
Subsequently, I made it clear that if I was elected as a senator, I would run in the presidential elections.
My goal is not the function, it is only an instrument allowing me to realize my vision.
That's why I need the greatest influence and the broadest mandate.
The problem is not only that as a nation we grumble at the bar or in front of our television set, but it is also that we trample on those who are trying to bring about change.
To this is added the press which breaks the reputations of people and confuses freedom of speech with freedom to lie.
For example, I said that I bribed the journalists or were Jiří Paroubek's adviser.
Let's come to your visions.
You have begun the struggle for the Castle with the themes of the material and criminal responsibility of politicians and retroactive tax declarations beyond twenty million [CZK].
To this end, you have to change the law.
As president, you do not have such power and only the Senate as a whole can propose laws.
How do you want to solve this problem?
When, as a citizen, I lobbied for tourist guide activities to be a liberal profession, I was successful.
The problem is politics: when someone arrives from the left or right with a good project, it is knowingly rejected and it delays the citizens.
As an independent member of no party, I am much more likely to gain the support of all parliamentary parties.
I have the advantage of being able, without partisan categorization and dogma, to take from every side the best for our country and implement it.
Do you think of yourself as a man of the right or the left?
From a Czech point of view, I feel like people put me on the left.
For me, whether it's a bit to the left or to the right, it doesn't really matter.
For me, the important thing is that we are moving forward.
What matters to me is not that people are from the left or the right but to bring people together.
I always support any solution contributing to the common good, even if it is proposed by the Communist Party or ODS and I act equally impartially against bad ideas.
Are you enraged when someone says that you’re a populist?
But doesn't what you've said prove it?
When you develop a business plan in a company, you also have an optimal purpose and vision.
You're trying to get closer.
Some may call it populism, but all the proposals I’m talking about have been implemented by the world and recognized experts have talked about it.
But without the support of the parliament, you only have slogans left.
You won’t survive long in politics with that.
Do you think that if you are going to see people and talk to them, you will succeed, for example, in imposing material and criminal responsibility?
I don't have any other options.
I have to convince politicians, journalists and public opinion and try to rally them to my camp in order to impose it.
If I were elected president, it would not be a problem to broadcast live on television my request to the presidents of parliamentary parties to legislate on the material and criminal responsibility of politicians, officials, judges and prosecutors.
Then they should eventually explain why they don't want it.
When there will be a strong personality who will identify the abuses, it will be possible to put pressure on the political scene.
Take, for example, the election of the president by direct suffrage, it was obtained thanks to pressure from public opinion.
I admit, unambiguously, being an amateur, not a genius or an intellectual.
I'm trying to find allies for my opinions and visions.
I just got into politics and I'm trying to get the support of the majority for my program.
I'm trying, alone, to move things forward, and if it doesn't work, I'll stop in six years and I'll return to the private sector.
It's like Okamura is supposed to save the Czech Republic.
I'm not a savior.
I know that only one person can't make the difference, that's why I went to see a number of personalities asking them to run in the senatorial elections too.
I met Radim Jančura, but he refused because of the amount of work.
At least I backed investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who exposed the oil fraud.
I introduced myself because people are really unhappy, but now I'm hesitating.
Sixty percent of people didn’t vote and what did, for the most part, gave their voice to an establishment representative.
There are only two independents in the Senate.
People have elected a Senate that can hardly impose changes.
In spite of everything, I will fight for my vision, for example the direct election of the mayors and the prefects of regions.
Are you planning to establish your own party?
No, not yet, because I don't have time to check if the past of each potential member is spotless and then I don't have the financial means.
I don't have money for my presidential campaign either, I only have twenty thousand on my "transparent" account.
You don't have money?
You're talking about tax returns, what's yours?
I estimate the value of my personal belongings at about 60 million.
I have in Prague a plot worth 25 million, an apartment worth ten million, another apartment worth 8 million, a collection of works of art worth about ten million, an Aston Martin worth 3.5 million and a Škoda Superb worth 1 million and a few million on my account.
By the way, I own an Aston Martin, because it was a kid's dream: I always loved James Bond, who was driving in the car, struggled with women and fought against evil and abuse.
You drive in Aston Martin, you have a heritage worth 60 million and you do not have money for your campaign?
Do you want to change the Republic but don’t want to invest your own money there?
That's not very convincing.
I don’t have 15 million for this campaign.
Maybe I should go into debt?
I've already invested 2 and a half million in the campaign.
The fact that I do not have any sponsors clearly shows that my program does not arouse much interest.
I don't have to pay this campaign out of my pocket.
My campaign expenses are generally covered by the remuneration I will receive as a senator.
But I couldn't make a living from it, for example paying for my son's English school which costs 30 thousand a month.
If my only goal was to make money, I won't run in any elections.
Are you going to continue your commercial activities to be able to live?
Didn't you say you'd mute your activities?
However, this is not related to the amount of this remuneration.
As I promised, I have partially reduced my activities.
For example, my deputy will take over the management of the travel agency in the spring.
People want me to be a Samaritan who saves the Republic.
However, I have to live on something.
What was your usual monthly salary when you were an entrepreneur?
I won and I continue to win between two hundred and four hundred thousand.
If I were president, I'd end my business activities.
You will find this interview in the Saturday edition of the newspaper Práva.
The architects of the firm MVRDV prove that the real adventures are not only a matter of imagination. To be convinced, it is enough to contemplate Spijkenisse and the newly erected mountain of books - 2 photos
"I find that this building is funny, it's very futuristic, finally something interesting to see," says Lisette Verhaig, a passer-by on the side of the street.
Stefan Spermon, a computer scientist at a large neighboring company, tells us: "Yes, it is indeed beautiful, this building."
I wonder why we still need a library today.
Everyone has the internet, an iPad and e-books.
Today, no one goes voluntarily in one of these nerdy libraries anymore, right?
Spijkenisse, a dormitory city that is not attractive at the gates of Rotterdam, holds a fairly special record.
The commune of 80,000 souls has the lowest education rate in the Netherlands.
In order to restore some neurons to the brains, the city decided, a few years ago, to contribute to the general culture and to reproduce the seven fictitious bridges that appear on the euro banknotes in the form of pretty small miniatures in painted reinforced concrete.
The success of the cultural offensive has remained limited.
This is how the authorities of the city realized that there was only one way to regain control of the statistics: the construction of a library!
Winy Maas, from the architectural office of Rotterdam MVRDV, master of boldary stick diagrams and creator of witty and often cynical buildings, accepted the project with his usual phlegm and presented himself to the competition in 2003 with five books under his arm and sneering on his face.
And while the members of the jury were still looking at each other, bewildered and shrugging their shoulders, Maas, the brazen, stacked his books of choice in order of size to make a pyramid, and ended his discourse supported by activist arguments by a "dear common!"
Here it is, my proposal for Spijkenisse's mountain of books, or as they say, the Boekenberg!
Nine years later, the mountain of 30 million euros springed from the ground.
The building is part of a revitalization project that also includes an underground parking lot, a supermarket, a post office and some terraced buildings and houses for a total of 50 dwellings.
At the beginning of November, the mountain of books received the second "Best Library of NL 2012" award.
In addition, the project was selected for the Dutch National Wood Award 2012.
This is how this small dull village, which until today had nothing more to offer than a postmodern pedestrian zone and a town hall with a mind-boggling ugliness, whose white facades invite us to think that they house a dairy, has been enriched with a contemporary architectural work.
The most important thing is that Spijkenisse now owns the first public cultural building in its history.
The Long Way to Reading
First impression: Eldorado of the book under cheese bell.
Certainly, an elevator transports you to the heart of the massif, but the real joys of reading and architecture open to you when you tread the floor of the building to discover its topography.
The interior made entirely of glass is bright and clear, the Dutch brick floor and elegant streetlights unequivocally speak the language of municipal public squares.
The urban atmosphere is perfect.
We are already looking at public benches, dogs and little boys and little girls playing ball.
And everywhere books, books and more books.
“Normally, the library shelves lie along the facade and in the middle is a large and dark room, which is often uncomfortable and impersonal,” Maas said.
We have completely disrupted the classical spatial configuration and opened the reading spaces from the inside to the outside.
The interior of the mountain of books has been skillfully landscaped: in the middle are the offices, the Internet library, the chess club, the center for the environment and the technical center of the building.
A particular beauty lies in the black shelves of the library, which are sometimes panelling, sometimes railings, sometimes stair railings.
A new experience for vision, touch and sense of smell.
Even hardened architects and building engineers don’t want unknown materials.
“We wanted to work with recycled materials,” says Joop Trouborst, project manager at the municipality of Spijkenisse, in response to the Standard.
This is how one day we found in a Frisian agricultural enterprise an ad hoc waste resulting from agriculture.
In the Netherlands, one millimeter thick synthetic membrane has been used for years as a substrate in greenhouses and fields.
It's cheap and we're buying time.
The thin membrane is used for one or two seasons, then it can be discarded.
For the library, the membrane has been compressed, for the first time in such quantities, into plates with a thickness of four centimeters.
Under the effect of heat and pressure, the "Landbouwplastic" (KPL) changes color and transforms into a dark, homogeneous and resistant material, the smell of which evokes both a new car and sports shoes.
At the top of the 105 steps is the top of the mountain.
After almost 500 meters, the literary café offers a breathtaking view of the city, but also Dutch croquettes and pot ficuses.
The latter create an atmosphere, but above all they contribute to the maintenance of the ideal level of air humidity of the literary mountain.
Gifts for the New Soul
"It's incredible, but despite all glass surfaces, this building is a model project in terms of ecology," says Trouborst.
The temperature of the building is regulated by geothermal.
Although the mountain of books lies below a glass bell, the sun illuminates only a short amount of time the interior, even on sunny days.
The thick wooden beams arched relative to the glass facade serve as sun visors, intercepting a large part of the shelves.
Ambient atmosphere is very pleasant.
Fully automated blinds take care of the rest.
Stefan Spermon, the aforementioned septic computer scientist, has already ventured into entering the new library.
Lisette Verhaig was already there too.
Cynthia Bogarde, professor of traditional Chinese medicine, even describes Boekenberg as Spijkenisse's "soul finally found".
A few weeks ago, during the inauguration, every citizen was invited to donate a book from his personal library.
This measure made it possible to fill the voids of the library which was not yet fully stocked (today it has 70,000 books).
Concept is a success.
The rays are overflowing on all sides.
For the architect Winy Maas, "there's nothing worse than a half-empty library."
"I believe that our invitation has created a certain relationship between every citizen and this new building.
Everyone knows that his book is part of this building.
Even if it's only for decoration."
This is how MVRDV has reached the master discipline that is called, in professional jargon, "the creation of an identity".
Spijkenisse has written a new page of literary history.
Even though she may seem young and poorly educated.
Finally, it is a new starting point for the birth of an identity.
Szabo: "Germans need to play a bigger role."
Germany chose abstention in the vote on the admission of the Palestinian state.
According to Stephen Szabo, an expert on relations between the United States and the EU, this decision puts Berlin in a delicate political situation.
Deutsche Welle: Earlier this week, Germany had announced its intention to vote against the request of the Palestinians to obtain observer state status with the United Nations.
She then chose abstention.
Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn’t approve of what the Israelis did in Gaza.
However, Berlin must exercise caution, as it maintains special relations with Israel.
But I also think she doesn't support the American position either.
Germany wanted to assert its independence, without, however, being too critical of Israel.
During the insurgency in Libya in March 2011, Germany had also chosen abstention during the vote on the establishment of a no-fly zone.
Finally, NATO imposed this measure.
Is Germany finding it difficult to take a clear position on major international issues?
The answer is yes.
The reason for this is that Berlin is reviewing its foreign policy, seeking to move away from an American-defined policy and to establish a German foreign policy.
In addition, the lack of a coherent and common policy within the Union complicates the situation.
As a result, the Germans face several fronts.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they are not used to it.
I believe that they are still groping to find this role, but are on the path to "more normal" foreign policy.
A foreign policy, such as in France or in Great Britain
So what does the term "normal" foreign policy specifically mean for the Germans?
It shows the desire to adopt positions independent of those of the United States and European partners, including with regard to national issues.
I believe that German foreign policy is also determined by its economic policy, therefore by exports and its relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
German economic interests are, to some extent, different from those of the other great powers: that is why Germany must protect its interests.
Were these economic interests influencing Germany's position on the conflict in the Middle East and abstention in the United Nations vote?
First, Germany has important opportunities in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf countries.
This is why Berlin must be careful not to clash with public opinion, but also the elites of Arab countries.
This is undoubtedly a determining factor.
However, I wouldn't give too much importance to it, because it's not an exclusively unilateral relationship.
In any case, this is a decisive factor in German strategic reflections.
Is Germany hurting its relations with the United States by choosing abstention in important votes, as in the case of Palestine?
I think that the Europeans and Americans fully understand the German position.
That is why I think that the divide is not so serious that it was in the case of the Libyan question.
Even that Germany will only be more respected.
Indeed, Germany has thus demonstrated that it must be taken seriously as an international actor and that its interests must be taken into account.
In Europe, there are different views on the Palestinian initiative.
On the other hand, the United States has announced its veto with a clear voice.
Do opinions differ between the United States and the majority of European countries?
There have always been differences because of America’s domestic political scene.
“I believe that the Obama administration secretly shows a great understanding of the European position.
However, in view of the political configuration of the country, the government cannot of course defend its position publicly.
I believe that the real differences of opinion are not so great that they always seem.
If you observe relations between Obama and Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, you’ll notice that Obama is far from overflowing with excitement about Netanyahu’s policy.
Is Germany struggling to reconcile its close relations with Israel and the United States, on the one hand, on the one hand, with the position of the major European partners, on the other?
I don't think it makes things easier for the Germans.
Of course, it would be a little simpler for the Germans if there was a coherent and uniform European policy, but today, this is not the case.
They therefore cannot register within a broader political line and must affirm their singular position.
This is what they are currently doing on the issue of the euro.
In my opinion, in the future, Germany will assume a locomotive role and push Europe to adopt a European position.
It is not easy for Germany, especially because of relations with Israel.
This issue has always been tricky.
But in my view, the Germans are well aware of the fact that they must play a role that further marks their independence.
Is Germany playing a strong role on the international stage, does Germany only want to take on a key role?
Or does it still have difficulty assuming leading roles?
Germany is still not used to it, it is always uncomfortable and, for obvious reasons, the country is still struggling to play a more important role.
If we look at, for example, the crisis of the euro, we notice that every time Germany decides to weigh more, many anti-German sentiments are heard.
It doesn't make things easier for the Germans.
In fact, it's always the same problem: you don't want to be encircled by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more delicate position than the United States.
She has to deal with very varied neighbors and opinions, and it's not easy.
Today, the influence of the United States on European policy is only diminishing, but at the moment the EU is not filling this void, so who remains?
The Germans will simply have to play a bigger role.
And even if they don't like it, even if it's unpleasant to them and they're less appreciated, it's life!
Stephen Szabo is the Deputy Director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, D.C., an institute in which academics and policy experts from Europe and North America jointly analyze the challenges of the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specializes in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
"Protection of brands" in China: when Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese
Armani and Polo Ralph Lauren are well-known brands around the world.
Armani Polo... what's it really about?
Behind this name hides a trademark officially registered in China, but which has nothing to do with the original company.
The trade mark thus enjoys such protection as the company bearing its name cannot file a complaint.
And even if she could, it's not certain she'd win.
“It’s becoming increasingly difficult for foreigners to protect their trademarks in China,” said Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer at Taylor Wessing who specializes in copyright infringement in the Far East.
Every week, a new file arrives on my desk.
Imitators only need a few additional letters to register their trademarks.
Thus, instead of Gucci, we record Lu-Gucci, and instead of Prada, we record Prada-Kny.
German companies are also legaly copied, such as Puma, the manufacturer of sporting goods.
Pattloch opens a workbook in which the registrations made with the Beijing Trademark Office are located.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company registered the trademark Zegna DF Puma, a denomination that the firm artificially created by appropriating part of the name Ermenegildo Zegna, the fashion house.
It is a well-known fact that the Chinese have become world champions of copying and violating intellectual property.
In large cities, there are multi-storey shopping malls that sell virtually only counterfeits.
Pattloch's cases, however, are different: his constituents instruct him to address the fact that the Chinese Trademark Office officially confers on Chinese companies the right to use a denomination that is already protected in another country.
The Chinese talk about "Bang Mingpai", a parent brand.
The term is derived from "Bang Dakuan".
It is with this term that women who marry rich men are referred to.
The Chinese authorities say they don't have anything to blame.
"It's bad for business, we have to fight against this trend," says Pattloch.
"The brand is watered down, the singularity disappears, the damage to the reputation is enormous."
Financial losses and legal costs arise in millions for the sectors concerned, especially with regard to costy flagship products.
According to data from the market analysis company CLSA, China represents, with an annual volume of 15 billion euros, the world's third-largest market for luxury goods and the fastest-growing.
However, it is not easy to write down questionable records from the trademark register and these procedures are expensive.
The latter can last up to nine years, and the outcome is never certain.
Pattloch refers to the trials in which the court dismissed the complaint, because after a long period of time, the criticised denomination had become a "market reality".
Pattloch explains that if the Complainant is not lucky, he may even be forced to pay the plagiarium, because he would have harmed his brand in China.
In this area, it is sometimes the law of the jungle that dominates.
Famous cases also involve graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler lost a legal battle against construction machine builder Sany, the same company that had just bought concrete pump producer Putzmeister.
This is why the Chinese can use an emblem that looks like the star Mercedes.
Geely, Volvo's acquisitor, used before a blue and white logo reminiscent that of BMW. The dispute was settled and Geely had to modify it.
Lacoste’s fashion house had lost against the plagiarists of Hong Kong and Singapore who had reoriented the look of the famous crocodile in the opposite direction.
According to the Chinese authorities, they are not guilty of anything.
However, the CTMO trademark office in Beijing admits to having experienced some difficulties in 2010 because of a lack of staff and space.
However, the office would have passed last year from an "emergency situation in the course of work to a restored normality".
Thus, the number of unprocessed opposition proceedings would have fallen by 22%.
Nearly 57,000 cases of this type would have been closed, which represents an increase of 75% compared to the previous year.
However, 81,500 proceedings remain outstanding at the Office.
Acting is expensive
As is often the case in China, the numbers are impressive.
Last year, the OCT received more than 1.4 million trademark applications, almost a third more than in 2010.
According to the office, this is a record and this means that China is at the top tenth time in a row for the number of new deposits in the world.
This would also apply to the total number of legal brands, which is 5.5 million.
In 2011, the office reportedly recorded 1.8 billion yuan of registration fee.
In other words, each recording reported on average 1.280 yuan, or 160 euros.
A legal battle costs much more, as illustrated by the case of the German family business Freudenberg.
For more than seven years, the company has been fighting against a Chinese plagiarist.
Admittedly, the Germans were able to obtain that the illegal manufacture of vehicle components was suspended.
However, the imitator was able to maintain his Chinese rights to the Freudenberg brand.
“It’s our fault, because surnames can’t be protected in Germany,” said Hanno Wentzler, chairman of the management of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The trademark office had therefore rejected the objection of the Munichois.
Freudenberg has certainly won with the following two instances, but the opposing side continues to fight until today.
Vigilance is required
Today, the case is pending before the Supreme Court.
Wentzler is confident, he believes in a positive outcome of the trial and praises the professionalism of the judges.
Nevertheless, he adds: "the procedure is long, challenging and costs us a lot of time and money."
It is also difficult to assess the internal costs, and the archives department has even had to review the deeds of the past hundred years in order to provide evidence.
Five years ago, Freudenberg had even proposed in vain to the opposing party a "big sum in six-digit euros" to settle the dispute.
"That's what we see what it means to us," Wentzler said.
The dangers of the Far East even threaten to overflow to Europe.
This would be the case if imitators from this region were to operate unprotected marks.
For example, a Chinese producer wanted to lay the name Freudenberg in Germany for a footwear and leather production company.
The company had long since abandoned this sector, but it was still able to prevent the deposit.
Wentzler adds: "Vigilance is required."
Pattloch and him advise German companies to exercise great caution in their trade relations with China.
It is not enough, according to them, to rely on international trademark law, foreigners should instead "register also in China everything that is likely to be protected", as Wentzler says.
Otherwise, the costs may be much higher than those of registration.
Indeed, if Freudenberg lost the battle during the last act of his legal drama, the company would have to pay license fees to the opposing party for the use of his own name, as Wentzler explains.
Or else, we would lose our place in the market segment in question.
World AIDS Day: Tapping Your Feet, Singing, Helping
Imbongi choir repeat in Heidelberg, and Swaziland’s AIDS orphans rejoice at their arrival.
Here is the story of a link that crosses much more than a distance of 8,733 kilometers.
First of all, we type: cowboy boots, sneakers, pumps for ladies and moccasins for men seek the rhythm by hitting the floor of the foot. Very quickly, they find it.
One, two, three, four.
Then, the vocals of the singers slowly amplify - viola, bass, tenor and soprano -, they get carried away, bewitch and follow each other.
Fiete Hopf, the 29-year-old conductor almost loses his canvas shoes when he conducts the orchestra with his gestures of wild flexibility.
It's Monday night, Imbongi choir repeats a new song in the music room of the Heidelberg Institute of Medical Psychology.
Fifty singers from 23 to 69 years old came, from the geneticist in human medicine to the man at home.
"Om'Obani" is not an easy piece, every chorister has different lyrics, all in a very exotic language: Zulu, a language spoken by 11 million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique and parts of Swaziland.
Being helped to help each other
UNAIDS, the United Nations AIDS programme, estimates that 34 million people around the world have the disease.
23.5 million of them live in southern Africa.
Swaziland has 245,000 people infected.
Moreover, more than 40% of the population is HIV-positive.
180 AIDS orphans from the village of Esitjeni have found godparents in Germany thanks to the "Voices for Africa" association.
70 of them attend a secondary school.
For 15 or 20 euros per less, you can become a sponsor.
This sum guarantees the child money for the school, a school uniform and a daily hot meal at the gogo center.
Imbongi means in Zulu "storyteller" or "singer of praise".
No one can fluently speak the Bantu language in this region, but everyone can sing it.
For nearly 10 years, the choir has been repeating songs in this foreign language that she brought to Germany to now return it to her home region: the South of Africa.
8.733 kilometers as the crow flies from Heidelberg, in the north-west of the Kingdom of Swaziland, is Esitjeni, the village which depends on the vocal power of the German choir.
40% of people affected
The village has about 2,000 inhabitants, some live in modest huts of straw or land and the majority of them are children.
More than 300 of them are orphans because HIV has taken away their parents.
In Esitjeni, on a smaller scale, we can see the evils that plague Swaziland: the highest rate of HIV infection and the lowest life expectancy in the world according to UNICEF.
The population almost never accepts male circumcision, a measure that guarantees a proven reduction in the risk of infection by half.
More than 40% of Swaziland's population carries the immunodeficiency virus and it is not uncommon to die in mid-thirties.
During a group trip to Africa in the spring of 2005, the choir was able to discover the village. The Imbongis mainly saw many children in the street who lacked not only parental care, but also simply everything: food, clothing, education.
In a poor country, without a school diploma, there is simply no prospect.
At the beginning, it was only a few people who personally committed to pay a few euros every year in order to be able to send a child to school and offer him a hot meal a day.
But just a year later, the choir founded "Voices for Africa", an association that has been caring for Esitjeni AIDS orphanes since then.
Facts about sexually transmitted infections
What are the Most Common Sexually Transmitted Diseases?
Bacterial STIs include syphilis, chlamydia and blenorrhagia (or gonorrhea).
Common STIs are HIV, human papillomavirus, genital herpes or hepatitis.
Pubic lice and scabies belong to parasitic STIs.
Which groups are mainly concerned?
Syphilis and blennorrhage often appear in men who have sex with other men.
The Robert-Koch Institute believes that sexual contact between men is responsible for at least four out of five cases of syphilis reported in Germany.
Heterosexual adults, chlamydia-related infections, trichomoniasis, candidiasis (fungal), blenorrhage, and human papillomavirus are common sexually transmitted diseases.
The rate of HIV spread among heterosexual adults is relatively low in Germany. However, some 20% of new HIV infections are observed within this group.
In young people, there is a significantly higher rate of chlamydia-related infections than in other population groups.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections affect young people aged 15 to 25.
Moreover, in Germany, the human papillomavirus is common in young people.
What is the evolution of the number of infections?
It is not mandatory to report all sexually transmitted diseases.
According to the Robert-Koch Institute, the number of cases of syphilis has more than doubled in 10 years: the figure rose from 1,967 in 2001 to 3.698 in 2011.
Since 2007, the number of new HIV infections has declined.
In 2011, there were approximately 2,700 cases.
This represents a decrease of one tenth compared to the previous year.
What Are the Symptoms of a Sexual Illness?
Infectious diseases can cause abscesses in the genitals, urinary pain, white discharge, low-belly pain as well as the appearance of pimples and warts.
However, they do not regularly cause any pain or other symptoms and therefore remain hidden.
How can you protect yourself?
Condoms may decrease the risk of infection, but they do not protect 100%.
The reason for this is that the pathogens of sexually transmitted diseases can also be transmitted through lubricating infection or close body contact.
Experts therefore recommend that anyone often changing sexual partners take regular examinations.
Most STIs quickly detected can be healed and late sequelae can be avoided.
Thanks to sponsorships, donations, but above all to the funds raised by choristers thanks to their voices throughout Germany, the means accumulate.
"In all, we have already sent about 200,000 euros to Esitjeni," said Annette Lennartz, president of the association.
In the village, Zodwa Dlamini, a self-confidencing woman who always gets what she wants, manages money from Germany.
It also ensures that orphans are well housed, with one of their grandmothers for example.
Gogos, this is the so-called old women in Zulu, are the pillars of the village.
Some of them welcome up to 14 orphans, they offer them a roof and ensure that they go to school every day at school in their uniforms.
The orphan who no longer has anyone is welcomed in a safe place at Khanyisile, a woman who lives alone and is paid for by the association, as are the two cooks who prepare meals every day for more than 200 hungry children.
In addition, "Voices for Africa" created a sewing school and allowed the construction of two chicken coops. With the assistance of the American organization for health PSI, the association organized an HIV test for many residents.
This does not go without saying, because even if the disease is clearly visible throughout the country, it remains a taboo subject.
A King and his 14 Wives
"AIDS is a real taboo," says Annette Lennartz, "because it's a subject that affects sexuality."
It's quite curious, coming from a country whose king has 14 official wives.
The last absolute monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his excessive lifestyle.
Polygamy instead of democracy.
These state-sanctioned mores, among others, are responsible for the rapid spread of HIV observed in recent decades.
Migrant workers have also spread the virus across the country.
There are however condoms on every street corner, adds Annette Lennartz, "but almost no one uses them.
Culture is different, flesh against flesh.”
In order to promote cultural exchange somewhat, Imbongi's choristers travel together every two or three years in southern Africa and sing songs filled with nostalgia, courage, trust and black identity that many inhabitants of the tip of the black continent are still familiar with apartheid.
The bus full of whites who sing in African language is a sign of recognition that it not only generates good humor and joy, but sometimes also pulls a tear from some dready border guards.
The bus always takes them to Esitjeni, where choristers visit their godchildren.
Although it is difficult to find the small village on a map, Esitjeni is well-known in the Ezulweni Valley.
"Go to Esitjeni, you'll find light there," said the people there.
And after a journey of 8,733 kilometers as the crow flies, back to Heidelberg, in front of these choristers who tap their feet in the rehearsal room of the Bergheimer Straße, we also find the light.
Messager: NASA discovers the presence of ice on Mercury
The Messenger spacecraft reported evidence of the existence of ice on the planet Mercury.
The layer of ice could reach 20 meters thick.
The U.S. Space Agency NASA has proven the existence of ice on the planet Mercury.
Although this planet is closest to the Sun, it contains frozen water, as explained by three studies published this Thursday in the specialized journal "Science".
The Messenger spacecraft has reported evidence of an ice cover in the area of the planet permanently plunged into the shadows.
This coverage would have a thickness of at least 30 centimeters of up to 20 meters.
The water probably comes from comets or perhaps asteroids that would have hit Mercury.
However, no one makes a connection between the discovery of water and the hypothesis of a presence of life on the planet, explains the chief scientist of the Messenger probe, Sean Solomon.
The temperature on Mercury can reach 426 degrees Celsius.
The discovery could help to understand how water and other elements essential to life arrived in other parts of the solar system.
Unnoticed by the inhabitants of our planet, probes, telescopes and small robots like Phoenix explore the depths of the universe.
From time to time, they send images to the earth, such as small interstices through which we observe infinity.
The image comes from a camera developed by German researchers from the Max-Planck Institute.
The eight planets of our solar system, as well as the dwarf planet Ceres.
Like Pluto, which orbits the Sun behind Neptune, Ceres is not considered a "planet", according to the new definition of the 2006 International Astronomical Union.
This detail of an infrared photograph taken by the Spitzer Telescope shows a "family portrait" of the countless generations of stars: the oldest stars are in blue and the pink dots, more difficult to identify, are the "newborns" in the delivery room of the universe.
The Spitzer Telescope has discovered this region where stars, dubbed "W5", a very unromantic name, in the constellation of Cassiopeia are born, 6,500 light-years away.
NASA’s Spitzer Telescope photographed the flamboyant brightness of a dying star.
The doughnut-shaped circle is composed of elements that the star projects when it dies.
In Earth's immense Trifide Nebula, 5,400 light-years from Earth, gas and dust form new stars.
The Spitzer Telescope photographed this galactic delivery room.
Pleiades, a cluster of stars also called "the seven sisters", can be seen with the naked eye during the night.
However, however, the telescope enhances the colors.
In this infrared photo, Helice's Nebula, like a red eye, looks directly at the viewer.
It is 700 light years away in the constellation Aquarius.
His resemblances to an earthly continent earned him the title of North America.
The combination of normal photography and infrared photography makes it possible to observe a spectacular hue.
Only the infrared detectors of the Spitzer telescope were able to capture an image of the new star in all its beauty.
One of the greatest enigmas of astronomy is the creation of Saturn's rings.
One theory is that rings are the remnants of a Saturn moon that disappeared 4.5 billion years ago without a trace.
One of the largest and sharpest photographs the Hubble Telescope has taken is that of the Tourbillon galaxy.
Depending on the hue, photographs of spiral galaxies can become real works of art.
The photograph published by the European Southern Observatory shows the Trifide nebula in the constellation of Sagittarius, several thousand light-years away.
Trifide’s name comes from the Latin word “trifidus” (divided, divided into three), because three dust trails divide the glowing heart from the birthplace of the stars.
Astronomers have photographed the beginnings of a cosmic carambolage in the Serpentary constellation: 400 million light-years from Earth, the nucleies of two molten galaxies rush towards one another, the collision is inevitable.
The Hubble Telescope observed the birth of these stars in the spiral galaxy M83.
If you don't like technical abbreviations, you can use its nickname "Southern Firewheel".
The photo of the Hubble Space Telescope shows a detail of the Iris Nebula, in the constellation Cepheus.
The nebula is 1,400 light-years away and is composed of dust grains that are ten to a hundred times smaller than common household dust.
This photo has been developed from the optical and X-ray shots taken by various telescopes.
We can see the circle composed of black holes which is 430 million light-years from Earth.
The Space Telescope photographed for NASA this group of galaxies called Arp 273.
Scientists have named the largest spiral galaxy "GC 1810".
In this nebula is the brightest group of young stars on our Milky Way.
The celestial cradle always gives birth to new stars.
These clouds of stars, which are connected by the Rosetta Nebula, also permanently create young stars 5,000 light-years from Earth.
In this galaxy with light rays and pierced with a small black hole, there is no dust yet, there is only gas.
Scientists believe it was born soon after the bigbang, when the universe was composed largely of hydrogen.
Our perspective on the universe: the main telescopes
The telescope was invented in 1608 by Hans Lipperhey, before Galilee used it a year later to observe the stars.
Since then, the mirrors of optical telescopes have not ceased to grow and thanks to them, it has been possible to see always further in the universe.
For 30 years, between 1947 and 1975, the Hale Telescope at Mount Palomar Observatory near San Diego was the world’s largest telescope.
The mirror on this image had a diameter of five meters.
In Arizona, in the United States, is the Large Binocular Telescope.
It makes it possible to observe the universe through two mirrors each having a diameter of 8.4 meters.
The belly of Gran Telescopio Canarias in La Palma, in the Canary Islands, is huge. The mirror alone reaches a diameter of 10.4 meters.
The Southern African Large Telescope mirror in South Africa has been segmented in order to save money.
The diameter still reaches about eleven meters.
The disadvantage of the method of economic construction is that the telescope is fixed at the level of the vertical angle, which limits its mobility.
The Hobby-Eberly Telescope in Texas is also fixed at the vertical angle.
Its particularity lies in its great capacity for collecting light.
Despite a relatively small mirror diameter, this capability is similar to those of the world’s largest mirror telescopes.
Scientists are listening to the universe in search of extraterrestrial signals using Arecibo’s (Puerto Rico) radio telescope.
This radio telescope has a diameter of 305 meters.
Anyone who owns a computer can help the "Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence" (SETI) by harnessing the computing power of their machine.
View on the European Southern Observatory (ESO) in the Chilean Andes.
Here is the Very Large Telescope, an installation worthy of its name.
Thanks to a total of four mirrors, this telescope can illuminate the average infrared spectrum.
The construction of a very large European Extremy Large Telescope is also planned for the ESO site in Chile.
Its main mirror is expected to reach the impressive size of 42 meters. It will consist of nearly 1,000 elements of mirrors.
However, the telescope will not be able to take pictures until 2018 at the earliest.
Until 2007, the two Keck telescopes on the Hawaiian volcano of Mauna Kea were considered the largest in the world.
They had two mirrors with a diameter of 10 meters each.
Keck telescopes are part of the Mauna Kea observatory that observes the sky with the Subaru Telescope and IRTF.
A giant new telescope should also be built on the Mauna Kea and the diameter of its mirror will reach 30 meters.
This illustration allows you to admire it.
However, images of the most important space come from the Hubble Space Telescope.
Since April 24, 1990, he gives us images of distant worlds.
Since March 2009, the Kepler Space Telescope has been looking for exoplanets, mainly those that are habitable.
NASA announced on February 2, 2011 that it had found 1,235 candidate planets since the mission began.
This photo illustrates the final preparations before the launch of the Kepler Space Telescope.
The James-Webb Space Telescope (JWST) will be sent into space as soon as possible in 2018 aboard an Ariane 5 rocket.
The first mirror of the Infrared Space Telescope has a diameter of 6.5 meters.
In particular, the telescope's mission will be to find light projected by the first stars and galaxies after the bigbang.
Scientists believe that Mercury’s South Pole is also home to ice.
However, no reliable data on this theory exists, as Messenger orbits much closer to Mercury's North Pole.
Radar data had been indicating the presence of ice on Mercury for decades.
Thanks to the launch of the Messenger probe in 2004, the first to orbit Mercury, scientists are now confident.
Drink butter on a daily basis and live up to 168
In southern Azerbaijan, many people reach ages worthy of the Patriarchs of the Bible.
There’s even a museum of longevity.
A survey of a country, in which we are still relatively young at 97.
In southern Azerbaijan, many people reach ages worthy of the Patriarchs of the Bible.
There’s even a museum of longevity.
A survey of a country, in which we are still relatively young at 97.
The trip to the Talysh Mountains is a dizzled romanticism.
At the bend of the many turns of the road, the small bus bumps along wooded hills, torrential rivers and modest farms.
Everything is green and fertile, one would almost believe oneself in the Black Forest.
But we are here in the deep south of Azerbaijan, the border with Iran is only a few kilometers away.
It is here that the Caucasian ethnic group named Talysh lives, about which we do not know much, except that they speak Perfectly Persian and Azeri and that they have a very great longevity.
Terminus: Lerik.
The small town plasters with its overly aggressive architecture of the Soviet era which does not agree at all with the picturesque mountain landscape.
European tourists are rare here, the journey from the capital of Azerbaijan, Baku, is far too painful.
It takes eight hours to travel the 323 kilometers, because the road has only one lane.
The province has not yet taken advantage of the legendary wealth that the country owes to its oil reserves in the Caspian Sea.
However, Pilata Fatulayeva, 48, is convinced that Lerik has the potential to become a tourist place.
“Bakou became famous in May thanks to the Eurovision contest, and next year there will be here the festival of the world’s oldest people,” Fatulayeva said.
She is director of the Museum of Longevity, the only museum of its kind in the world.
We can discover the life of about eight tens of Talysh in the region who are more than 100 years old. Fatulayeva shows us a photo in black and white.
Here's my grandfather, he's turned 120.
Have another child at 136 years old
But the controversial star of the museum is Şirali Müslümov, a shepherd who would have turned 168.
However, there is no birth certificate.
And when we think that the oldest documented person was 122, Müslümov's age seems more than questionable.
"He was born in 1805 in this region, he died in 1973," Fatulayeva said.
The man allegedly married three times and had 23 children. At the age of 136, he again designed a girl.
Would Şirali Müslümov have been wrong by a few decades by calculating his age?
Rembrandt Scholz, a gerontologist at the Max-Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of the man at the age of Central Asia.
“Surprisingly elderly people also live in some parts of China, Japan or the Hunza Valley in Pakistan,” Scholz says, “in Sardinia too, there are men living incredibly old.”
However, it is impossible to establish scientific evidence because of the non-existence of the documents, not to mention that there was no civil status register.
Every day a glass of melted butter
It is nevertheless in fact that the people of the region of Lerik often reach an age worthy of the Patriarchs of the Bible.
Currently 20 people are over 100 years old.
So what is the reason for the longevity in the South of this country?
Farid Mugimzadeh, the Azeri tourist guide, explains that the Talysh have particular genes.
However, Fatulayeva, the director of the museum, thinks it's about food.
However, it does not seem plausible, from a nutritional point of view, that Talysh’s high-calorie meals, which love meat, bread and especially dairy products, many of which drink a glass of melted butter daily, are healthy.
Or is it the simple way of life that preserves the youth of people? In Cengemiran, a tiny village near the city of Lerik, lives Rubaba Mirzayeva.
At the age of 97, she is still relatively young for the region.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 descendants, lives in a modest wooden house, as it is typical to see throughout the Caucasus region.
She sits on the floor next to a chuck, whose contents she tirelessly beats.
Eight people live here under one roof, including one of Mirzayeva’s sons and a daughter, who have long been adults.
Two little children also roam in the house.
In the kitchen, tea is prepared for the guests served in typical belly-shaped armadus glasses.
Mirzayeva's white teeth are perfectly aligned, and under his scarf hide long braids of a dark blonde that his son proudly reveals.
I've always washed my hair with milk, and that's how I never lost it and kept their color.
“I’ve never used shampoo,” Mirzayeva adds.
Monthly Retreat is Enough to
She has always eaten what comes from her own farm: tomatoes, potatoes and peas.
In my whole life, I've never bought any food from a supermarket.
Then she tells us about her husband who was in the army.
The worst moments were after World War II.
But everything improved when the "beloved father" Heydar Aliyev took command.
The language of propaganda astonishes in the mouth of an old lady.
However, however, the worship of the nation's paternal figure, a man who ruled his country as a dictator, knows almost no limits in Azerbaijan
He was in power until 2003, his son Ilham later succeeded him.
In any case, the elderly in Azerbaijan do not know distress.
Mirzayeva receives monthly 230 manats (approximately the same amount as in euros) for his retirement, an amount that guarantees a good lifestyle in the region.
And perhaps Mirzayeva's long-blown son is right: "elders enjoy great respect in our culture."
They are the center of the big family, they are loved, cared for and happy.
That’s a good reason to live as long as possible.
“Human rights” omitted from the Constitution
Revolution is reborn in Cairo.
Rival demonstrations unveil the country’s deep fracture.
The Sharia-Based Draft Constitution is violently controversial.
The Egyptian president is not stingy with words full of emotions.
We need to successfully transition.
“And this success is my responsibility to the people and to God,” he told state television.
His discourse was intended for the entire people, but also above all for Christian Copts, liberals, enlightened and secular Muslims.
Because all members of the confused and desperately disunited opposition are afraid.
They are afraid of a theocracy on the Nile at the hands of the mighty Muslim Brotherhood.
Mohamed Morsi said, almost apologizing, having temporarily restricted the jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court and expanded his own "in order to save the revolution".
However, the Egyptians and the world don’t really know what this 61-year-old engineer, who holds a PhD from the American University of Southern California.
Will the judiciary be overturned?
The 234 articles hastily drafted by the predominantly Islamic constituent assembly of 100 people during a fifteen-hour night tour de force are indeed worrying to some extent.
As in previous constitutions, the project provides that the jurisprudence is based on "the principles of Islamic law".
But what does the word "principles" really mean?
It was and always remains a matter of interpretation, and there are good reasons to fear that Islamists will use the vague wording and the resulting legal leeway to apply a stricter interpretation of Sharia.
In any case, it is to suggest a newly introduced article stating that, with regard to all subjects relating to Sharia, al-Azhar University must be consulted. The institution is the most important Islamic institution in the country and its radiance radiates on the entire Sunni Islamic world.
This can, but does not necessarily mean that the clergy will monitor the legislative apparatus, which would de facto lead to the guardianship of the judiciary.
Many points of the draft Constitution are subject to interpretation
Another problematic aspect is the likely maintenance of military jurisdiction.
During Mubarak's reign, these tribunals worked to quell opponents.
After the fall of the dictator, up to 11,000 civilians were detained by the military.
The state must also, according to the project, protect "the true nature of the Egyptian family" and "promote its morality and values".
From a legal point of view, the formulation is so vague that state institutions could even control the content of cinematographic art and literature.
Clearly, this would simply be censorship.
In addition, there is no article that expressly guarantees equality between men and women.
Instead, another article forbids offending or defaming the Prophet Muhammad or his envoys.
However, the article does not define what an offense is or how it should be punished.
Just as doubtful is the formulation suggesting that it is forbidden to "offend people".
Is it enough to draw a caricature of the president or tell a joke at the expense of a lawyer?
Many points are therefore subject to interpretation in the draft that Morsi submits to the vote and which, in his own words, will "soonly" be the subject of a referendum with the Egyptians.
"Revolution Is Reviving"
The opposition has been mobilizing for weeks against the digital superiority of Islamists.
On Friday night, tens of thousands of protesters gathered in an unusual agreement on Tahrir Square in Cairo, promising to bring down Shariah before it came into force.
“The revolution is reborn and we are going to win,” said Hamdin Sabbahi, who came third in the presidential elections.
Nobel Peace Prize and former head of the International Atomic Energy Agency Mohamed El Baradei said that the draft Constitution is "to be thrown into the trash cans of history."
On Twitter, the instant messaging service, he accused Morsi supporters of wanting to foment a "putsch against democracy".
"If he proclaims the referendum, we'll go to his palace and we're gonna overthrow him," said Jasser Said, an opponent.
“We’ren’t tired yet, the blood of our brothers isn’t atony yet,” Khaled Ali, a politician and member of the opposition, said.
In addition, several judges said they did not want to monitor the referendum, which would result in its nullity.
"The Qur'an is Our Constitution"
For its part, the well-organised Muslim brotherhood called for a counter-protestation not on Tahrir Square as a precautionary measure, but on the other side of the Nile, where a great prayer was organized in front of Cairo University.
The veiled women and supporters of the Salafists, who participated in large numbers, chanted: "the people demand the application of the law of God."
"Cleans our country!" they asked Morsi, asserting that "the Qur'an [was their] Constitution."
A fight for sovereignty over the symbolic Tharir Square, where it all began, would soon have spawned a situation similar to that of a civil war.
Clearly, that's what Morsi's supporters wanted to avoid.
The Muslim Brotherhood said opponents of the draft Constitution, along with its supporters, had spoken loud and clear.
According to them, it is now time to let the people decide by the ballot boxes of the direction the country must take.
The Islamist project will certainly win the majority.
"Human rights are not mentioned once"
Hafez Abu Saeda is angry at this imposed constitutional process that should have extended until February and encompassed all social forces.
The 48-year-old human rights lawyer and president of the Egyptian Human Rights Organization (EOHR) defended the Muslim Brotherhood when they were in prison or in court under Mubarak.
He did so not because he shared their conception of the world, but because for him human rights are indivisible.
That's why he's been beaten, tried and imprisoned.
"And now, the term is not even pronounced once in the new Constitution," he regrets during his interview with the Welt am Sonntag.
Morsi's extension of powers to the three powers discouraged the lawyer.
These measures are glaring attacks on the rules of democracy and will generate a new dictatorship in Egypt.
"Instead of strengthening civil society, the president de facto strips him of all his powers," Saeda said.
However, a democracy cannot function without civil society organisations.
Saeda feels neglected, including by the international community, which looks at the battle between the different political ideologies on the banks of the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and nervousness.
That could be paid for.
A protester in Tharir Square warns: "You give birth to a monster that it will no longer be possible for you to control."
Norway's rakfisk: Is it the world's most fragrant fish?
The five million people who populate Norway benefit from one of the highest standards of living, not only in Europe, but also in the world.
Could the secret of the country's success be related to the appetite of the locals for an extremely fragrant fish?
Gain advanced cheeses.
Place them in the middle of a stack of wet and dirty football clothes.
Wait a week.
And you get the disturbing smell of rakfisk, one of Norway's most-prized dishes.
I'm in the small town of Fagernes, about 3 hours from Oslo.
It snows, the landscape is spectacular - and this smell, ubiquitous, permeates the air.
Rakfisk consists of trout sprinkled with salt and fermented in water for a period of time – depending on the odor you want it to exhale – which can go up to a year.
When the darkness settles down and it's cold, the Norwegians gather en masse to attend a festival that takes place here in Fagernes, dedicated to this incredible culinary specialty.
“You’re eating raw, then you’re swallowing a glass of aquavit,” says Havard Halvarsen, a full-time local firefighter who’s also known as “Rakfisk General.”
Around us, people swallow small pieces of fish and put on a large amount of drink.
“Some people prefer aquavit to rakfisk,” Havard adds.
Drinking kills the smell.
I'm trying a few pieces.
If you can avoid passing it under your nose, it’s not bad – it makes you think of a sushi that would have spent long hours on a bus.
Rakfisk dates back to a time when Norway was poor, before the invention of refrigeration, the fish was immersed in airtight water barrels, then salted in the autumn.
Then, during the winter, once well fermented, the fish came out and - without a doubt with the senses diminished by the alcohol - eaten.
In the previous generation, thousands of Norwegians were forced to leave their country to find labour, emigrating mainly to the United States.
Now, the population is growing rapidly - more than 13% are immigrants, attracted by full employment, high wages and a comprehensive health care system.
The Swedes, their old rivals of which the country was not so longer than Norway, cross the border to come to work.
Rakfisk is regarded as something important, a vital element, although fragrant, of Norway's past.
That’s one of the most expensive dishes.
But it’s all expensive - a little pint of beer or a sandwich will cost you €10 each.
Norway doesn't often talk about it in international news, which isn't to displease it.
Here, people aren’t willing to talk about Anders Breivik, the right-wing extremist racist who killed 77 people, including women and children.
When they mention this killing, they talk about the "July 22 incident".
The Norwegians find it difficult to accept that in their peaceful country, one of them could have committed such an act of barbarism.
Since the beginning of the 70's, Norway owes its wealth to its oil and gas industry, one of the largest in the world.
"But oil is not the only reason that justifies our success," says Anna, our waitress, with blonde hair and the eyes of a piercing blue, the image of northern well-being, with a rakfisk tray in hand.
We're - how do you say - cautious people.
His English, like that of most of the people who live here, is perfect.
We're quiet, we don't like getting noticed.
Norway has managed its oil wealth with great caution - everything, with the exception of a small percentage of money from this industry, is invested in a special fund for the benefit of future generations.
When other countries threw money out the windows, in the years that led us to the international financial crissis, Norway was able to keep the strings of its purse tight.
"As long as you can ski in winter and go hiking in the summer, we're happy," says Anna.
"Not to mention the rakfisk," she adds in a carefree laughter.
I'm standing under the snow and I'm queuing to buy something to eat - I've had my dose of rakfisk.
On the menu, a burger composed of momentum, a real discovery, it's excellent.
But tonight, the smell of fish is everywhere.
The hotel in which I stay is one of the many places where rakfisk is served for dinner and where you can vote for the best of them - or perhaps for the most fragrant of them.
There's a TV show where the presenter wears a butterfly knot, surrounded by rakfisk plates.
You'd believe in the Eurovision competition.
"What score did you give to the best fish in the Thor-Juergen Mountains?"
"Here's our points, Havard."
Laughter and applause.
A man falls from his chair, perhaps because of the abuse of aquavit.
Or maybe it's because of fish emanations.
The Mexican Enrique Pena Nieto enjoys a difficult start.
As incoming President Enrique Pena Nieto prepares to take office in Mexico, Will Grant of the BBC is interested in the difficulties that await him and the expectations of the people, which are numerous.
Road traffic in Mexico City is particularly bad right now.
In a city where traffic jams are legion in normal times, a security barrage has been set up since Monday, blocking several key road accesses in the capital and causing real chaos on the roads.
Nevertheless, the goal was not to prevent people from going to work, but to prevent protesters from reaching Parliament.
Next Saturday, the new president of Mexico, Enrique Pena Nieto, will receive the presidential scarf to become the new president at the head of the nation.
A complicated task awaits him.
Mexico's economy is doing well, the results we owe to outgoing President Felipe Calderon, but the country is plagued by a drug war that has already claimed the lives of about 60,000 people in six years.
“My government has made a commitment to the people of Mexico to reduce violence,” Pena Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama in the Oval Office earlier this week.
I'm going to propose a new security strategy that will enable us to achieve that goal.
Prior to rubbing shoulders with the U.S. president, Mr. Pena Nieto was Governor of the State of Mexico.
A very populated and sprawling state that encloses the capital. On its land of choice, opinions concerning this new leader are divided.
A Direct Man
In the bucolic commune of Valle del Bravo, for example, it is inscribed in the memory of all.
The premises attribute to him the development of tourism and infrastructure in the municipality.
To reach the city, you can take one of the new highways of M Pena Nieto, an important improvement has been made to these roads, once bumpy and full of crevices.
Plaques bearing his name are hung outside a new sports complex and an impressive interactive museum dedicated to climate change.
“We are counting on him to implement lasting changes,” said his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, a member of Mr. Pena Nieto’s Congress and Party, the PRI.
In terms of security and economy in particular, we hope for a real change that our country so badly needs.
After 81 unwavering years in power, the PRI was ousted in 2000 by Vicente Fox.
Congressman Olvera admits that after 12 years outside the Los Pinos Presidential Palace, there are many expectations within the party regarding Enrique Pena Nieto.
He rejects the critics of opponents who accuse the new president of running out of charisma.
He's a whole man, very committed, with an excellent vision of the country.
He's an excellent statesman and, above all, he knows how to listen.
However, this is not always the image that the people of their former governor have.
In Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast with the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo could not be more glaring.
Hidden under the autoponts of highways, it is, in many ways, a suburb of Mexico City.
And the problems in the municipality are also concrete and urban.
Earlier this year, the military was called to the rescue to fight drug gangs in the neighborhoods, and violence against women is particularly serious.
In a disaster area, the bodies of dozens of women murdered over the past two years have been found on the edge of the discharge.
More than 1,000 women were killed in the state of Mexico while Mr. Pena Nieto was governor, a rate much higher than in the very violent city of Ciudad Juarez - an infamous place for the number of innocent women who were killed there.
The critics of Mr. Pena Nieto, say that at best, he did not manage to stem the problem of murders perpetrated against women when he was in office.
At worst, they accuse his administration of refusing to see reality in the face.
In a concrete house, typical of this dilapidated neighborhood, Irinea Buendia struggles to hold back her tears when she shows me pictures of her deceased daughter, Mariana Luna.
The official version mentions a suicide committed by Mariana in 2010.
However, her family believes that she was killed by her partner.
“When I arrived home, it seemed to me that her body had been washed,” says the Señora Buendia.
Signs indicated that she had been beaten, and cadaveric rigidity had already begun.
As her mother tells the story, a photo of Mariana dresses the wall, next to a cross featuring the word: Justice.
However, this is exactly what the family was denied.
The authorities treated me like I'm an old gossipper, a troublemaker, a whiner.
They want us to accept what they say and shut up.
“But it’s not normal that there are so many irregularities and omissions,” she said.
When President Pena Nieto receives her scarf on Saturday, she will be accompanied by a heavy responsibility.
Tens of thousands of families have been affected by crime in Mexico over the past six years and the new president has made a promise that he will make it his priority during his tenure.
"I hope he'll be as good as he is as president as he did when he was governor," Olvera told Congressmen's Valle del Bravo.
However, this is exactly what the families of the victims of Ciudad Neza fear the most.
Bradley Manning has not complained of mistreatment, prosecutors said.
Prosecutors are trying to counter the claims of mistreat that Bradley Manning allegedly suffered during detention.
The trial concerns the detention of Manning in a military prison in Quantico, Virginia.
The defense wants to close the case on the grounds that Manning's kidnapping took place under extremely difficult conditions.
The soldier is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents.
Prosecutors tried to demonstrate on Friday that Private Bradley Manning — accused of being behind the biggest breach of confidential information in U.S. military history — has missed many opportunities to complain about the ill-treatment he claims to have suffered during his military detention.
In cross-examination with Manning, during a preliminary hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, Prosecutor Maj. Ashden Fein claimed that recordings of weekly visits to Manning by officers during his nine-month detention in Quantico, Virginia, did not reveal any complaints about his treatment.
Cross-examination -- during a hearing, the defence filed a motion requiring that the Manning case be closed on the grounds that the kidnapping took place under very difficult conditions and constituted a sufficiently severe punishment -- took place a day after Manning testified of his desire to commit suicide during his detention.
This military intelligence analyst, arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents during his service in Iraq.
The documents were then published online by WikiLeaks.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed that Manning was their source.
At Friday's hearing, Fein discussed with Manning the forms that the officers had filled out after their meeting with Manning, during his detention in Quantico military prison, where he was subjected to maximum segregation from July 2010 to April 2011.
Officers asked Manning questions and recorded his answers.
When Fein mentioned the forms on Friday, Manning acknowledged that the treatment his guards gave him was "excellent" and that the treatment he had had within the prison was overall "very professional."
The forms do not document any complaints of ill-treatment, although the officers directly questioned him about his prison treatment, Fein said.
Manning responded that he had orally expressed concerns about some of the issues and that the visiting officers had discussed his concerns and indicated that they would deal with them, but they did not document the issues raised.
"They wrote "no problems" (after addressing the concerns), and yet, it didn't mean I didn't raise any issues," Manning said.
Military Judge Col. Denise Lind also asked Manning why he had not complained about his treatment during an interview in January 2011 with the special commission that had met to discuss the suicidal thoughts he had expressed in writing a few months earlier.
Manning responded that his intention during the meeting was to change his restrictive status indicating a "risk of injury".
The military stated that they had assigned him this restrictive status -- a level below close surveillance for the risk of suicide -- for his protection and the safety of others.
“I wanted the staff to know I was okay, and I wanted to be removed from this status... in order to enjoy a better quality of life from my point of view,” Manning said.
Manning testified Thursday about his arrest in Iraq and his transfer to Kuwait, where he was detained for nearly two months before being transferred to military prison at the Marine Corps base in Quantico, Virginia, in July 2010.
He stated that he had considered committing suicide when he was in Kuwait and fainted once because of the heat.
He added that the fact of not having been kept informed of what was going to happen to him or what was happening in the rest of the world was very distressing.
“My world was reduced to Camp Arafjon, to this cage,” Manning said Thursday.
I thought I'd die in that cage.
Once at Quantico, Manning stated that he had spent most of his time in a small cell — at least 21 hours and sometimes more than 23 hours — without a company.
Manning also added that the only objects he had were a mattress, blanket, flip-flops, clothing and glasses.
He tried to keep moving, because sleeping or lying down during the day was contrary to the rules.
Manning also clarified that when he was trying to sleep, he had a light in his eyes, ignited outside his cell.
If the guards didn't see his face when he turned around at night, they would wake him up so they could see his face.
Manning's lawyer filed a formal complaint for Manning's salaries in January 2011.
Manning was placed in a military prison in Fort Leavenworth, Kansas, in April 2011.
Also on Friday, the judge asked Manning about an allegation reported in his testimony on Thursday -- that after being forced to sleep naked overnight in his cell at Quantico, he was forced to stand naked in front of guards and other inmates one morning during the call.
Manning testified that at no time he was allowed to cover himself with coverage during the call.
During the judge’s questions on Friday, Manning said he had inferred from his guard’s order that he had to let go of his coverage that could have hidden his nakedness, but he acknowledged that no one had ordered him to release it.
Manning testified Thursday that he had been forced to sleep naked the night before, as he allegedly tried to prove to an officer that he wasn't a danger to himself.
Manning reportedly told the officer that he could have used the elastic of his underwear or flip-flops to hurt himself, but that he hadn't done so.
That night, Manning testified that his underwearings, flip-flops, and glasses had been removed from his cell.
His lawyers hope that at least the judge will take into account the experiences he experienced during his detention and that he will significantly reduce his sentence if he were to be found guilty before a court martial, a trial that is expected to be held early next year.
The defense said Manning plans to plead culprit for the less serious offenses and fight for the abandonment of other charges deemed too extreme.
The hearing is expected to resume this weekend, with prosecutors arguing that conditions of detention were justified.
The Pentagon has confirmed that Manning has been incarcerated in accordance with the regulations that govern the conditions of detention of all detainees at Quantico.
Charges against Manning include the theft of public property or documents, collusion with the enemy, giving rise to the illegal publication of information on the Internet, and the transmission of information relating to national defence.
If all counts are withholded, he faces life imprisonment.
My Mexican-American Identity Crisis
He says that many have been forced to leave Mexico because of the lack of opportunities
“Mexicans tend to blame those who have left; they remind Mexicans of difficult times,” he said.
Navarrette says that the Mexico-Americans find themselves caught between two worlds.
During a recent trip to Mexico, arriving in the lobby, in front of the customs and immigration services, I felt lost.
Panels indicated two lanes: one for "Mexicanos" ("Mexicans"), another for "Extranjeros".
I stood still for several seconds, not knowing which line to point me.
I grew up in central California, where I've been called the "Mexican" all my life.
It’s an ethnic qualifier, just like the one my Boston friends use when they call themselves “Irish,” or my friends in New York when they say they’re “Italians.”
Then I became "Mexico-American."
But here it was Mexico.
And, on the earth that saw the birth of my grandfather, there is no need for a special qualifier or a link.
I was just an American.
I'm speaking Spanish, good enough to handle an interview in either language.
But I don't have the vocabulary of a native, and I can't lose my American accent.
So I took my U.S. passport and headed to the "Foreign" queue.
I thought at that time of the week when new Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto visited President Obama in the White House.
Generally, when the leaders of these two countries meet, their favorite themes are immigration, drugs and trade.
Pena Nieto also wanted to talk about the growth of the Mexican economy, which is one of the reasons why Mexicans stay in Mexico rather than risking themselves in the United States.
He wants to ally himself with the United States and Canada, and create a Union for Trade on the model of the European Union, but in North America.
And Pena Nieto pledges to continue the war against drug cartels in Mexico, although he did not provide further details.
For Mexico, the relationship with the United States is complicated and filled with bitterness.
Most Americans probably never thought about the fact that in 1848, the United States invaded Mexico and forced its leaders to cede in writing half of their territory at gunpoint.
But for the Mexicans, who think in terms of centuries, and no minutes, the memories are everywhere.
Therefore, as soon as a U.S. official makes any criticism of Mexico, you begin to hear — in the Mexican press, and among the elites — complaints about how the Americans are undermining their neighbor’s sovereignty.
And Montezuma's children are looking for the fight.
And yet, for Mexico, the most difficult relationship is that the country has with the more than 35 million Mexico-Americans living in the United States.
Do you want us to talk about bitterness?
There's a lot.
Mexico has its winners and losers, people for whom the country offers opportunities and others for whom the country does not.
The only reason for the large number of Mexican ancestors in cities like Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio, is that at one point, in our family tree, a person, maybe a parent or grandparent, found no opportunity in Mexico and had to go to the North.
And more often than not, this person corresponded to a typical profile -- a dark, poorly educated skin, from a poor village, etc.
We are their descendants, and we are loyal to them.
Not Mexico.
And even though we’re living the American dream right now, we’ve gone to good schools and we’ve got good situations, we never have to lose sight that it’s the American dream we’re living, not that of the Mexicans.
Sometimes our identity is troubled, but our loyalty is obvious.
We're giving it to the United States.
Moreover, we know that a part of the Mexican elite among the ruling class does not like us.
The feeling is reciprocal.
We remind them of a humiliating defeat and they loom us as if we were individuals of inferior stumps and not enough Mexican.
Our Spanish will never be good enough, our ties with Mexico will never be strong enough.
For them, our existence is synonymous with failure.
If our families hadn't failed when they lived in Mexico, they'd never leave.
And we wouldn’t find ourselves stuck on the right side of the fence, living comfortably in the United States, but as lost souls.
My wife who was born in Guadalajara and emigrated legally to the U.S. while she was a child, reminds me that there is friction between the Mexicans and the Mexico-Americans, because the Mexicans more firmly claim their identity and the Mexico-Americans are upset about it.
Although she's an U.S. citizen, she feels she's part of two countries.
Moreover, many Mexican-Americans I know do not feel that they are part of one country or another.
We love the music of the Mexican band, Los Tigres del Norte, but also Bruce Springsteen.
We have the best of both worlds, but we are not rooted in both.
In Mexico, we are considered Americans.
And in the United States, we're seen as Mexicans.
And now, to complicate the relationship a little more, as I learned during my stay, some Mexican leaders and part of the intelligentsia want to get closer to Diaspora.
They want Mexico-Americans to work as makeshift ambassadors for Mexico, representing his interests in the United States.
We will have to explain to our U.S. compatriots that Mexico is a superb country that must absolutely be visited and put pressure on political leaders to strengthen ties with Mexico.
Yes, of course.
This won't happen.
There's too much bitterness.
And, looking at wage inequality, corruption, and drug-related violence that are pervasive, many of us aren’t convinced that it’s a fantastic country.
You're going to have to manage on your own, amigos.
That's normal.
If some Mexicans are not yet ready to forgive the United States for how they treated Mexico a century and a half ago, they must therefore accept that some Mexico-Americans still want them in the way their family members were treated not so long ago.
Hmmm.
Maybe we're more "Mexicans" than I thought.
Old quarrels, new Middle East
The cease-fire between Israel and Hamas is still a failure for peace.
Can there never be a lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
Another outpouring of blood seems to indicate that such a hope is futile.
As the same futile arguments for who began, dozens of buildings were reduced to dust; more than 140 Palestinians, most of them were civilians, and six Israelis were killed; and, for the first time, rockets fired from Gaza landed near Tel Aviv, Israel’s metropolis, and the holy city of Jerusalem.
But although the Israelis and Palestinians appear to be stranded in their former conflict, all around them, the Middle East is rapidly changing.
The Arab Spring has shattered everything, and whether we like it or not, the Palestinians and Israelis are caught up by this regional upheaval.
Perhaps their struggle will intensify a little more.
Still, there’s every reason to think it could help them get out of this deadly impasse.
A war that makes no losers or winners
At first glance, it seems difficult to be optimistic.
Even if the cease-fire granted on November 21 continues, this week's fighting has strengthened supporters of the force on both sides.
Hamas leaders, the Islamist movement that has ruled Gaza since 2007, will claim to have forced the Israelis to give up, even though Gaza has been struck down.
To have killed some of its leaders and repressed Gaza's 1.7 million people in one of the world's most miserable and overcrowded corners, Israel failed to destroy Hamas.
Indeed, Hamas is gaining ground on the West Bank, the other part of Palestine currently in the hands of their Fatah rivals, the other more moderate Palestinian faction.
Moreover, the leaders of Hamas could say that time is their ally.
The influence of Islamists in the Arab world is getting stronger, and Hamas has made rich and powerful friends.
Turkey, a booming regional power, formerly Israel’s closest Muslim ally, has made and caused Hamas; just like Qatar, one of Golf’s wealthiest and most dynamic states.
Hamas operatives argue and say that the crescent Islamist curves around Israel, from Lebanon, in the north, where Hezbollah’s militia exerts its domination, passing through Syria, where Islamist-trending rebels can overthrow Bashar Assad, and to Jordan, where Hamas allies threaten the king.
And most importantly, on Israel’s southern flank, the rise of the Muslim Brotherhood under the presidency of Muhammad Morsi in Egypt, by far the most populous and central of Arab countries, has changed the balance of the region.
Hosni Mubarak, the despot who ruled Egypt for 30 years until its fall in 2011, had little time to devote to Hamas.
On the other hand, the Muslim Brotherhood are cousins of Hamas, and their leaders rely more easily on public opinion.
In future diplomacy, Hamas can emerge as an important actor that can no longer be excluded even by Israel and America.
Meanwhile, proponents of Israel’s hard line will draw the opposite conclusions.
In military terms, Hamas has been put back in a box.
Iron Dome’s missile interception system has proven its effectiveness and many Hamas missiles have been destroyed.
The Israelis will be able to sleep in both their ears – for a while.
In terms of diplomacy, America renews its unwavering support; and many European countries have blamed Hamas for being at the origin of the last wave of violence.
But above all, Israel flourished, especially under Binyamin Netanyahu, the Prime Minister, who totally ignored the peace process.
Althoung Gaza’s rocket attacks have killed about 30 Israelis since 2004, Israel has been spared by suicide bombings, including through the separation barrier that encroaches on the West Bank, the main piece of what could be a Palestinian state, and that protects Jewish settlements that continue to expand despite their illegality under international law.
Netanyahu, whose Likud party merged with an even more wellicist party, leader Avigdor Lieberman in the run-up to the January 22 election, has a beautiful part.
Why pamper these damn Palestinians by giving them a condition?
If they ran the West Bank, wouldn't they launch rockets, as their compatriots did in Gaza?
It's best to keep them behind this wall and hit them as soon as they get their heads out.
Perhaps the hard-lined proponents will prevail; but it may be that the Arab Spring changes the situation.
Even if Islamists who take power in Egypt and elsewhere do not appreciate Israel, their priority will be to tackle the problems in their own countries.
Israel’s defense budget is more important than that of its four Arab neighbors together.
Starting a war with the local superpower will not help the new Arab governments arrange their economies.
Mr. Morsi worked with Barack Obama to achieve a ceasefire which bodes well -- and could mark the beginning of something.
Israelis should also think about the future.
With the rest of the Arab world becoming more democratic, depriving Palestinians of their right to self-determination could create a powder magazine that is destined to explode one day in Israeli-occupied territories – much like the bus that exploded in Tel Aviv this week.
Repression is already weakening democracy in the Jewish state, and demography is exacerbating this problem as the Arab population grows.
The bloody missions against Gaza every two or three years to sound Hamas will have a diplomatic prize.
Both sides need to be pushed by those from the outside.
The answer remains that advocated by the reasonable people found on either side, and by most of the outside world and by this newspaper: two states, with an assignment of territory by Israel in exchange for security.
A small and short-term hope is that the cease-firefire will provide an opportunity for outsiders to defend this cause.
Egypt, which must now work to end the flow of weapons to Gaza, in the same way as Turkey and Qatar, is better positioned than ever to persuade Hamas to accept the idea of a Jewish state in accordance with the 1967 borders with exchanges of land and shared Jerusalem.
Arabs from outside countries should also put pressure on Hamas and Fatah to unite.
This would contribute much more to the creation of a Palestinian state than the forthcoming proposal of a pseudo-state to the United Nations.
Mr. Obama also has a vested interest in that Israel agrees to negotiate.
During his first term, he did not present his own peace plan.
Returning to the White House, he seems equally reluctant to establish one.
That's a very narrow vision.
The stability of the Middle East is a vital issue for America.
This requires a peace agreement between Israel and the Palestinians.
Laws for Neutral Cigarette Packages came into effect in Australia
Warnings and pictures of diseased organs cover the dark green packages that are all identical, regardless of the mark
This law came into force in Australia is a world first, logos and colors have been replaced by a dark olive green and horrific photos of sick organs and representations of children and babies whose diseases have been caused by the smoking of their parents.
Apart from the various photos and health warnings, the only difference between the packages, since this Saturday, is the name of the brand, printed in fine print on all packages.
It is the strictest tobacco packaging scheme in the world.
The Australian federal government said the goal was to deter young people from smoking by removing any glamorous representations.
Studies have showned that if people haven’t started smoking before the age of 26, there’s a 99% chance they’ll never start.
“Even when they’re very young, children understand the message conveyed by tobacco manufacturers through their brand image,” Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek said, citing studies that revealed, for example, that children were pairing the wreath of a logo with a princess.
Even if Australia has the lowest smoking rate in the world and if these changes have little impact on the profits of multinationals, other countries are considering a similar approach.
The tobacco industry lobbied against this law.
Tobacco manufacturers said this would revive the trade in the black market, which will make it easier to access cheaper cigarettes.
"Pedicable consequences will result from this legislation," said Scott McIntyre of British American Tobacco Australia.
Forgers from China and Indonesia will sell far more counterfeit cigarettes on the streets of Australia.
Others stated that this law boosted their activity.
Sandra Ha of Zico Import Pty Ltd, a small family business, said the demand for cigarette cases, including silicone cases to mask horrific packaging, rose sharply two months ago since British American Tobacco, Britain's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco challenged the law and lost before Australia's high court.
Ha said Zico has sold 6,000 to wholesalers and is awaiting stock renewal.
For us, business is good.
The only boredom, experts say, is the popularity of social media and the segment of population targeted by the plan.
After a series of Australian laws prohibiting TV commercials, sports sponsorship, and the requirement for resellers to conceal cigarettes, tobacco marketing is now available online.
Australia has banned web advertising by businesses and local sites, but it cannot prevent foreign sites from doing so.
“If you’re working in tobacco marketing and you only have a small showcase to promote your products, the Internet is the must-see place,” said Becky Freeman, a public health researcher at the University of Sydney.
Freeman has noticed an increase in the contributions of some average citizens in favor of brands on social media like YouTube, Twitter and Facebook.
We have to ask ourselves if it’s just a citizen who loves Marlboro cigarettes and took the trouble to make a video, or if it’s a marketing campaign orchestrated by an agency?
British American Tobacco Australia has stated that the industry is focusing on the new regulations in force rather than marketing.
Industry went so far as to pay Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to challenge these new court rulings – these countries told WHO that trade is unfairly disadvantaged, although none of these countries had significant trade with Australia.
A decision by the WTO is expected to be taken in the course of 2013.
Plibersek said the government had held discussions with other countries regarding similar packaging laws.
Canada was the first country to make packaging warning photos mandatory in 2001.
These warnings are now present in 40 countries, including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
There are currently stricter laws being studied in England, New Zealand, South Africa and India.
Many smokers in Australia remain insensitive to these photos.
Pictures don't bother me.
I don't know.
“You’re taking a cigarette and you’re storing the package,” Victor El Hage said at the time of purchasing a package that features a tumor photo of his mouth.
To be frank, the only reason I'll get arrested, it's my little girl.
James Yu, manager of the "King of the Pack tobacconist" in central Sydney, said the uniformity of the packages made it difficult to store packages on the shelves.
"Before, it took me an hour to unload a package, now I need four hours," Yu said.
"The government only had to ban them once and for all and we would have said OK, it's okay, we're stopping and we're putting the key under the door," he added, raising our arms to heaven.
In a constantly connected world, it can be good to get bored.
I spent five hours at an airport during the period of Thanksgiving, because our plane had mechanical problems and we had to wait for the arrival of another plane.
As a result, I had enough time to reflect on the theme of boredom.
I won't lie to you.
Spending half a day at an airport to wait for a flight is boring enough, even when you have books, magazines and iPhones to distract yourself (not to mention duty-free shops).
But increasingly, some academics and experts specializing in the development of the child praise boredom.
It’s perfectly normal for us – and our children – to get bored from time to time, they say.
This forces the brain to take interesting paths, even to nurture creativity.
And since we’re, for the most part, constantly in front of several types of screens, we no longer know the benefits of boredom.
So should we welcome boredom with open arms?
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.
And no.
But I'll come back.
First of all, like many people, I thought boredom was a rather recent phenomenon, especially since we have more free time.
But that’s not the case,” says Peter Toohey, professor of Greek-Roman history at the University of Calgary, Canada, and author of “Boredom: A Lively History” (Yale University Press, 2011).
"Boredom goes back very far," he said.
On the walls of Pompeii are Latin graffiti that evoke boredom, they date back to the first century.
Then there’s the definition that we’re giving to boredom.
The problem is that it has been defined, and debated, in different ways," said John D. Eastwood, Associate Professor of Psychology at York University in Ontario, Canada.
After going through numerous publications and submitting the idea to a focus group of about 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as an experience of “wanting to do something, but without succeeding in embarking on a satisfying activity.”
What differentiates boredom from apathy, he says, is that the person is not undertaking a task even though they would.
With apathy, he says, there's no desire to do anything.
What’s at the heart of boredom, he said, is “the interruption of the attention process, coupled with a gloomy mood and the feeling that time passes slowly.”
Boredom can very much look like depression.
But Professor Eastwood said that while the two may be related, people who are bored tend to think that the problem comes from the environment or the world, while depressed people think the problem comes from them.
Sometimes we happen to think that we are bored when it is actually a problem of concentration.
As part of their study, “The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention,” which was published in the journal “Perspectives on Psychological Science” in September, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues highlighted an older experiment in which participants listened on tape, the voice of someone reading a magazine article.
Some groups have heard, from the room next door, the too high sound of a TV show that had no reports, others have just heard the TV show, and the third group has not heard the soundtrack at all.
Those who heard TV at a low sound level said they were more bored than the other two bands – they struggled to concentrate, but didn’t know why, and attributed this problem of concentration to boredom.
When you’re trying to focus on a difficult or engaging task, an interruption of attention can lead to boredom,” said Mark J. Fenske, an associate professor of neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario and one of the study’s authors.
On the other hand, when performing a boring task, "like watching objects on an editing chain, the music can help you don't get bored."
In fact, he says, now, we know that when we swirl or scribble, these gestures, often seen as signs of boredom, are actually a good way to stay physically more alert.
“Studies have shown that children who are allowed to wriggle learn more and withhold more information than those who are forced to stay unmoving,” Fenske said.
We all get bored one day or another - the delay of my plane flight, a speaker with a single-string voice, a particularly boring film.
However, some people are more likely to get bored than others.
To measure this, researchers developed a "Scale of predisposition to boredom" in the 80's.
The scale includes claims like, “Among the tasks I do, many of them are repetitive and monotonous,” and “I have so many interests that I don’t have time to do everything.”
Using such a scale, researchers have discovered that boys tend to get bored more often than girls, said Stephen Vodanovich, a professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, and in particular need numerous and varied stimulations.
In general, teenagers are quicked.
In 1991, Reed Larson, an individual and community development professor at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment during which he contacted nearly 400 teens and their parents seven to eight times a day via a beeper.
He noted that 32 per cent of teens reported being bored at school and during their homework, while 23 per cent reported being bored when they were not in school.
On the other hand, 3 percent of parents reported getting bored.
Professor Larson said he did not know whether today’s boredom percentages, 21 years later, would be higher or lower.
But he added that he knew that “adolescence is a period when boredom reaches its highest level,” especially because children and teenagers don’t have much control over what they want to do.
If I rest my question: Is boredom good for you?
Sometimes not, because in its extreme form, it can cause people to take absurd physical risks, make bets or abuse certain substances to pass the time, as studies show.
In contrast, many philosophers and writers point to a link between boredom and creativity, said Professor Vodanovich, who has studied the problem for more than two decades.
“Boredom is the way the brain has found to let you know that you should do something else,” said Gary Marcus, a psychology professor at the N.Y.U.
But the brain doesn’t always know which is the most appropriate thing to do.
If you get bored and use that energy to play the guitar and cook, it will make you happy.
But if you’re watching TV, it’ll make you happy in the short term, but not in the long run.
So if your child gets bored and you give him an iPad, he won't get bored anymore, but he won't have learned how to take care of himself or self-adjusting himself, says Professor Fenske.
And "this self-regulation goes from one situation to another," he said.
Your child doesn’t just learn how to have fun, he gets more mastery of himself in other areas.
I don't think we should glorify boredom.
Just like I don't think we should criticize him either.
Feeling comfortable outside of the constant bubbling of activities and technology is the goal we should strive towards.
Professor Eastwood agrees on this point.
“We need to get bored more often, but boredom arouses an acute and frenetic desire to be connected to something that makes sense,” he said.
According to him, what people are looking for is a way to disconnect and enjoy downtime.
“In an environment where we’re constantly hyperstimulated,” he added, “it’s hard to find ways to get into something when the noise stops.”
In Colorado, there is no direction for the new marijuana law
Anthony Orozco, 19, a university student and a football player in Colorado, is charged with something that will soon become legal in his condition: possession of marijuana pellets and a pipe used to smoke them.
Orozco said that one day, in September, he and a few friends were aboard a car in Lamar, in the middle of the fields, near the border with Kansas, when they were arrested.
After the police officer found the marijuana in the car, Mr. Orozco received a subpoena for possession of drugs and equipment that could be used for its use - minor offenses, each accompanied by a fine of $100 - and an appearance date.
“We’re treated like criminals,” Orozco said.
Is there one of them?
During weeks of uncertainty following Colorado’s vote to legalize the use of small amounts of marijuana for its personal use, the response to hundreds of cases involving low drug use depends more on the location than the law.
Here and in the state of Washington, which has adopted a similar measure, hundreds of offenses involving the use of marijuana have been dismissed.
Police services have stopped indicting adults 21 years of age and older for minor possession of marijuana, as the law will come into effect in the coming weeks.
But prosecutors in Colorado’s more conservative constituencies have called for speeding up existing marijuana cases and continuing to indict the people who own them.
At the same time, several cities in the suburbs of Denver to the Western Mountains are currently voting to prevent the opening of state-licensed marijuana shops in their neighborhoods.
"Things are changing so quickly that I don't know what the future holds for us," said Daniel J. Oates, Aurora's chief of police, east of Denver.
Washington state regulators are also asking quite a few questions.
They are seeking guidelines on how to install a licensing system, authorizing to produce, manufacture, distribute and sell - prior to the December 1, 2013 deadline.
They say that Colorado, whether we like it or not, is ahead of most states when it comes to regulating marijuana, first of all when it comes to use for therapeutic purposes and now when it comes to personal use.
"Colodola has a more regulated market, and is a good indicator," said Brian E. Smith, spokesman for the Washington State Liquor Control Board.
But no place or system, Mr. Smith acknowledges, can do more than suggest what works.
"There's no real precedent that we could rely on," he said.
Washington’s I-502 law comes into effect on December 6, leaving a one-year legal vacuum, during which the licensing system will still not exist, while possession of marijuana will be legal.
During this time, practical questions remain outstanding: how to balance the state's mandate that guarantees appropriate access to licensed marijuana and prohibits the installation of cannabis shops within a radius of 30 km around a school, park, playground or nursery.
“There won’t be more difficult places than urban areas to set up a licensed cannabis sales business, especially in the Seattle metropolitan area,” said Ben Livingston, a spokesman for the Cannabis Legalization Center, a recently formed research group.
On 21 November, the chief of police, Oates in Aurora, sent an e-mail to his officers announcing that the "City attorney" (representative of the public prosecutor) would no longer prosecute people 21 years of age and older who possess small amounts of marijuana, and that the police had to stop indicting people for this type of "immedicatable" crime.
Chief Oates said the police would enforce the new court rulings that regulate the activity of those who grow marijuana for medical purposes, and that he would continue to prosecute drug traffickers and drug dealers.
In Weld County, in northern Colorado, "district attorney", Ken Buck embodies a stricter vision.
After the vote, he said his office would continue to deal with marijuana possession business, mainly in order to put pressure on users to be detoxified.
Currently, 119 people are charged with possessing 56 grams or less of marijuana, but many of them are the subject of other charges.
“Our office has an obligation to prosecute those people who committed an offence who, at the time, was a recognized offence,” Buck said.
Reactions have been complicated even in rural areas like Mesa County, where voters have rejected the initiative in favor of restricted marijuana use.
Police in Grand Junction, the county's largest city, no longer appearing adults for possession of restricted quantities.
The representative of the county's prosecution, Pete Hautzinger, supported this decision, but also decided not to classify all marijuana detention cases without follow-up.
“I don’t feel like I’m wasting my time continuing to enforce the law until it changes,” he said.
Although 55 percent of Colorado voters supported the measure, providing an appropriate legal and governmental framework for the personal use of marijuana is not easy.
And the contradictory reactions that have been observed in different U.S. cities highlight the profound ambivalence among local officials when it comes to the legalization of marijuana.
“It’s a cultural barrier” with prosecutors, said Sean McAllister, a lawyer in Denver who represents people accused of marijuana use and speaks out as a local spokesperson for the National Organization for the Reform of Marijuana Laws.
“They’ve spent much of their lives suing people who used marijuana, so they’re struggling to accept that it’s legal,” he said.
As the first states to treat restricted marijuana use at the same level as that of alcohol, Colorado and the state of Washington are about to become a case of jurisprudence for the legalization of drugs.
As advocates and state officials provide for a new legalized sales frontier, they are anxiously awaiting guidelines from the federal government, which still views the sale and cultivation of marijuana as federal crimes.
Advocators for the legalization of marijuana hope that the Ministry of Justice will yield.
Despite some publicized arrests of patients on medical marijuana and vendors, the federal government has mostly allowed medical marijuana sales businesses to practice in Colorado, Washington and 16 other states.
If it’s true that the police are probably not going to break down doors to grab small packets of drugs, there’s a bet that they’re opposed to self-certed businesses selling self-used marijuana authorized by the new laws, said Kevin A. Sabet, a former political adviser to the Obama administration.
Many cities in Colorado did not wait for federal authorities to act.
Long before the votes, some local governments approved moratoriums regarding the creation of the new marijuana sales businesses, even if it is only a one-year deferral.
Last week, the city of Montrose obtained a 6-month moratorium, and is expected to impose this ban next week.
“We don’t want to end up in a situation where a permit is granted to someone and then find ourselves facing a federal problem,” said Bob Nicholson, a city council member.
Our community voted against this amendment.
We are interested in the vote of the community rather than the vote of the state.
A lot of questions arise.
Petronella Wyatt: I was abused and excluded from Oxford because I was a "Tory".
It’s not just today’s students who are attacked for their opinions.
As long as I can remember, I've always dreamed of entering the University of Oxford.
My father and my older brother studied in what was for me an eminent place of knowledge, a kind of Greek amphitheater of modern times that stimulates the two pillars of civilization, free thought and tolerance.
However, two weeks after moving to Worcester College in the late 80's to study history, I decided to pack my bags, causing the first scandal of my life.
My dad collapsed and started crying.
My friends were bewildered.
L'Evening Standard's diary claimed that I had left this university because I objected to the fact that undergraduate students had sex in the room next to mine.
Author A. N. Wilson stated with facety that I had left after being forced to drink in chipped cups.
The truth was less funny.
I've run away.
Yes, I fled, because I had been bullied and subjected to constant bullying.
Not because of my rather outrageous name, or because I came from a private school.
I was persecuted for a single reason, and in this cradle of so-called knowledge, I was the victim of racist and barbaric behavior: my late father, Woodrow Wyatt, was a very influential adviser to Margaret Thatcher and I was a supporter of the Conservative Party.
Why bring this up today, you might say.
Well, recent studies have revealed that a new generation of center-right students are experiencing the same type of persecution.
The growing and institutionalized hatred of Tory students in Oxford is such that last week, some of them have demanded protection of their rights, as well as those of gays, people with disabilities and ethnic minorities.
Conservative members of Corpus Christi College’s Student Room (JCR) said they were “often isolated, personally attacked and felt undesirable” because of their political views.
They want there to be a representative on the University Committee for Equal Opportunity so that their views can be freely expressed.
Their situation was not facilitated by the recent documentary broadcast on BBC Two "Wonderland: Young, Bright and on the Right", devoted to student politics, which portrayed the "Tories" as eccentrics and neo-Nazis.
This included student Joe Cooke, former president of the "Oxford University Conservative Association (OUCA)", who travels in Rolls-Royce, wears a silvery whole and travels with a chrome-headed cane.
In other universities, conservative students say they are treated as scapegoats of setting up the tuition increase.”
Luke Black, 20, vice president of the "Nottingham University Conservative Association", told a Sunday newspaper "that there is a growing left stake in universities.
“People think we’re like the Bullingdon Club without even meeting us.”
Samuel Roberts, a 21-year-old history student at Corpus Christi, who moved a motion for more protection, says such a climate is "uncomfortable", and Stephanie Cherill, 19, president of OUCA, says there has been a deteriorating attitude of JCR members towards people who are center-right.
"This is an impediment to the freedom of intellectual debates, as well as the well-being of members," she said.
I was the only one to think like this during my first weeks spent in Oxford.
I entered in September 1986, I was then 18 years old and shyness.
The hatred of the Conservative party was very virulent.
In the previous year, the university had voted the refusal to grant Margaret Thatcher - a former student - an honorary title, because of the reduction in the funding of higher education.
The atmosphere was charged with Stalinist vibrations.
From the first days of the welcome week, when the new students got acquainted with the others and the professors, I had a taste of what was going to happen afterwards.
I discovered that professors not only participated in sarcasm against Tory students, but that in addition, they took pleasure.
It was as if the policy about striking miners, privatization, and the government’s opposition to sanctions against apartheid in South Africa was etched in the woods of classrooms.
The first sarcasm of which I was the victim concerned a translation into English of the 18th century French texts, and I was not prepared for it.
"Miss Wyatt," Professor Harry Pitt (now deceased), said, "please translate the first paragraph"
I've lost my feet.
Pitt, a little man with a pustulous face, won easily.
“Are Thatcherists refusing to learn French or are they just stupid?” he asked.
The other students laughed.
Tears were burning my eyes.
"I suggest that you take French classes in your free time - well, if you're not too busy making friends," he said.
I went back to my room, inconsolable.
At dinner that night, I sat alone and then I felt a slight slap on my shoulder.
He was a second-year student of English language studies, named James, who introduced himself as a member of OUCA.
"I know who you are," he said, gently.
I'm afraid it'll be that way.
Anyone suspected of being a Tory (Conservative) is targeted.
It's hard enough for me, but they know that your father's close to Margaret Thatcher, so it'll be even harder for you.
Most young Tory claim to be Labour.
Later, at the local pub, I cowardly tried to hide.
I insisted that I did not agree with everything Mrs Thatcher said.
This scheme has failed.
A first-year student of philosophy, political and economic sciences who, ironically, had studied in Eton, said, "You're the daughter of a fascist pig."
You're contaminated.
Other students took the chorus again.
I was perverted, dirty.
"How do the Tories make love?" asked one of them.
“They’re hitting each other, aren’t they?”
I felt like homosexuals before the liberal legislation of the sixties.
Could I have just lived a normal life in Oxford?
Would I have been forced to meet like-minded people only after dark?
Should I have become Labour and denied my opinions?
The next three years were going to look like a purgatory of ostracism and isolation.
The only openly Tory professor was Norman Stone, professor of modern history, who taught at my university.
He was hated not only because he was conservative, but also because he was Thatcher's foreign policy adviser and one of the authors of his speeches.
He was almost never there.
He hated this provincial and insignificant place, as well as his adherence to the Marxist-deterministic point of view of history.
In 1997, he accepted a professorship at Bilkent University in Ankara, Turkey.
"You won't be happy here," he told me.
I started commuting between Oxford and my parents' house in London, finding refuge with my family and friends more open-spirited city dwellers.
I told my father that I hated Oxford and explained why.
He was unbelieving.
During his studies at Oxford in the forties all political viewpoints were accepted.
“But it’s the best place in the world,” he told me pathetically.
They wouldn't do that, they wouldn't do it in the city with the dreaming bell towers.
Even my communist friends had irreproachable ways.
He had tears in his eyes.
Give them a chance.
I'm sure it's to tease you.
It would break my heart if you left Oxford.
Tired by my frequent travels to London, my emotional resistance has gradually deteriorated.
A friend, also Tory, succumbed to the pressure and gave up his convictions.
During a session of works conducted the following week, when another history teacher suggested the most seriously in the world, that I was "an enemy of the people", I decided to do the same.
Blushing internally with shame, I admitted to being the victim of the brainwashing of my parents and called them "old fools".
Respite was short-lived.
It was my father who broke the last nail of my Oxford coffin.
At that time, he wrote two columns in Murdoch's press each week.
My door was locked.
I lurked in my room, and after five minutes, my pursuers gave up.
When they left, I packed and took the first train to London.
I've never returned.
You can call me a wheavy chicken.
But no 18-year-old should be bullied in a school.
The most tragic is that it was Oxford, which not only produced 14 Prime Ministers Tory, but also enjoys an unwavering reputation for equality and freedom of thought.
"Valentino prefers elegance to notoriety"
As part of the exhibition "Valentino: Un Maître de la Couture", inaugurated this week in London, ABC interviewed Naty Abascal, Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza and other famous clients of the Italian designer.
Somerset House, the former residence of Elizabeth I of England, is the only place of the British capital worthy of hosting an exhibition on Valentino Garavani.
The couturier recognized it during the inauguration of "Valentino: a Master of the Couture", a prestigious retrospective that brings together more than 130 models of haute couture created for his fashion house over the last 50 years.
“I love this palace,” he said with his inimitable Italian accent.
This exhibition is the highlight of a story whose only protagonist is "he signores" Garavani, but which could not have been written without his famous clients.
Valentino has always been fascinated by the closed world and apart from nobility.
In the first room of this exhibition, opened until March 3, we find an impressive number of private letters and photos signed by the cream of the aristocracy, from Princess Salimah Aga Khan to Lord Snowdon, passing through Princess Marie-Chantal of Greece and Margaret of England.
Valentino exposes his personal memories as the trophies of his social ascent: from the modest designer of the city of Voghera, in northern Italy, to the idol of the international jet-set.
There's nothing wrong with loving royalty.
“At least they don’t crush their cigarette butts on your beautiful rug like some pop music stars do,” says Baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza.
In the 60's and 70's, we both lived in the Alps and were good friends.
Valentino is a spectacular host and receives with generosity and elegance.
"We all loved to be invited to his chalet in Gstaad," explains the ex-wife of "Heini" Thyssen, an intimate friend of beauties of the past like Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
Valentino has always preferred elegance to notoriety.
Even so, he’s a star.
Valeria Mazza, wearing a model Valentino
The Argentinian model, Valeria Mazza, also did not forget the charisma of the designer.
Several years ago, after a parade on the Spanish Steps, in Rome, we went for dinner at his apartment.
There were about twenty people, including Sharon Stone and John Kennedy Jr.
You could see and feel your mind in every detail of your apartment, in decoration, food, music.
"With him, each guest felt important and appreciated," recalls the "top model", who began working with him during Haute-Couture week in Paris in 1995.
"He will never pass fashion, because his creations are works of art," she concludes.
Lineage of nobility parade
Garavani's life is not a story of obsessions but of shared loves.
He likes well-behaved people and with the biggest titles and they love him too.
One of Somerset House's galleries has been transformed into a glamorous sixty-meter wide podium that proposes to exchange roles: the visitor takes the place of the models and has to parade on the podium to admire a dream "public" bearing the main creations of Valentino, like the tailor that Jackie Kennedy had chosen for his wedding with Aristotle Onassis, the costume that Monica Vitti wore in "La Notte", or a coat
Among this audience of models, there are names such as Sybil of Luxembourg, Gloria von Thurn und Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosario of Bulgaria or Sophie of Habsbourg.
Naty Abascal and the Couturier in 2006
Many of his clients say that the first Valentino clothes are like the first love, "impossible to forget".
I remember it perfectly.
It consisted of trousers, a shirt, a "gillette" vest and a jacket from the autumn-winter 1971-1972 collection.
"It's a gift he gave me," said Naty Abascal, one of the designer's muses.
"I prefer it to everyone else for his femininity, his great love of women, and because it sublimates our beauty," adds the ex-Duchess of Feria.
I really like the colors he uses, they are bright and illuminate the face.
Proportions are perfect.
The princess and fashionist Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenberg cannot forget the first time she wore a Valentino model.
When she was a teenager and lived in Italy, she dreamed of being old and the opportunity to wear one of her evening dresses...
Finally, it was possible in the late 90’s.
I bought my first Valentino model for a party in the castle of my cousin, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red evening dress, with a ruffled skirt, a draped bustier, and a décolleté to fall.
“It was a dream come true,” Rodrigue d’Arenberg’s widow said.
"Valentino is indifferent to fashion, he is obsessed with the timeless," explains the Italian aristocrat who divides his life between Paris, New York and Buenos Aires.
The princess of Arenberg preserves the evening dresses of the designer with "the utmost care, because an evening dress is not only a dress, she is also all the memories she conveys".
The "King" of Fashion
The grand finale of Somerset House's exhibition is Marie-Chantal Miller's wedding dress worn at her marriage to Paul of Greece in 1995.
It took four months of work and 25 "ragazze" (the couturier calls his seamstresses) to make this ivory silk dress with inlays of beads, twelve kinds of different laces and a trail of four and a half meters.
According to journalist Suzy Menkes, supreme authority of the specialized press, this dress represents a historical fact of haute couture of the end of the 20th century, "the return of clients of high society".
Obsessed for years by the "know-being" of the nobility, Valentino is now its best representative.
Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the greatest distinction in Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commander of the Order of Arts and Letters, decorated with the Legion of Honor, Garavani has as many honours as any spouse of his clients.
"His refinement, its calmness, its neat and perfect appearance have always jumped out to my eyes," admits d'Arenberg.
The last time I saw him, it was a month ago at a gala dinner at the Musée d'Orsay.
He sat at Countess Jacqueline de Ribes' table, one of my great friends.
"He was impeccable, the years have no effect on him."
If it's a princess saying it...
The hardest job in the world: the human mules of Kawah Ijen
For four euros, the bearers of the Indonesian volcano risk their lives and their health by carrying 70 kilograms of sulfur through the steep stone trails.
There are people for whom work is hell and others who literally work in hell.
This is the case of Anto Wijaya, one of the 400 miners who make a living by carrying the sulfur of Kawah Ijen volcano, east of the Indonesian island of Java.
It has to work every day to the bottom of the crater, where the sulfuric gas emanating from the bowels of the earth solidifies in contact with the air.
Having harvested large sulphur rocks, which weigh a total of nearly 70 kilos, he installs them in two bamboo baskets that he loads on his shoulders through the steep trails.
There are only 250 meters up to the top of the volcano which rises to 2.386 meters altitude, but the exhausted carriers take more than 40 minutes to climb this distance, at a turtle pace, keeping the balance and measuring their steps so as not to stumble and fall into the precipice.
They know that a misstep could cost them their lives, as happened to a French tourist who made a deadly fall in Kawah Ijen volcano several years ago.
Kawah Ijen’s miners earn 5 euro cents for every kilogram of sulfur extracted.
Once at the top, they make their way between the tourists who photograph them as if they were circus beasts, and painfully carrying the heavy baskets, they walk the 3 kilometers to the balance that a mining company installed a little below at 1.850 meters of altitude.
They are PT Candi Ngrimbi, a company that operates the volcano since 1960 and, it can be said, its employees, to whom it pays 662 Indonesian rupees (5 euro cents) per kilo of sulphur.
It then sells it at 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents) to the petrochemical industry since this ore is very present in everyday life and is used to manufacture candles, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and is even used to bleach sugar.
"As we carry about 70 kilos, we earn about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) with each trip," explains Anto, who usually makes three trips a day.
Each takes 3 hours and he finishes exhausted, but it allows him to earn 138,000 rupees (11.50 euros) at the end of the day.
Although it seems to be a misery for such a superhuman effort, it is the triple of what it would gain in the fields.
"The income of miners is very high here, where the coffee harvest is paid 15,000 rupees (1.20 euros) per day and where the average monthly salary is two million rupees (167 euros)," says the bearer who once worked as a mason on the tourist island of Bali.
There, his salary was 75,000 rupees (6.20 euros) a day and the job was not as difficult, but Anto returned near his family to Banyuwangi, a village close to the volcano, for a reason which, in Indonesia, is as indisputable as sulfur: "I married a young girl from Bali, where they are Hindu, and I brought her back to Java so that she
Anto suffers from asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases.
At the age of 27, he spent three of his life defying danger on the Kawah ljen, but the sulfur has already begun to make him pay the bill even though he covers his face with a mask and wears special gloves.
Anto suffers from asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by the toxic gases released by the volcano.
That's the price he has to pay to pursue his dream.
"I'm going to work for another two years, because I want to open a shop or study Spanish or French," he promises in more than acceptable English.
Struggled by life, this friendly and intelligent young person could be a tourist guide, waiter or receptionist in a hotel, but, instead, he does the work of a mule.
He shares an unsanitarious body of wood with other porters, rises every day at two o'clock in the morning because sulfur does not cease to spring up at night, when its characteristic yellow color turns blue and shines in the midst of darkness.
Defying the darkness, Anto descends into the crater by lighting himself with a small lamp attached to his helmet, which he bought himself with his own money.
Some 400 carriers charge the sulfur baskets on their shoulders from the bottom of the crater.
Despite its comfortable profits, the mining company did not mechanize the extraction of sulphur to save costs and does not provide any equipment to carriers who are self-employed and are paid by weight.
In addition, they also do not touch one of the 30,000 rupees (2.50 euros) of surcharge per camera, in addition to the entrance of 15,000 rupees (1.20 euros), which the guards of this natural park charge tourists who come to photograph the volcano and its human mules.
“This work is done for beasts, not for humans,” said Madrusin, a 42-year-old corpulent carrier who has worked in Kawah Ijen for thirty years, since leaving school.
Capable of carrying up to 110 kilos, he assures that he will not stop working "as long as he can", because he needs money to raise his 3 sons, aged between 10 and 18 years old.
I won't retire, I'll die here because the volcano is all my life.
Although sulfur damages the throat and irritates the eyes when the wind turns and traps the miners in the thick columns of smoke coming out of the volcano, they are so hardened that no one complains of suffering from serious diseases... to which are obviously added their usual breathing problems, osteoarthritis, pain in the knees and shoulders, distorted by the weight of baskets.
By balancing the basket on his back, Unainik, 53, can only charge 50 kilos.
Every day, his companions extract 15 tons of sulfur from the volcano, which three trucks transport to the Tamansari warehouse, 18 kilometers away by a goat path invaded by weeds.
"I won't retire, I'll die here because the volcano is all my life," Unainik said, opening his mouth, revealing several missing teeth.
The eldest of his five children, aged 30, also works as a sulfur carrier.
Time passes, but misery perpetuates from generation to generation one of the most difficult trades in the world: that made by the human mules of Kawah ljen volcano.
Singapore looking for babies to save the economy
Singaporeans blame this lack of children on the career, the stress and the cost of homeownership.
“The people of Singapore need to develop.”
I am a husband and patriot, you are my wife and a patriot, let's fulfill our civic duty and give life!
It may seem implausible that these lyrics come from an advertisement for mint lozenges, but in spite of that, or perhaps thanks to that, the video has spread like wildfire on YouTube in Singapore earlier this year.
The lyrics come from a rap group that uses local references like "Let's get a polichinelle in the drawer" to make fun of Singapore's birth rate.
BBH, the advertising agency behind the video, hopes to draw attention to the problem in a fun way through this advertisement.
Its creative director, Douglas Hamilton, said he wanted to use the power of music for people to fulfill “their national duty.”
It’s only for the Internet, so we were able to make it entertaining and fun.
That is the main problem in this country.
We are the worst in the world in the field of health, so we felt that this was a theme that we had to address.
We knew that the government had tried several things such as throwing perfumes at pheromones or organizing speed-dating evenings.
Many of these ideas were creative, but they didn’t necessarily work.
So we said to ourselves the following thing: what could be more creative to solve this problem than to turn it into a rap song?
1.2 Children
The government of Singapore doesn’t take it so lightly.
It spends US$1,300 a year on policies to encourage its citizens to have more children.
A government measure in favor of marriages and fathers grants up to US$15,000 per child, extends maternity leave and distributes tax benefits.
But all of this has had little effect.
Singapore is a rich, high-tech city-state of Southeast Asia, also known for the conservatism of its rulers and strict social controls.
Singapore's birth rate, in line with its national population distribution, currently stands at 1.2 children per woman.
The last time it was above 2, considered the renewal rate, was in 1976.
Why don't Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, director of marriage and family policies at the National Population Division, says it is due to "better education" and "a wider choice of career opportunities".
"This has allowed people to have a wider range of options in terms of life goals and priorities, beyond marriage and the constitution of a family," she explains.
These changes in social norms have contributed to an increase in the number of singles and delayed marriage and births, resulting in a decrease in the birth rate in Singapore.
In the meantime, an immigration policy aimed at radically increasing immigration to counter the decline of the population has created resentment among the local population.
In Singapore, there are websites that abound barely disguised xenophobia against new immigrants, especially against the Chinese who are accused of pulling wages down and not integrate.
The increase in immigration is also seen as one of the reasons why the party in place in Singapore experienced its worst electoral results last year since independence.
Since the elections, they have tried to correct the problem with the introduction of higher quotas and taxes for foreign workers.
Unexpered consequences
While the fall in the birth rate has known effects on a country’s economic growth, tax revenues, health costs and immigration policies, Singapore’s example also has unintended consequences.
The government is trying to avoid the construction of many small houses.
For example, this has begun to affect the real estate sector.
Urban development authorities have begun to control the number of small apartments, known as a "shoe box", which can be built in specific areas of the city.
These apartments have an area of 46 square meters and their sale is very successful.
However, authorities are concerned that they can promote a single lifestyle and discourage promoters wanting to build large family homes.
But Lim Yew Soon, managing director of real estate company EL Developers, says his “shoeboxes” sell much faster than larger homes.
They are more popular, in the sense that these dwellings are built much faster than those of larger size.
For this reason, it is much more profitable for our treasury.
However, he admits that the new standards give clearer instructions to the developers, who were previously put sticks in the wheels if they wanted to offer too many small dwellings in a project.
Too Much Stress
Singapore is a city-state.
While these new regulations can be a step towards raising the national birth rate, by talking with Singaporeans working in the centre's financial district, it would seem that they will not have many effects.
"People are very stressed, homes are expensive, just like education, that's why people put it off," says a young executive.
Other people may have children.
"But for me, it's important to be able to dispose of my money and my time," says another twenty-year-old.
Men and women mention their careers, the stress and cost of access to property and education as the reasons preventing them from having children.
Thus, although the government is trying to convince its citizens to have children, when it comes to making babies, it is the Singaporeans themselves who have the final say.
What's Private Offline Is Private Online
Confidentiality
In accordance with the Dictionary of the Royal Spanish Academy, this is the quality of the private domain or "the field of privacy that we can protect from any intrusion".
What is in the private domain for those under the age of 16?
How to apply this definition in everyday life and on social networks?
Does he understand the dangers to which he exposes himself by broadcasting events on the Internet that he would certainly not share offline?
The elPeriódico newspaper interviewed five young boys between the ages of 10 and 15, frequent users.
In four of the cases, they associated the term "something very personal" on a personal level and "the password and username" when they applied it to social networks.
“I won’t reveal my most personal secrets in a post,” says Jorge, ten, when he’s trying to explain the meaning of the private term on sites like Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail, and Windows Live Messenger, where he’s owned an account for two years.
“They’re very secret secrets, my mother can know them but not everyone,” he said.
On FB, I post nice pictures or games.
I'm chatting with my friends, too.
“I won’t share one of my photos or someone who would make the fool,” he says.
The boy acknowledges that it is wrong to post obscene photos, bare persons, crimes or write humiliating or violent comments.
Jorge says he knows his 35 friends on FB as well as his nine followers on Twitter.
The majority are of the family.
Her mother is one of them, she knows the password of one of the accounts.
I opened a Twitter account to express myself and write interesting tweets.
"I don't know if they're answering me, I'm just posting them," he added.
“Social media is fun, I can talk with distant relatives or with my friends quickly,” he says.
He says he would never accept the request of an unknown person.
He won't trust anyone who would recommend a stranger either.
José’s case, 14, is different.
The teenager has accounts on Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask, on the latter, he admits not to know 20 of the people registered on his list of friends.
“It doesn’t worry me because we have something in common, music for example,” he said.
According to the young boy, no one made advances or asked for his address or phone number.
"If they put pressure on me or demanded it, I'll just delete them from my account," he admits.
Jose subscribed to Ask after reading a recommendation on Twitter.
The experiences we know today as cyberbullying are no strangers to the young boy.
A relative of one of my friends was racketed on a social network.
They threatened him and demanded money.
“He never knew who it was,” he said.
The victim, according to José, did not close his account.
"He only put it in a private profile."
He then explains a series of manipulations to configure his account safely.
Unlike Jorge, this younger would post photos of acquaintances in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
Yeah, I'll do it if I was angry or pissed.
“Even though I know it’s cyberbullying,” he said.
Key Questions
Marielos Porras, professor of English and graduated in Education and Learning, considers that, in order to guide children and teenagers, it must be understood that the purpose of social networks is to inform.
“The Internet arose as a way to look for information but, with the appearance of these sites, the rule of the game has changed,” she said.
Porras reports that academician Marc Prensky, who holds a master's degree in pedagogy from Yale University and author of the book "Natives and Digital Immigrants", uses these terms to explain the phenomenon.
Numerical natives are them, children and young people who were born with technology.
"We are the digital immigrants who have to teach them there, while we ourselves are learning," she says.
He adds that the theme is complex, "because we ask them to have a defined criterion on what or not to disclose, publish or say at an age where they do not have the maturity to do so."
"We also ask them to be selective when what matters to them most is to be popular, to have thousands of friends without thinking about the consequences," she added.
According to the specialist, the most effective way to teach privacy to children and adolescents is to use questions that make them think.
“Telling them not to do it isn’t helpful,” she said.
Then, Porras lists a few examples: there are things you wouldn’t tell a stranger, why are you doing it on the Internet?
Or, would you liKe they're posting a picture of you like that of a friend you published?
Do you know what others post about you?
When you identify holiday photos, do you ask other people for permission to identify them?
Also, does everyone need to know what you’re doing every moment?
Another point is to make them understand that they have to act online as they do offline.
These are the same rules.
"Outside of the Internet, everyone has to act with respect, morality and other principles, it must be the same on social networks," she said.
Supervision
Estuardo Guardia, university professor, primary school teacher and education counsellor, says it is essential that parents conscientiously read the conditions of use of social networks.
By understanding each of the terms, they will have a solid foundation to discuss with their children the implications of opening an account on the Internet.
“For example, age and what’s permissible to share or publish,” he says.
According to Guardia, it is essential to remind children that we should not talk to strangers.
Unasur summit ended without the Lima Declaration being made public
The Sixth Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) ended today in Peru without the Lima Declaration having been made public, although it has been announced previously and theoretically adopted by the 7 representatives present.
Efe tried several times to access the document signed at the 6th Ordinary Meeting of Unasur Heads of State and Government, but the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Presidency initially reported that it would be disclosed after the closing of the summit, then claimed that it would be posted from time to time on the Peruvian government's website.
When questioned about the text, they reported that its content had been exposed by the President of Peru, Ollante Humala, in brief statements to the press.
Journalists' access to information about the summit has been constantly restricted.
During the day, only the video without the sound of the presidential assembly with the message "private session, restricted audio" was broadcast in the press room.
The lack of information circulating between the journalists was that given by the press attachés of some of the Unasur governments who attended the rendezvous, with the exception of the Peruvian spokesman.
The only document leaked during the day was the list of presidents present, resulting in some unease among the hundreds of journalists from various national and international meditaries who demanded more important information.
Later, Peru’s presidency sent an e-mail to the media with the “final declaration” of the summit, but it was President Humala’s speech and not the official document that concluded the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the 3rd South America-Arab Summit (ASPA) and, on that occasion, although this had been requested by the press, it had also not released the statement also called the Lima Declaration, as had previously been announced.
On the official website of the ASPA, we can verify that the document was published last March.
In the two international appointments, the Peruvian authorities have tried to set up secure transmission systems for all journalists, but they have limited obtaining information as much as possible.
The summit also concluded with the joint agreement of Chile and Peru to accept a judgment of the Hague Court which decides a different border between the two countries.
The presidents of Peru, Ollanta Humala, and Chile, Sebastián Piñera, met at the regional meeting and said they would abide by the ICJ's decision to hear the pleas of the two sides in the lawsuit that Lima filed against Santiago, which begins Monday in The Hague.
"We will respect and execute the verdict that will resolve the disputes we make today before this international court," Humala said.
“Chile has always been, and will continue to be a country respectful of international law, the peaceful resolution of international controversies, treaties and tribunals,” Piñera added, giving President Humala a handshake alongside the flags of both countries.
Confirmation by the two presidents that they would submit to the ICJ came after Colombia denounced the Bogotá Pact this week by which it had agreed to submit to the decisions of that international tribunal, following a decision on maritime boundaries with Nicaragua that it considered gravely erroneous.
The summit was held without the presence of the presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff, Venezuela, Hugo Chávez of Bolivia, Evo Morales, and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, suspended from Unasur since 2011 after the impeachment of its ex-president Fernando Lugo, did not participate in the appointment.
The host president, Ollanta Humala, was tasked to open deliberations in the morning and close the summit shortly after noon in Lima.
The representative read the final document in which he announced that 16 agreements had been adopted and that the action lines of 31 projects had been established between the South American countries for a total of US$17 million in investment.
Among the agreements adopted, he mentioned that Unasur countries would put in place "important steps to achieve the goal of South American citizenship through the development of agreements on residence permits".
He informed that actions would be put in place to improve "co-operation in the fight against urban insecurity and against international organized crime, actions for better accessibility to medicines, low-cost Internet access throughout South America, and to jointly and effectively assess the risks of natural disasters".
With a Europe in crisis, "the economic consolidation (of Latin America) must not have a triumphalist matrix but must help to increase our productive matrix and glimpse a better future for our populations," concluded President Humala.
“We have decided to prioritize a group of 31 iconic projects that improve the connection of South America’s spaces, especially in rural and border areas... by uniting our countries and generating new economic circuits,” the Peruvian president said in a speech.
Among these projects, he mentioned that five concerned Peru and were on the transverse routes of his territory, from the coast to Brazil, and that two aimed at a greater connection with Ecuador although he did not give further details.
Similarly, the outcome document mentions the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that this country's electoral process will allow its reintegration into the Union of South American Nations", from which it is currently excluded.
The need for Latin America to remain a region of prosperity and peace, integrated and having good relations with its neighbours, is another theme discussed at the summit.
In this sense, the Colombian president, Juan Manuel Santos, said, before starting the regional meeting, that he hoped to meet his Nicaraguan counterpart, Daniel Ortega, the following Saturday in Mexico City to calmly discuss the maritime dispute through a judgment of the ICJ requested by Bogota.
"It is possible that tomorrow (Saturday), I have a meeting with President Daniel Ortega," Santos said.
"We are going to revise all these paths, they are not exhaustive, including the treaty with Nicaragua which will ask to have a discussion with this country," he said.
"I hope to be able to tell President Ortega that we are going to deal with this in the most civilized and respectful way possible," Santos said.
Presidents Santos and Ortega will meet Saturday in Mexico City where they have planned to attend the takeover of the country's new president, Enrique Peña Nieto.
In the same way, within the framework of the summit, the defence ministers of the bloc will meet beforehand to approve the Action Plan of the year 2013, which seeks to strengthen dialogue and consensus in terms of defence in the region.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Suriname and Paraguay are part of Unasur, although the latter country is suspended.
Peru holds the pro tempore presidency of the regional bloc.
“South America needs to learn from Europe how to join a citizenship,” says Rafael Correa.
The president of Ecuador, Rafael Correa, said today that the creation of a common citizenship was a goal for which "South America, in this case, had to set an example on Europe".
President Correa, who participated in the 6th summit of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur), located in Lima, told state television, TV Péru, that Europeans "had killed themselves during the Second World War" and in other conflicts, "but they were now almost a homeland".
In this sense, he defended the plan to establish South American citizenship encouraged by Unasur member countries.
"We must achieve the free movement of citizens and workers in any South American country, as is already the case for the members of the Andean Community, but there are still reactionary areas that we want to belong to the past," he warned.
In addition, the President of Equatorian has shown himself in favour of the reconstitution of the Organization of American States (OAS) with the objective of diminishing the influence of the Anglo-Saxon states and taking into account the signatories of the San José Pact on human rights.
Those who reshuffle our ears never compromise anything, conversely we South Americans have all signed it.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights is located in Washington under the funding of the United States," he said, referring to the political asylum granted by Ecuador to WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange.
Correa assures that he does not regret his decision, because he himself has not betrayed his principles, but on the contrary has respected his "deep democratic values and human rights".
He concluded that at the time, “there were well-founded suspicions that Assange would be extradited to a third country and that his trial would not be respected.”
In addition, he criticizes the Swedish judiciary, which has demanded that he submit to its territory for interrogation concerning an alleged sexual offense while "the Swedish legislation allows him to do so by videoconferencing, which could be done from the Embassy of Ecuador in London".
Correa stated that there was a risk that Assange's mental and physical health would deteriorate.
"I haven't spoken to him since he's been in our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that he had a small lung problem, but there's nothing serious."
It is possible that his physical and mental health will deteriorate by being enclosed in a small space without being able to do outdoor exercises.
"It affects anyone's health," he concludes.
Correa points out that the asylum solution granted to Assange since June in the Ecuadorian embassy of London, which would be the allocation of a safe-conduct that would allow him to travel to Ecuador, is in the hands of Britain, Sweden and European judicial authorities and he emphasizes that he has had discussions in London in order to find a solution to the enclosure of the founder of WikiLeaks.
We do not negotiate with human rights, we do not use that word in this case, but there have been permanent discussions.
"The solution to this problem is in the hands of Britain, Sweden and European judicial authorities because Assange's lawyer, Baltazar Garzón, has launched a whole series of proceedings before various European bodies," he said.
And he thinks that "if Britain granted the safe-conduct tomorrow, it would end."
And if Sweden, as its legislation allows quitely, and as it has already done in other cases, was interrogating Mr. Assange in the Ecuadorian embassy in London, or interrogating him via Skype tomorrow, the problem would also be solved.
Correa took the opportunity to reaffirm himself as a defender of freedom of the press and he said that what he did not tolerate was "mediocrity, bad faith and lies that harm freedom of expression".
“The greatest enemies of press freedom are not malicious or perverted politicians but bad journalists in terms of greed, blackmail and extortion,” he said.
From this point of view, he welcomed the fact that it was not this kind of journalists "neither bankers nor the hegemonic and bourgeois countries that dominate in Ecuador" and, rather than putting forward his re-election, "he would deepen the revolution to pursue the path and chart the right path".
Correa also supported the decision to keep the veto on Paraguay within Unasur at least until the next elections, arguing that the organization "must be firm and not tolerate opportunism and disguised coup d'état attempts", because in reality, "it destroyed the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy."
Moreover, the Ecuadorian president considers as "perfectly relevant" the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, to negotiate with Nicaragua the maritime boundaries between the two countries through the judgment of the International Court of The Hague, which will strengthen Nicaragua's maritime sovereignty.
At the moment, this judgment is not respected.
It's a problem between a South American country and a Central American country.
Conflicts are unavoidable, but they must be overwhelmed by the desire to move forward together.
We have to solve them as a whole to overcome them and move forward.
At the same time, he expressed confidence in the good resolution of the maritime border dispute between Peru and Chile in the same tribunal and he said it is "beneficial for Latin America to refer international bodies if the two countries agree to accept a judgment so firm as it is".
Referring to the eventuality of his presentation as a candidate in the future presidential elections in Ecuador, with a view to running for a third consecutive term, he reported that he saw this possibility "with a lot of optimism and joy, although this is sometimes quite difficult".
Correa assured that, if he lost the primaries of February 2013, he would withdraw from public life.
At the personal level, power has never interested me, but in situations as unjust as those of Ecuador, this socio-economic poverty can only be resolved through political power.
“My political movement believed that it was me who guaranteed this eventual victory but we have to accept that responsibility,” he said.
If I won, this would be my last presidential term and I will then withdraw from political life.
If we ever lose, I'll do it too.
"That's my decision," he said.
Correa also referred to the new medical treatment of Venezuelan President Hugo Chávez in Cuba.
I just spoke with Venezuelan Vice-President Nicolás Maduro and he told me that routine medical treatment was already planned and that he was waiting for the end of the campaign to return to Cuba.
“That doesn’t mean a relapse of President Chávez’s state of health,” he said.
The Ecuadorian Head of State participated today in Lima at the Sixth Summit of Heads of State and Government of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) which ended with a call for better regional integration in order to support progress, equality and security.
AIDS-related deaths are now due to late detection.
Fabrizio was 21 years old when he was confirmed the result of the test: HIV-positive.
"It's as if the sky fell on my head," he said, referring at the time of the announcement, that the doctor had tried to make "less painful", in vain.
The young man hid it from his family.
He decided to take care of his illness alone and began to inquire about him and his relentlessness has been such that he has just celebrated his 43rd birthday.
He is, without a doubt, one of the oldest patients of the HIV unit at the Guadalajara Civilian Hospital (HCG), in which he found himself in 1994 after many battles.
Fabrizio has been living with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) for 22 years, something difficult to imagine in the early 90s, where there were a lot of doubts, few treatment choices and more stigmas.
So, even the director of an IMSS clinic avoided saying goodbye to him “because he was speechless.”
By that time, having AIDS was synonymous with death.
Today it is possible to survive this syndrome and have a good quality of life.
However, ignoring their disease, many people still arrive when the virus has already caused damage, "exhausted" their immune system and are victims of opportunistic infections.
31 years after the onset of AIDS in the world, in any case after the first reported cases, "the great victory is today that the survival of a patient who has started treatment at the right time and the survival of the population in general is exactly the same," said Jaime Andrade Villanueva, head of HCG's HIV unit and he takes as a reference that this information was validated in April this year in a prestigious scientific publication.
Infectiologist and HIV/AIDS expert Andrade Villanueva commented that since 2008, scientists have concluded that AIDS is no longer a deadly disease, but that the number of years of survival and quality of life depended on the level of assignment of the immune system that patients presented at the time of the diagnosis, with better prospects for those who do not consume drugs: up to 30 years for a CD4 rate of up to 500
To put it simply, this means that for someone who would receive a positive HIV diagnosis at age 25, “as long as he remains under control, will be able to live without problems until the age of 75,” the scientist explained.
In order to put this progress in context, it should be noted that the life expectancy of Mexicans is now 76 years on average.
While it is true that mortality has decreased significantly in recent years, in the case of Mexico, the number of people who died from AIDS increased from 6,678 cases in 2007 to 4.862 in 2011 (according to ONUSIDA's annual report), but it is also true that, since the onset of AIDS, 60% of patients registered on the national database have died.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011, and there were 187 deaths until May of this year; however, we are assured that access to antiretroviral drugs has been universal since 2005.
So why are there still deaths?
I don’t think the problem is access to treatment.
That's how I see it, that's what happened in our hospital.
Over the last 12 years at least, we have never missed drugs, the problem is that patients arrive at very advanced stages because they do not know that they are infected, or they learn it in advanced stages of the disease.
“Nine out of ten patients are arriving when they already have an opportunistic infection, what it takes to have a greater impact on overall mortality is to carry out earlier diagnoses and, for that, it is necessary to offer screening tests in a massive way, to all those who ask for it.”
Specialists and members of the State AIDS Prevention Council of Jalisco (Coesida) agree on his proposal, as well as the patients themselves, such as Fabrizio, who had to do his screening test in a private laboratory, simply because a friend had done so and that, despite his young age, he was already a carrier of AIDS and suffered from Kaposi's sarcoma, a cancerous tumor.
Everything changes when you learn that you have AIDS.
Some people think they're going to die and don't want to know.
"If I'm going to die, it's better for me to have fun three times a week," they say, "it's not my case.
The change has been beneficial, I eat well, I exercise, I take my medications.
To this day, his parents only know that he has cancer.
I've got a normal life, just like anyone else.
"I work, I have a lot of activities, I travel, I have an active sex life, but co-responsible, I pay attention to myself and the other person," says Fabrizio, who agreed to share his intimacy with MILENIO JALISCO to help, thanks to his testimony, those who are afraid today, during the World AIDS Day march.
Let them do the test, if they have taken risks, the sooner they will know if they are HIV-positive and the better, and if they already have the diagnosis, they must know that we can live like everyone else, being responsible.
His message summarizes the slogan of the battle against AIDS in 2012.
Condoms on the counter
There are many gaps between health programs and the ordinary citizen, argues Ricardo Salazar, Guadalajara journalist who embraced the cause of HIV.
The biggest thing is prevention.
In all places dedicated to this, "we have indeed increased the distribution of condoms, before we were given one or two, now we are given packets of one hundred, it is very good, but it results, that even today, some do not have access to condoms," he says.
Among those most vulnerable to new infections are adolescents.
The question: "What are you going to do with it?", asked with sarcasm and valuable judgments by social workers, guidance counselors, pharmacists and health professionals, is the question that teenagers don't want to hear.
It proposes to reorient this inefficient distribution; that condoms should not only be sold at the counters, but to be found in public toilet dispensers and in places frequented by young people.
It's not about encouraging promiscuity.
It is also not a question of paying them the daughters and the motel, as Governor Emilio González had replied when asked if there would be a distribution of condoms within his administration.
“That’s not how sexuality works, it’s best to provide condoms for people who are already sexually active,” he said.
Numbers to Jalisco
There are 13,435 cumulative cases (12.158 of AIDS and 1,317 of HIV).
The state is the 4th State of the country in terms of the number of new cases and cumulative cases of AIDS and 13th for the number of HIV patients.
92% of infections occur by sexual route, 6% by blood and 2% by perinatal route.
It is estimated that 50,000 people are living with HIV, since for each recorded case, there are 4 or 5 people who ignore it.
A judgment ratified on appeal by a U.S. court ignores the restructuring of Vitro Group’s debt through commercial bankruptcy in Mexico. The scenario causes a harmful precedent for all national companies with ramifications in the neighboring country with solvency problems.
It seems, therefore, that the procedures for the survival of companies authorized by Mexican laws are not valid in the country with the starred banner, against international agreements.
From a practical point of view, Judge Harlin Hale's ruling on June 15 from the Bankruptcy Court for the District of North Texas leaves Mexican companies defenseless in the face of the possibility of embarking their properties beyond Bravo.
However, the resolution will allow Mexico’s leading glass manufacturer to appeal to the U.S. Supreme Court based on three inconsistencies.
First of all, while the case judge reports that creditors must register under the U.S. Bankruptcy Act, the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeal, headquartered in New Orleans, claims that the main procedure is formalized commercial bankruptcy in Mexico.
The first point would involve denouncing the cooperation of international proceedings in the insolvency cases of companies with a transnational profile.
In fact, the United Nations Model Law for the Uniformity of International Trade Law had been established for this purpose by taking the American Law Institute as arbitrator.
Secondly, the judgment establishes that without the inter-company voting, recognized in the critical mass of commercial bankruptcy, the debts incurred by Vitro's subsidiaries towards the parent company, the majority required for the approval of the restructuring could not have been obtained.
However, Mexican law recognizes this possibility.
Vitro's case is not the first to use this plan.
There are half a dozen examples here, including Agremex and Comercial Mexicana, whose Federal Institute of Commercial Bankruptcies had guaranteed debts.
In addition, what is certain is that, since he remained the votes of the subsidiaries, in every way the creditors of Vitro who fought against this in the courts of the United States, i.e. the "vauturous" funds like Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergencia, Elliot Internacional and Liverpool Limited, would not have obtained a majority.
The vote would have been 45 votes to 37.
A datum omitted by the Court of Appeal.
On the other hand, Vitro has been blamed for the difficult situation it has been facing since 2008 without stopping at the serious economic crisis facing the United States, passing on the costs to the country.
For the moment, the González Sada family company will appeal to the same court of appeal so that the vote takes place in its entirety, i.e. with the five magistrates, since only three had voted.
The recourse for insurance will claim non-prosperity and requires the review of the trial by a higher court, in this case the U.S. Supreme Court of Justice.
The most serious is that the court ignored a document sent by the Government of Mexico on behalf of the amicus curiae ("friend of the Court") in this detailed the procedure followed by Vitro under the Commercial Bankruptcies Act, noting that it released it from the agreements signed by the two countries to bring it to the same level as paragraph 15 of the U.S. Bankruptcy Act.
In addition, he pointed out that the country had complied with the principles of the United Nations Commission on International Trade, i.e. the rules laid down for cross-border insolvency cases, which guaranteed fairness for debtors and creditors.
Carambolage of two parts: they assume Vitro and they knock out the country.
General balance sheet
Complaints by the trade unions of the Mexican Aviation Society against the company's former boss, Gastón Azcárraga Andrade, have been ignored for several months, he was accused of fraudulent administration.
The body, headed by Carlos Díaz Chávez Morineau, has just submitted a criminal application against the National Banking Commission and Values which is accused of obstruction of justice.
According to him, the inspection authority has systematically denied having provided information to the Attorney General of the Republic on an operation carried out by the contractor to withdraw from the F/589 trusteeship of the bank IXE 198 million pesos on behalf of the Mexicana de Aviación.
The funds would have been used for the purchase of shares of the Hotel Professional Administration.
Azcárraga Andrade is the main shareholder of the Posadas hotel chain.
They besiege the Dragon Mart
Gathered this weekend in an amphitheatre of the University of the Caribbean, a group of national and foreign ecologists, university professors, entrepreneurs and members of civil society have approved the creation of a broad front of opposition to the inauguration of the Chinese Dragon Mart in Cancun.
As you know, we’re talking about a colossal sales and distribution center in Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean products from the country of the Great Wall, with a residential area for employees of 150 companies.
Previously, the Canacintra (the National Chamber of Industry and Transformation) had succeeded in uniting the governors of the South-Eastern zone in order to oppose the monumental project, which is partly on protected areas and represents the greatest of all the threats to the industry.
ACTA is dead
The government put forward a requirement of the senate to explain under what terms and circumstances Mexico's ambassador to Japan had signed the Anti-Counterfeiting Trade Agreement, known by its English acronym of ACTA, according to the Mexican Institute of Intellectual Property, this theme is already ancient history.
As everyone knows, this was done well that the senate itself condemned this possibility, because it was considered an attack on freedom of expression on social networks.
Long-Term Homex
In order to pay long-term debts without affecting those in the short term, the real estate developer Homex has entered the stock market for 500 million pesos.
This issue of securities is the first of the four issues for which they propose to pay interest every 28 days.
Birth of Competival
Integrated by NYCE, e-Quálity and Kernet, leaders in IT, a new consortium with Competival’s corporate name has just emerged, whose objective will be the Central and South American clustering software services market.
Investments in this area exceed US$1.5 billion.
Hector "Hetin" Reyes "Basketball is all my life"
Basketball globetrotter "Hetin" Reyes has spent more than 60 years of his life in relation to the field and, thanks to him, has traveled around the world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have knowledge of the history of local basketball as big as that of Héctor "Hetin" Reyes.
The reason for this is that before a cerebral hemorrhage condemned him to the wheelchair in 2008, Reyes was immersed in this sport for more than 60 years, whether it was as a lower leaguer, player of the national basketball team, manager and leader of the national team, the BSN, with the Vaqueros de Bayamón or as president of the basketball federation.
“I’ve worn several caps in basketball throughout my life, and even several at the same time, such as when I was president of the BSN, managing director of the national team and president of the federation during the 90’s,” Reyes recalls during a visit by Primera Hora to him in Bayamón, where he resides with Isabel, his faithful wife for more than 50 years.
“Basketball is all my life.”
Reyes doesn't exaggerate when he's making that claim.
The walls of his house, almost all decorated with photos and souvenirs that recall his extraordinary itinerary, bear witness to this.
Bayamón in the heart
Of all his memories, the ones he loves the most are those who remind him of his time as a player on the Vaqueros team in the middle of the 1950s until 1982, having spent 15 years as a co-manager or manager of the team.
"It's been my best years, the ones I've benefited the most because I've had the chance to participate in eight Vaqueros championships starting in 1967, whether it's a manager, co-manager or manager.
It's been many years of satisfaction, including the five championships played from 1971 to 1975.
I then retired during the championship in 1981, the year Jerome Mincy joined the BSN team.
From there, "Cuco" Ortiz, who was a great administrator, took the lead of the team, "said Reyes.
I remember that Gene Bartow, who had been a leader here and was at the University of Alabama (Birmingham), told me: "I have a strong player for you, he measures two meters.
“Would you?”
That's how to start Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico had.
Bartow then recommended Raymond Gausse, who was naturalized and was one of the best shooters.
I remember him saying that if Mincy had allowed Bayamon to win a championship, Gausse was going to help us try to win another.
He rejoiced in the Vaqueros championship with Gausse, but only at a distance since in 1988 he was already the great manitou of the federation.
At that time, he preferred to take advantage of his charges and those of Mincy in the national team.
I remember when we first won it in the United States at the pre-Olympic tournament in Mexico in 1989.
Then there was the 1990 World Cup where we arrived 4th and where we should have won the bronze medal if this Canadian judge hadn’t made us replay the last game,” Reyes said.
Is the World Cup team the best national team you’ve seen?
She is one of the best, just like the one who beat the Dream Team at the 2004 Olympic Games.
But my favorite is that of the 1991 Pan American Games in Cuba, when we won gold and squeezed the team from the United States, which was quite similar to the one who won bronze in the World Cup.
This team was not only composed of Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz), there were also the young (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter, brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who had not been able to attend the world of 90 because
A team that may not have been the best in terms of individuals, but it gave us a golden medal and great joy at the pre-Olympic tournament in Neuquén, Argentina.
With players like "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and the young Rolando Hourruitiner replacing the players suspended by the imbroglio of the Pan American Games of Mar del Plata, we won the gold against all predictions.
Which was the best Puerto Rico player?
Undoubtedly, this is Piculín Ortiz.
His statistics for international tournaments are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico dominated at this level like Piculín did.
And this without taking into account his trajectory in the different leagues for which he played.
Who was the best Puerto Rican leader?
That's very hard to say.
We had a good team consisting of Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
In young people, I really like the work Leo Arill does.
For you, what is your greatest success within the federation?
Having been part of the most glorious time of the national team between 1988 and 1995, and the fact that in the early 1990s the BSN had up to 17 teams in one season.
What do you still have to do?
There are things that I would have liked to do, such as the regionalization of minor categories.
For example that the boys of Ponce play only in their area and face only teams from other corners of the island during the national play-offs.
Today, young people travel and play too much while this is not necessarily necessary.
At least I have seen the advantage of mandatory certifications and courses for executives, field staff and referees.
That makes me happy.
What are you doing today?
In general, I listen to music, I watch clips of my time on YouTube, I enjoy my grandchildren and, from time to time, I attend basketball games.
And of course, I take advantage of the company of my wife, Isabel, who has always been by my side.
Actor Larry Hagman's Death
Larry Hagman (born September 21, 1931, in Forth Worth, Texas), became known worldwide by the lead role of John Ross Ewing, better known as J.R., in the television series "Dallas", in which he played an unscrupulous, dreaded and manipulative businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose unscrupulous oil tycoon J.R. Ewing in the TV series "Dallas" became a symbol of greed in the 1980's, has died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who had returned this year as J.R in a new season of "Dallas", died Friday night from complications of cancer, according to a statement from the family sent to The associated Press for Warner Bros., production company of "Dallas".
“Larry was back in his dear hometown of Dallas, again playing the ironic role he loved the most,” the family said.
Larry’s family and close friends were with him in Dallas on this Thanksgiving holiday.
Linda Gray, who played the role of his wife in the original series and in the sequel, was with Hagman when he died in a hospital in Dallas, his press secretary Jeffrey Lane said.
He brought joy to everyone who knew him.
He was creative, generous, funny, affectionate and talented, and I’ll miss him very much.
"He was an exceptional person and he was living life thoroughly," Gray said in a statement.
The doctors had diagnosed Larry Hagman with liver cirrhosis in 1992, and he admitted to drinking heavily for many years.
In 1995, he had been diagnosed with a malignant liver tumour and had undergone a transplant.
Several years before "Dallas", Hagman had made himself known on television in the role of an ordinary guy in the series "Jinny of my dreams" broadcast by NBC from 1965 to 1970.
In it, he played the role of Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life was transformed when he discovered an alluring genius, played by Barbara Eden, and brought her home.
He also starred in two comedies that remained soon on air, "The Good Life" (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC, 1973).
His film roles include roles well received by critics in "The Band", "Harry and Tonto" and "Primary Colors".
But it was thanks to his masterful rendition of the delightfully detestable J.R. that Larry Hagman had his greatest success.
The CBS drama series about the Ewing clan and their loved ones aired from April 1978 to May 1991.
The slogan "Which killed J.R.", invented to maintain the suspense around an episode full of emotions in which Hagman's character is almost murdered, has generated speculation around the world and millions of dollars played in bets.
Thanks to this, among other things, the series reached an audience record at that time.
When the response was revealed in an episode of November 1980, 41 million viewers were on average in front of their screen and made "Dallas" the second most watched entertainment program in the world, after the final episode of "MASH" in 1983, which had gathered 50 million viewers.
It was the sister-in-law of J.R., Kristin (played by Mary Crosby) who shot him.
J.R. had pregnanced her and then threatened to accuse her of prostitution if she did not leave the city, but other people also had reason to attack her.
Hagman played the role of Ewing as a corrupt and insatiable man with a charismatic smile: a dishonest entrepreneur and an unfaithful husband who was trying to have his alcoholic wife Sue Ellen (Linda Gray) interned.
"I know what it's going to be as an epitaph for J.R," Hagman said in 1988.
One should write: "This is the honest citizen J.R. Ewing."
Here's the only part he's lost.
Victoria Principal, coprotagonist of the original series, recalled this Friday of Hagman as a man "immense, both on the small screen and in life".
It’s unforgettable, and irreplaceable, for millions of fans around the world, and in the hearts of all of us, who’ve had the chance to get to know and love him.
Ten episodes of the new edition of "Dallas" aired a few months ago with great success on the TNT channel.
The recording of five episodes is already finished for the second season and a sixth is in the making, the channel informed.
For the moment, neither Warner nor TNT channel have communicated about how Hagman’s death will be integrated into the series.
Originally from Fort Worth in Texas, he is the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who knew fame with classical works such as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan".
Mary Martin was still a teenager when she gave birth to her in 1931 when she was married to lawyer Ben Hagman.
He took his first steps on the stages of New York theaters in the early 1950s and then served in the Air Force from 1952 to 1956 in England.
That's where he met young Swedish interior decorator Maj Axelsson and married him.
The couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and lived for many years in the California town of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
In 2001, he titled his memoirs "Hello Darlin': Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales about My Life": Hello Darlin: The Merciless Memoirs by J.R
"I didn't write anything in this book that could hurt someone or affect them in any way," he told reporters at the time.
Following his liver transplant, he became an active organ donation activist and worked as a volunteer in a hospital to assist worried patients.
"I advise them, I trust them again, I come with them when they come to have surgery and then," he said in 1996.
I’m trying to offer them some comfort, like “Don’t be afraid, it won’t be easy for a little while, but it’s going to be okay.”
He has also been an anti-smoking activist and has participated in several campaigns.
A course dealing with the "End of the World" has just begun
Each week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs and global warming.
This semester, when religion teacher Stuart Charmé decided to give a course on the end of the world, he knew he was throwing an irresistible hook there: the end of the "great counting" of the Mayan calendar, on December 21, to which many people refer as being the proof of the imminent end of the world.
But Professor Charmé had no idea what to expect it in the following months: the catastrophic hurricane Sandy, a task chasm that some have dubbed “Debt Armageddon” and a growing conflict involving Israel, the place where end-time Christian theorists thought the apocalypse would trigger.
“I didn’t realize it was going to be the most apocalyptic semester I’ve ever known,” Professor Charmé told his students at Rutgers-Camden (New Jersey).
If we analyze what happened in the world today as if we were 30 days later and told it, it was a very good period.
And we have to remember that what is bad is good for those who believe in apocalypsis.
And he is not the only professor to give courses on "the end of the world" this semester, which will, in theory, be the last in history.
At Temple, Assistant Professor Barry Vacker delivers the course "Medias, Culture and the End of the World".
Each week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs and global warming.
“We’re analyzing this because these ideas proliferate over time,” he said, and how they offer hypothetical scenarios guides some human conduct.
If nuclear weapons fell into the hands of terrorists, for example, a war could erupt.
This month, students will analyze films with apocalyptic themes and study how they can be compared with real-life examples.
“I tried to inform my students about what was possible, likely, credible and impossible,” Vacker said.
On the main campus of Pennsylvania State University, Latin American History Professor Matthew Restall and colleague Amara Solari, an assistant professor of Art History and Anthropology, teamed up to give a course, simply titled "The End of the World."
“We’re adding ‘2012’ to have the opportunity to give the course again, in case the world doesn’t disappear,” Professor Restall said.
Despite the "imminent disaster", students must study, create projects and pass their final examinations.
In the state of Pennsylvania, the final exam will pass the eve of the Apocalypse, which doesn’t leave students with any choice but to work “until the very night of the supposed end of the world,” Restall said.
Classes have proved to be quite popular.
"It's been complete in two hours," Restall said of its course for high-level students.
We received emails several weeks prior to the start of the semester, from people who asked if there was still room.
Students, for their part, ensure that the course is one of the most interesting.
“It fascinates me to see what people are able to do to comfort themselves,” says Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old religious and sociology student from Haddonfield, New Jersey, Rutgers-Camden University.
For the fear of Revelation, whether secular or religious, is only a matter of comfort, or lack of comfort.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old student in Psychology and Infirmary, claims to have watched all apocalyptic films.
“I had never heard of a course teaching this subject,” he said.
I appreciate it.
But none of the students surveyed, much less a professor, told us to believe in the end date of the world of December 21.
“Our first project dealt with Mayan prophecy, and in a way we discredited it,” says Julie zegle, a 21-year-old student at Temple from West Chester.
The Mayans have never predicted the end of the world, it’s only a key date on the calendar, Restall said.
But I concede that there is anxiety related to the apocalypse in Western culture, dating back several centuries, which causes people to react to changes in their environment by predicting the end of the world.
The Internet has provoked an increase in these speculations.
"In other parts of the world, people don't think about this," he said.
The majority of these are the English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a professor of religion at La Salle University who lectures in the Philippines this year, quickly responded when asked if he knew he was being given classes here on "the end of the world".
“The Philippines is not taking part in the end of the world,” he replied, as if it were an exception coming from a higher authority.
We have the grace of the Pope.
Restall points out that over the years there has been talk of the many supposed dates of the last judgment, and he says that if nothing happens on December 21, "people will immediately begin to think about the next date" or philosophize on the fact that December 21 marks the beginning of a 7-year period at the end of which the world will disappear.
Students and professors take the date lightly.
Many told us that they were thinking of going to “the end of the world” parties.
“Sometimes I call friends and we’re laughing about it together,” said 20-year-old communications student Samira Ford.
